   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Welcome and learning agreement","steps":["Welcome learners by name as they join. Start on time even if some are late; latecomers will catch up in the icebreaker.","Show the programme map on one slide: Week 1 shared core (sessions 1 and 2), Weeks 2 to 4 specialist track (sessions 3 to 8), session 8 is the showcase. Say: everything you make in this programme goes into a portfolio you can show an employer, a client or a funder.","Build the learning agreement with the group. Ask: what do you need from each other to learn well? Write their answers. Make sure these five appear: respect different views, one person speaks at a time, cameras or attention on, what is shared personally stays in the room, phones on silent unless used for an activity.","Explain safeguarding in plain words. If anyone, inside or outside the programme, makes you feel unsafe, tell the facilitator or use the support option in DIRA LEARN. Share only the support contacts that programme management has formally confirmed for this cohort (from the facilitator briefing). If a Safeguarding Lead or Deputy has been formally appointed and confirmed, name them; if not, do not name anyone or suggest that someone holds the role - say that concerns go to the facilitator or the DIRA LEARN support option and are passed to programme management. Do not promise secrecy; say concerns are shared only with the people who need to act on them.","Ask everyone to type or say 'I agree' to the learning agreement. Save it and post it in the cohort space after the session."]},{"title":"Icebreaker - two truths and a strength","steps":["Model it first: 'I am Wanjiru from Nakuru, I sell second-hand shoes on weekends, and one strength I have is patience with difficult customers.'","In breakout groups of four (or around the room), each learner gives their name, where they are from, two true facts and one strength. Give 60 seconds each.","Group members name one more strength they heard in the story (for example, the shoe seller also shows planning and negotiation).","Bring the group back. Ask three volunteers to share a strength someone else spotted in them. Point out that others often see strengths we overlook."]},{"title":"Values, confidence and self-leadership mini-workshop","steps":["Explain three words. A skill is something you learned to do (typing, cooking, editing video). A strength is something you do well and that energises you (listening, organising, making people laugh). A value is something that matters to you and guides decisions (honesty, family, faith, independence, community).","Values exercise (5 minutes). Show a list of 20 values. Learners choose five, then cut to two. Ask: when did one of these two values help you make a hard decision? Two volunteers share.","Teach self-leadership: you cannot control everything that happens, but you can choose your response. Use the example of a learner whose phone data runs out before a session: blame and miss it, or plan ahead (buy a bundle early, download materials, tell the facilitator).","Teach the confidence loop: prepare, act, reflect. Confidence comes after action, not before it. Each small action you reflect on makes the next one easier.","Self-talk. Read two sentences: 'I always mess up presentations' and 'My last presentation was nervous, so this time I will practise twice.' Ask which one helps. Learners rewrite one unhelpful sentence they say to themselves.","Explain growth mindset in one sentence - abilities grow with practice and feedback. Connect it to the programme - you are not expected to be good at digital marketing, AI, content or business yet; you are expected to practise."]},{"title":"Strengths map and personal brand statement","steps":["Hand out or share the strengths map template: four boxes - Strengths, Interests, Values, Help I can offer others. Learners fill each box with at least two items and one real example (8 minutes).","Explain personal branding. Your personal brand is what people say about you when you are not in the room. It is built from what you are good at, what you stand for and how you help others, shown consistently online and offline. It is not pretending or showing off.","Give the formula: I am a [strength] person who cares about [value]. I help [who] to [what] by [how]. Example: 'I am an organised and patient person who cares about honesty. I help small shops in my estate to keep customers coming back by answering messages quickly and politely.'","Learners write their own two-sentence statement (5 minutes). Circulate and help anyone stuck by asking what friends usually ask them for help with.","Three volunteers read their statements. Give one specific compliment and one improvement each (for example, make the 'who' more specific).","Tell learners they will improve this statement in session 2 (online profile) and use it in the session 8 showcase."]},{"title":"Confidence at work - practice rounds","steps":["Demonstrate three phrases. Introducing: 'Good morning, I am Brian, I am new in the team and I will be supporting customer orders.' Asking for help: 'I have tried X and Y, could you show me how you would do Z?' Receiving feedback: 'Thank you, can you give me an example so I can improve it?'","In pairs, learners take turns using each phrase in a work scenario (a first day at a supermarket, an internship at a county office, a client call for a small design job). Two minutes per person.","Ask the group which phrase felt hardest and why. Note that asking for help after trying something shows initiative, not weakness."]},{"title":"Wellbeing and resilience","steps":["Ask learners to name signs of stress they notice in themselves (poor sleep, irritability, scrolling for hours, headaches, avoiding people). Write them down without judgement.","Teach four basics: sleep (a regular time helps more than long weekend sleep), movement (a 10-minute walk counts), food and water, and screen boundaries (no phone for the first 30 minutes after waking, mute busy groups).","Teach one recovery tool: the 4-4-6 breath (breathe in for 4, hold for 4, out for 6, repeat three times). Practise it together once.","Support routes: a trusted friend or family member, a mentor, the facilitator, DIRA staff through the support option, and professional help. Say clearly that if anyone feels unsafe or is thinking of harming themselves, they should tell someone immediately and contact the emergency services or a crisis line; the facilitator will share the programme's approved contacts.","Each learner writes two wellbeing habits they will keep during the four weeks on their goal card."]},{"title":"SMART goal and accountability partner","steps":["Explain SMART - Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound. Weak goal: 'learn marketing'. SMART goal: 'By the end of week 4 I will create and present a basic marketing campaign for my aunt's cereals shop and get feedback from two real customers.'","Learners write their goal and their first step for this week on the goal card (5 minutes).","Pair learners as accountability partners (from different tracks if possible). Partners read each other's goals and make one suggestion to make it more measurable.","Partners agree how they will check in (for example a WhatsApp message every Sunday evening). Remind them to keep messages respectful and on topic, and that they can tell the facilitator if a partnership is not working."]},{"title":"Reflection and exit ticket","steps":["Learners complete the baseline self-assessment in DIRA LEARN or on paper (confidence 1 to 5 in communication, digital skills, teamwork and goal setting).","Exit ticket - three sentences: one thing I learned about myself today, one thing I will do before session 2, one question I still have.","Close: thank learners, remind them of the session 2 date and time from the timetable, and ask them to bring a phone or computer with access to their social media or LinkedIn account if they have one."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Modelling a personal brand statement","steps":["Write your own strengths map live (keep it honest and simple).","Turn it into a statement using the formula, thinking aloud about each choice.","Show a weak version ('I am hardworking and passionate') next to the improved one, and explain why specific beats general."]},{"title":"Confidence phrases","steps":["Act out a poor example first (mumbling an introduction, apologising for asking a question), then the confident version.","Ask learners what changed (eye contact, clear name and role, specific question)."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Name three of your strengths and give a real example of one of them.","guide":"Look for strengths (not just skills) with a concrete example. Prompt with 'when did you do that?' if the example is vague."},{"question":"What is the difference between a skill, a strength and a value?","guide":"Skill is learned ability; strength is what you do well and that energises you; value is what matters and guides decisions."},{"question":"Read your personal brand statement. Who do you help, and how?","guide":"The statement should name a specific group of people and a specific way of helping. 'Everyone' or 'many things' needs narrowing."},{"question":"Is your four-week goal SMART? Which part is the measurable part?","guide":"There should be a number, a deliverable or a clear observable result and a date (end of week 4)."},{"question":"Where can you go for support during the programme if you are struggling or feel unsafe?","guide":"Any of the facilitator, the DIRA LEARN support option, a confirmed support contact shared in the session (including a formally appointed Safeguarding Lead or Deputy, if one was named), a mentor, a trusted adult, or emergency services for immediate danger."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Check the DIRA LEARN cohort list and attendance register before the session; mark attendance in DIRA LEARN within the session.","Prepare the programme map slide, the values list, the strengths map and goal card templates.","Before the session, get from programme management the support contacts formally confirmed for this cohort, and whether a Safeguarding Lead and Deputy have been formally appointed. Share only confirmed contacts and appointments. Never invent a contact or name someone who has not been formally appointed.","Write your own strengths map and personal brand statement in advance so you can model them."],"delivery":["Keep the energy high in the first 20 minutes; this session sets the tone for the whole programme.","Use learners' names often. Praise effort and specifics, not just talent.","Watch the time on the strengths map; learners who are stuck often need one question ('what do friends ask you for help with?')."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Values and wellbeing discussions can bring up personal difficulties. Never force anyone to share; allow 'pass'.","If a learner discloses harm or risk, do not investigate. Listen, reassure, explain who you must tell, and record and escalate it through the DIRA LEARN safeguarding process the same day (immediately if urgent).","Learners with low data or older phones can complete templates on paper and photograph them later."],"after_session":["Post the learning agreement in the cohort space.","Check exit tickets and answer common questions at the start of session 2.","Note any learner who seemed withdrawn or did not participate and check in privately."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
