uts. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners build the shared digital, communication and workplace-readiness foundation every specialist track depends on. They learn safe and professional online conduct, organise their learning files, see what AI tools can and cannot do, practise clear written and spoken communication, improve one professional online profile using their personal brand statement and deliver a 30-second self-pitch. They leave having confirmed their specialist track.","objectives":["Apply five rules of professional online conduct (email, WhatsApp, video calls) and organise a programme folder","Explain three things AI tools can help with, three limitations and why human checking is always required","Write a clear prompt and improve the output using verification and their own judgement","Write a professional message and an email with a clear subject, greeting, request and sign-off","Improve one professional online profile (LinkedIn, Instagram bio, WhatsApp about or Facebook) so it matches their personal brand","Deliver a 30-second self-pitch and give respectful feedback to a peer","Recognise common online scams and privacy risks and know how to respond","Confirm their specialist track choice with a reason"],"topics":[{"title":"Digital foundations for learning and work","subtopics":["Files and folders (cloud and phone)","Naming files so you can find them","Strong passwords and two-step verification","Data-saving habits"]},{"title":"Professional behaviour and workplace readiness online","subtopics":["Email and WhatsApp etiquette","Video-call etiquette","Response times and reliability","Respectful disagreement","Punctuality and keeping commitments"]},{"title":"AI awareness","subtopics":["What AI tools are","What they are good at","Limitations (errors, made-up facts, bias, out-of-date information)","Privacy - what never to paste into an AI tool","Human judgement and disclosure"]},{"title":"Communication and self-presentation","subtopics":["Clear messages (purpose, audience, request)","Tone","Listening and summarising","Body language and voice on camera"]},{"title":"Professional online presence","subtopics":["Profile photo, headline and bio","Matching your personal brand statement","What employers and clients check","Privacy settings"]},{"title":"Pitching yourself","subtopics":["The 30-second self-pitch (who, strength, proof, ask)","Adapting it for an employer, client or funder"]},{"title":"Online safety","subtopics":["Common scams in Kenya (fake jobs, M-Pesa reversal requests, fake prizes)","Phishing links","Sextortion and image-based abuse - what to do","Reporting and support"]},{"title":"Track confirmation","subtopics":["The four specialist tracks","Matching the track to your goal"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":5,"title":"Recap and digital confidence pulse check","kind":"introduction","summary":"Recap session 1 and rate your digital confidence."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Digital foundations and professional conduct","kind":"lesson","summary":"Organise your programme files, secure your accounts and learn the rules of professional online behaviour."},{"minutes":20,"title":"AI awareness and prompt practice","kind":"demonstration","summary":"See what an AI assistant can and cannot do, write a prompt and check the output."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Clear communication practice","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write a professional message and email, and practise listening and summarising."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Professional online presence and self-pitch","kind":"exercise","summary":"Improve one online profile so it matches your personal brand, then deliver a 30-second self-pitch."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Online safety scenarios","kind":"group","summary":"Spot scams and privacy risks in real-looking scenarios and decide what to do."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Core module check and track confirmation","kind":"assessment","summary":"Complete the short core knowledge check and confirm your specialist track."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Reflection, digital safety checklist and next steps","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Complete the digital safety checklist, reflect on the week and prepare for your track."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Prompt and improve","instructions":"Write a prompt for your own introduction message, improve it twice and note what you corrected yourself."},{"title":"Professional email","instructions":"Write an application email with a clear subject line, greeting, two-sentence introduction, request and sign-off."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Profile upgrade","instructions":"Improve one online profile (photo, headline, bio, contact, privacy) so it matches your personal brand statement. Private option: draft the improvements in a document without changing a public profile."},{"title":"Digital safety checklist","instructions":"Complete and tick every item on the checklist; ask for help with any you cannot do."},{"title":"Track confirmation","instructions":"Confirm your track in DIRA LEARN with one sentence linking it to your four-week goal."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Listening and summarising pairs","instructions":"Explain your goal for 60 seconds; your partner summarises it in one sentence. Swap."},{"title":"Self-pitch pairs","instructions":"Deliver your 30-second pitch twice; your partner gives one 'keep' and one 'improve'."},{"title":"Online safety scenarios","instructions":"In groups, identify warning signs, actions and reporting routes for a scam or abuse scenario."}],"examples":["Fake job adverts on WhatsApp and Facebook that ask Kenyan job seekers for 'registration' or 'medical' fees before an interview.","SMS messages claiming 'I have sent you money by mistake' that try to trick people into sending M-Pesa to strangers.","A Nakuru graduate whose LinkedIn headline changed from 'Student' to 'Junior accountant | Helping small businesses keep clean records' and who started receiving recruiter messages.","Using Meta AI in WhatsApp to draft a polite message to a landlord, then checking the facts before sending."],"tools":["Phone or computer with internet (pairs for learners without a device)","Google Drive or phone file manager","An AI assistant (any free one available in Kenya, such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot or Meta AI)","LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook or WhatsApp (learners' own accounts, optional)","Online safety scenario cards, digital safety checklist and core knowledge check"],"assessment":[{"question":"Name three things an AI assistant can help with and two of its limitations."},{"question":"List three pieces of information you must never paste into an AI tool."},{"question":"What does PAR stand for in a professional message?"},{"question":"A WhatsApp job offer asks you to pay KES 1,500 before the interview. What do you do?"},{"question":"Why should you turn on two-step verification on WhatsApp and email?"},{"question":"Deliver your 30-second self-pitch. Did it include who, strength, proof and ask?"},{"question":"What would an employer think of your online profile? Name one change you made."},{"question":"Which track did you confirm and how does it connect to your goal?"}],"expected_outputs":["Programme folder with the standard file names","Short professional introduction message created with AI support, improved and checked by the learner, with a one-line AI disclosure","Professional application email","Improved online profile (or paper draft) matching the personal brand statement","Delivered 30-second self-pitch","Completed digital safety checklist and core knowledge check","Confirmed specialist track"],"homework":["Finish your profile upgrade and ask your accountability partner for one improvement. If you prefer not to change a public profile, write the improved photo choice, headline and bio in a private document instead.","Practise your 30-second pitch aloud three times; record it on your phone once and listen back.","Make sure two-step verification is on for email and WhatsApp."],"cross_track_notes":["AI awareness here is the shared minimum for everyone. The AI Tools track goes much deeper (prompt engineering, research, automation, evaluation). Other tracks use AI only as an optional helper with the same disclosure rule.","Professional online presence here is about the learner as a person. Digital Marketing session 3 applies the same ideas to a business's presence and reputation."]}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and digital confidence pulse check","steps":["Answer two or three common questions from the session 1 exit tickets.","Quick poll (hands, chat or DIRA LEARN): rate yourself 1 to 5 on finding files, writing emails, using AI tools, staying safe online. Note the lowest-scoring area and give it a little extra time today."]},{"title":"Digital foundations and professional conduct","steps":["Demonstrate a programme folder in Google Drive or on a phone: DIRA LEARN > Week 1, Week 2, Week 3, Week 4, Portfolio. Show a good file name: '2026-11 Wanjiru Kamau - goal card.pdf'. Bad: 'IMG_2034.jpg'.","Passwords: use a long passphrase (three random words plus a number), a different password for email, and turn on two-step verification on email, WhatsApp and social media. Show where the WhatsApp two-step setting is (Settings > Account > Two-step verification).","Professional conduct - five rules: (1) reply within one working day, even if only to say when you will answer fully; (2) greet and sign off; (3) one clear request per message; (4) no voice notes for work requests unless the other person prefers them; (5) be on time for calls and test your audio and camera first.","Workplace readiness: punctuality, keeping promises, telling your supervisor early when you will miss a deadline, dressing appropriately for video calls and for the workplace, respecting confidentiality. Ask: which of these have you seen go wrong at a job or a group project?"]},{"title":"AI awareness and prompt practice","steps":["Explain simply: AI assistants (such as ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, Claude or Meta AI in WhatsApp) predict useful text from patterns in huge amounts of data. They can draft, summarise, explain and brainstorm. They do not 'know' facts the way a reference book does, and can be confidently wrong.","Live demo: ask an AI tool 'Write a short introduction message for a young person applying for an internship at a county government office.' Show the result. Then improve the prompt with role, task, context and format: 'You are a careers adviser. Write a 60-word WhatsApp message for Kevin, 21, from Machakos, who has a certificate in ICT and wants an internship at the Machakos County ICT office. Friendly and professional tone.' Compare.","Show a limitation: ask the tool for a fact that changes (for example the current minimum wage or an application deadline) and explain that answers must be checked against an official source.","Privacy rule: never paste ID numbers, M-Pesa PINs, passwords, other people's private messages or personal health details into an AI tool.","Learners try one prompt for their own introduction message and improve it twice. They mark what they changed themselves (facts, tone, details AI got wrong). Learners without a device pair with someone who has one.","Disclosure rule for DIRA LEARN work - if AI helped, say how in one line (for example 'First draft by AI, edited and fact-checked by me')."]},{"title":"Clear communication practice","steps":["Teach the PAR structure - Purpose (why I am writing), Audience (who will read it), Request (what I want them to do). Show a poor message: 'Hi, am interested in the job pls reply.' Show a good one with greeting, who you are, which job, why you fit in one line, clear request, thanks and name.","Learners write an email to a fictional employer (Subject: Application - Sales Assistant, Nyeri branch) with greeting, two-sentence introduction, request for an interview, sign-off and phone number. 5 minutes.","Pair activity - listening: one partner explains their four-week goal for 60 seconds, the other summarises it in one sentence starting 'So what you want to achieve is...'. Swap.","Ask two pairs to share. Highlight that summarising shows respect and avoids misunderstandings at work."]},{"title":"Professional online presence and self-pitch","steps":["Show two LinkedIn or Instagram profiles of fictional young Kenyans side by side. One: blurry photo, headline 'student', no bio, party photos public. Two: clear photo, headline 'Content creator | Helping Kisii small businesses get seen online', short bio, contact, examples of work.","Learners improve one profile they already have (or draft one on paper): profile photo, headline (role or aim plus who you help), bio from their personal brand statement, contact method, one thing to hide or delete. Check privacy settings so personal phone numbers and home locations are not public. 8 minutes.","Teach the 30-second self-pitch - Who I am, my strength, proof (one example), my ask. Example: 'I am Achieng from Homa Bay. I am good at explaining things simply - I tutor three Form Two students in maths. I am joining the Digital Marketing track and I am looking for a small business I can practise with.'","In pairs, each learner delivers the pitch twice; the partner gives one 'keep' and one 'improve'. Remind them this pitch will grow into their session 8 showcase introduction."]},{"title":"Online safety scenarios","steps":["Groups of four get one scenario each: (1) a WhatsApp job offer asking for a KES 1,500 'registration fee'; (2) an SMS saying 'I have sent you M-Pesa by mistake, please send it back' with no matching M-Pesa message from the official sender; (3) a stranger on Instagram asking for private photos and then threatening to share them; (4) a link to 'claim your free data bundle'.","Each group answers: what are the warning signs, what should you do now, who can you tell, how can you report it. 6 minutes.","Groups report back. Key teaching points: real employers do not charge application fees; check your actual M-Pesa balance and messages rather than reacting to an SMS; never send money or codes under pressure; for threats or image-based abuse do not pay, keep evidence (screenshots), block and report on the platform, tell a trusted person and the facilitator; image-based abuse and threats are crimes that can be reported to the police.","Remind learners that the facilitator, the DIRA LEARN support option and the confirmed support contacts shared in session 1 are available if something like this happens during the programme."]},{"title":"Core module check and track confirmation","steps":["Learners complete the 8-question core knowledge check (see assessment).","Present the four tracks in one minute each - Digital Marketing (reach and win customers online and run a campaign), AI Tools (use AI responsibly to work faster and better), Content Creation (photos, videos and design that tell stories), Entrepreneurship & Business (turn an idea into a tested business concept and pitch).","Learners confirm their track in DIRA LEARN with one sentence on how it connects to their four-week goal. Remind them the track choice follows the programme's rules in DIRA LEARN; anyone unsure should speak to the facilitator before the deadline."]},{"title":"Reflection, digital safety checklist and next steps","steps":["Learners tick the digital safety checklist: two-step verification on email and WhatsApp, strong unique email password, privacy settings checked, programme folder created, profile improved.","Reflection round: each learner says one word for how they feel about starting their track, and one action for this week.","Explain what happens in week 2 (first track session) and what to bring (a charged phone, data or Wi-Fi, their programme folder).","Thank learners for completing the core. Remind them that attendance at both core sessions is required before track learning, following the programme's attendance rules in DIRA LEARN."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Organising a programme folder and turning on two-step verification","steps":["Create the folder structure live in Google Drive or on a phone's file manager.","Rename a badly named file to the standard format.","Open WhatsApp settings and show where two-step verification is (do not enter a real PIN on screen)."]},{"title":"Prompt improvement live","steps":["Type a weak prompt and show the generic output.","Add role, task, context and format; show the improved output.","Find one error or invented detail and correct it, explaining why human checking matters."]},{"title":"Profile before and after","steps":["Show a fictional weak profile and talk through what an employer would think.","Edit the same profile into a strong one, reading the new headline and bio aloud."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Name three things an AI assistant can help with and two of its limitations.","guide":"Helps with drafting, summarising, explaining, brainstorming, translating. Limits include wrong or invented facts, out-of-date information, bias, no understanding of your situation."},{"question":"List three pieces of information you must never paste into an AI tool.","guide":"Passwords and PINs, ID or passport numbers, other people's private messages or data, health or financial details, confidential work information."},{"question":"What does PAR stand for in a professional message?","guide":"Purpose, Audience, Request."},{"question":"A WhatsApp job offer asks you to pay KES 1,500 before the interview. What do you do?","guide":"Do not pay. Real employers do not charge fees. Verify the company through official channels, report and block, tell the facilitator."},{"question":"Why should you turn on two-step verification on WhatsApp and email?","guide":"It protects the account if someone gets your password or SIM; the attacker also needs the PIN or second step."},{"question":"Deliver your 30-second self-pitch. Did it include who, strength, proof and ask?","guide":"Observe all four parts and a clear ask. Use the pitch rubric (clarity, confidence, specific proof)."},{"question":"What would an employer think of your online profile? Name one change you made.","guide":"Expect a concrete change such as photo, headline, bio or privacy setting."},{"question":"Which track did you confirm and how does it connect to your goal?","guide":"The link should be explicit (for example 'Business track because my goal is to test my juice business idea')."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Test the AI tool, screen sharing and the internet connection before the session. Prepare a backup screenshot of the AI demo in case the connection fails.","Prepare the fictional before-and-after profiles (never use a real learner's profile without permission).","Print or share the scenario cards, the digital safety checklist and the core knowledge check.","Check the track selection rules and deadline in DIRA LEARN so you can answer questions accurately."],"delivery":["This session is full; keep segments to time and use a visible timer.","In the AI demo, model checking and disclosure every time; learners copy what you do, not what you say.","Profiles - learners should only change their own accounts. Do not ask anyone to log in on a shared device."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Online safety scenarios may be close to learners' real experiences. Offer a private conversation after the session and follow the DIRA LEARN safeguarding process if a learner discloses abuse or exploitation.","Learners without smartphones work in pairs or on paper; never shame anyone for their device or data.","Remind learners to keep personal phone numbers and home locations off public profiles.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Record attendance and core completion in DIRA LEARN.","Check that every learner has confirmed a track; follow up with those who have not.","Share the slides, checklist and templates in the session resources."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 3,
   $cv13${"title":"Digital marketing foundations, online presence and reputation","description":"How digital marketing works, the main channels and strategies, digital footprint and online reputation, and building a strong personal and business presence. Learners choose their campaign brief.","objectives":["Explain digital marketing and the customer journey (awareness, consideration, conversion, loyalty) using a local business example","Compare owned, earned and paid channels and name at least five digital channels used by Kenyan businesses","Audit a digital footprint (personal and business) and identify three risks and three improvements","Respond professionally to a negative online review","Improve one personal or business profile for clarity, trust and contact","Choose a campaign brief and draw a first channel map for it"],"activities":["Welcome to Digital Marketing and track overview","What digital marketing is, the customer journey and channels","Digital footprint and online reputation","Demonstration - footprint audit and review reply","Stretch break","Presence audit and profile improvement","Choose your campaign brief and draw a channel map","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Personal digital footprint audit (kept private) with privacy settings fixed; Improved personal or business profile (bio, image, contact, trust signals); One-paragraph campaign brief; Channel map with two main channels and one support channel","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners understand what digital marketing is, how customers move from first seeing a business to buying and returning, and which channels Kenyan small businesses use. They audit their own and a business's digital footprint, learn how reputation is built and repaired online, improve a profile, and choose the real business or cause they will build their campaign for.","objectives":["Explain digital marketing and the customer journey (awareness, consideration, conversion, loyalty) using a local business example","Compare owned, earned and paid channels and name at least five digital channels used by Kenyan businesses","Audit a digital footprint (personal and business) and identify three risks and three improvements","Respond professionally to a negative online review","Improve one personal or business profile for clarity, trust and contact","Choose a campaign brief and draw a first channel map for it"],"topics":[{"title":"What digital marketing is","subtopics":["Marketing versus selling","Digital versus traditional marketing (posters, radio, word of mouth)","Why small businesses in Kenya market on phones","What a campaign is"]},{"title":"The customer journey","subtopics":["Awareness","Consideration","Conversion","Loyalty and referral","Where each channel helps"]},{"title":"Digital marketing channels and strategies","subtopics":["Owned channels (profiles, WhatsApp Business, website, Google Business Profile)","Earned channels (shares, reviews, word of mouth, media)","Paid channels (boosted posts, ads, influencers)","Marketplaces (Jumia, Kilimall, Facebook Marketplace)","SMS and email","Choosing channels by audience and budget"]},{"title":"Digital footprint and online reputation","subtopics":["Active and passive footprint","What employers and customers find when they search you","Reviews and ratings","Responding to complaints","Protecting privacy and safety"]},{"title":"Personal and business online presence","subtopics":["Name, photo or logo, bio and contact consistency","Trust signals (reviews, real photos, location, opening hours, payment details)","The difference between a personal and a business account"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Welcome to Digital Marketing and track overview","kind":"introduction","summary":"Meet your track group, see the six sessions and the final campaign you will build."},{"minutes":20,"title":"What digital marketing is, the customer journey and channels","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how customers move from noticing a business to buying again, and which channels help at each stage."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Digital footprint and online reputation","kind":"lesson","summary":"Understand what your online trail says about you or a business, and how reputation is protected and repaired."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Demonstration - footprint audit and review reply","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Watch a live audit of a business's online presence and a professional reply to a bad review."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Presence audit and profile improvement","kind":"exercise","summary":"Audit your own footprint and a business profile, then improve one profile."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Choose your campaign brief and draw a channel map","kind":"group","summary":"Pick the real business or cause for your campaign and map which channels its customers use."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer short questions on the customer journey, channels and reputation."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise the session and confirm your homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Customer journey mapping","instructions":"Map one business you know through awareness, consideration, conversion and loyalty, naming the channel at each step."},{"title":"Review reply","instructions":"Write an ACE reply to a fictional complaint about a late delivery or a wrong order."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Personal footprint audit","instructions":"Search your name and usernames, list what you find, fix privacy settings and remove anything you would not want an employer to see."},{"title":"Business profile improvement","instructions":"Audit one business profile with the presence checklist and write the improved bio, contact line and profile image choice."},{"title":"Campaign brief","instructions":"Write a one-paragraph brief for the business or cause you will market in this track. Choose a real business or cause (with the owner's agreement) or the simulated brief from the facilitator."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Channel map challenge","instructions":"Present your brief to two peers, draw the channel map, choose two main channels and one support channel and defend your choice."}],"examples":["Shiru's Bakes, a fictional Ruiru home bakery, used to illustrate the customer journey throughout the track.","A mama mboga in Kawangware who takes orders by WhatsApp and announces fresh deliveries on WhatsApp status.","A kinyozi in Kahawa West that gained new customers after listing on Google Maps with photos and opening hours.","A Gikomba mitumba trader who sells on Facebook Marketplace and TikTok live.","A youth recycling group in Kibera using Facebook and X to recruit volunteers and find buyers for recycled products."],"tools":["Phones or computers with internet","Google Search and Google Maps","Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn and WhatsApp Business (for viewing; learners use their own accounts)","Presence checklist, campaign brief template and channel map template","Prepared screenshots for the demonstration (backup for poor connectivity)"],"assessment":[{"question":"Name the four stages of the customer journey and one channel that helps at each."},{"question":"What is the difference between owned, earned and paid channels? Give an example of each."},{"question":"What is a passive digital footprint?"},{"question":"A customer posts an angry review. Write the first sentence of your reply."},{"question":"Why should a business use a business phone number on its profiles?"}],"expected_outputs":["Personal digital footprint audit (kept private) with privacy settings fixed","Improved personal or business profile (bio, image, contact, trust signals)","One-paragraph campaign brief","Channel map with two main channels and one support channel"],"homework":["Talk to the business owner (or yourself, if it is your idea) and confirm the brief - what they sell, who buys, and what they want to improve. Ask for written permission (a WhatsApp message is enough) to use the business's name, logo, photos and prices. If you cannot get permission, or prefer not to work in public, use the simulated brief from the facilitator.","Make the profile improvements you wrote in class.","Ask three real customers of the business how they first heard about it. Bring their answers to session 4. Tell them why you are asking, and note answers without names."],"cross_track_notes":["Core session 2 covered the learner's personal profile. Here the focus is a business's presence and reputation and the strategy behind channel choice.","Content Creation also covers platform formats, but from the creator's craft angle; Digital Marketing chooses channels and content to reach a business goal."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Welcome to Digital Marketing and track overview","steps":["Welcome learners to the track. Show the six-session journey on one slide: S3 foundations and presence, S4 audiences, social media and WhatsApp Business, S5 brand, content plan and graphics, S6 copy, conversion and search, S7 ads, budget, measurement and campaign build, S8 showcase.","Show a one-slide example of a finished campaign for a fictional business, 'Shiru's Bakes' (a home bakery in Ruiru selling cakes and bread on WhatsApp and Instagram): objective, persona, three posts, a KES 1,000 budget and results. Say: by session 8 you will have one like this for a real business.","Ask learners to share in the chat or aloud one business they buy from because of something they saw online."]},{"title":"What digital marketing is, the customer journey and channels","steps":["Define it: digital marketing is using online channels - social media, WhatsApp, search, marketplaces, SMS and email - to help the right people find, trust, choose and return to a business. Selling is the moment of purchase; marketing is everything that makes that moment more likely.","Draw the customer journey with the Shiru's Bakes example. Awareness - a neighbour shares her cake photo on WhatsApp status. Consideration - the customer checks Instagram for prices and reviews. Conversion - orders on WhatsApp and pays with M-Pesa. Loyalty - gets a thank-you message and a birthday discount next year and refers a friend.","Explain owned, earned and paid channels with examples. Owned - the business's own Instagram, WhatsApp Business, Google Business Profile, website. Earned - reviews, shares, customers tagging the business, a local blog mention. Paid - boosted posts, Meta or Google ads, paying a micro-influencer. Most small Kenyan businesses start owned and earned, then add a little paid.","Tour the main channels quickly: Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn, WhatsApp Business, Google Search and Maps (Google Business Profile), marketplaces (Jumia, Kilimall, Facebook Marketplace), SMS and email. One line each on who uses it and what it is good for. Say that the next sessions go deeper into each.","Strategy in one sentence: right message, right people, right channel, right time, with a clear action. Ask: which channels would a mama mboga in Kawangware use, and which would a Nairobi graphic designer use? Why different?"]},{"title":"Digital footprint and online reputation","steps":["Explain the digital footprint. Active - what you post, comment, like and share. Passive - what others post about you, tags, reviews, location data, search results. Both stay for a long time and screenshots last forever.","Explain why it matters: employers and clients search candidates; customers read reviews before buying. A single rude reply to a complaint can travel further than ten good posts.","Reputation rules for a business: respond to every review (good or bad) within a day, thank publicly, solve privately, never argue or reveal a customer's personal details, fix the real problem, and ask happy customers for reviews.","Teach the review reply model ACE - Acknowledge ('Thank you for telling us, we are sorry your order was late'), Correct or explain briefly without excuses ('our rider had a breakdown'), Engage offline ('please WhatsApp us on our business number so we can make it right').","Privacy and safety: business accounts should use a business number, not a personal one; do not post customers' faces, children or home locations without consent; under Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019, customer data such as phone numbers must be collected and used fairly and for a clear purpose."]},{"title":"Demonstration - footprint audit and review reply","steps":["Search a fictional or consenting local business name on Google and social media (prepare screenshots in case of poor connection). Score it with the presence checklist: name consistent, clear photo or logo, bio says what and where, contact and payment details, opening hours, recent posts, reviews, replies to reviews.","Show a fictional one-star review: 'Waited 2 hours for my cake and it was the wrong flavour. Never again.' Write the ACE reply live and read it aloud.","Show a bad reply ('You are lying, we delivered on time') and ask learners what damage it does.","Do a personal footprint search on yourself (prepared in advance) and show what you cleaned up: old public posts, a phone number in a bio, an unprofessional username."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Presence audit and profile improvement","steps":["Part A (10 min, individual): learners search their own name and usernames. They list what they find, mark anything they would not want an employer or customer to see, and check privacy settings. Remind them not to share private findings with the group unless they want to.","Part B (15 min, individual): learners audit one business profile (their own business, a family business, or a business they will use as their campaign brief, with the owner's agreement) using the presence checklist, then write the improved version of the bio, the profile photo or logo choice, contact line and pinned post or highlight.","Give a bio formula: what you sell + who it is for + where + how to order. Example: 'Fresh cakes and bread for birthdays and offices in Ruiru and Juja. Order on WhatsApp 07XX XXX XXX. Pay via M-Pesa Till.'","Circulate. Check that learners use a business number and do not publish home addresses."]},{"title":"Choose your campaign brief and draw a channel map","steps":["Explain brief options: (a) a real local business with the owner's permission (a salon, cyber cafe, kinyozi, poultry farmer, fashion seller, food vendor); (b) a youth enterprise or community organisation (a youth recycling group, a church project, a sports club); (c) the learner's own business idea. The brief must be real enough to test with real people.","Each learner writes a one-paragraph brief: business name, what it sells or does, location, current customers, the problem (for example few new customers, quiet weekdays, low trust online), and one thing the owner wants in the next month.","In groups of three, learners take turns presenting their brief and drawing a channel map: list the channels, mark which ones the customers actually use, and choose two main channels and one support channel. Peers challenge choices ('Do your customers really use LinkedIn?').","Learners save the brief and channel map in their DIRA LEARN portfolio folder. Remind them to tell the business owner what they are doing and get agreement before posting anything for the business."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the five assessment questions as a quick quiz (DIRA LEARN, a poll or on paper).","Go through answers and correct misunderstandings immediately."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask three learners to say one thing they will change in their profile or business presence this week.","Set the homework and remind learners to bring their brief to session 4."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Business presence audit with the checklist","steps":["Search the business on Google, Maps, Facebook, Instagram and TikTok.","Score each checklist item live and note the top three fixes."]},{"title":"Replying to a negative review with ACE","steps":["Read the review, identify the real problem, write Acknowledge, Correct, Engage, then compare with a bad reply."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Name the four stages of the customer journey and one channel that helps at each.","guide":"Awareness (status, TikTok, shares), consideration (Instagram, reviews, Google profile), conversion (WhatsApp order, M-Pesa till), loyalty (thank-you message, offer, referral)."},{"question":"What is the difference between owned, earned and paid channels? Give an example of each.","guide":"Owned - business controls it (WhatsApp Business, Instagram page). Earned - others give it (reviews, shares). Paid - you pay for reach (boosted post, ad)."},{"question":"What is a passive digital footprint?","guide":"Information others create about you or that is collected automatically (tags, reviews, location data, search results)."},{"question":"A customer posts an angry review. Write the first sentence of your reply.","guide":"Should acknowledge and apologise or thank, without arguing or blaming the customer."},{"question":"Why should a business use a business phone number on its profiles?","guide":"Privacy and safety for the owner, professionalism, separating personal and business messages, continuity if staff change."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the Shiru's Bakes example campaign slide and the presence checklist.","Prepare screenshots of a business presence (fictional or with written consent) for the demonstration.","Prepare a list of possible local briefs for learners who do not have a business to work with (for example a community group DIRA works with, with their consent)."],"delivery":["Keep theory short and practical; every concept should be tied to a Kenyan business learners recognise.","When learners choose briefs, push for real and specific businesses; a vague brief makes every later session harder.","The footprint audit is personal; do not ask learners to show results publicly."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Remind learners never to post content about a business, customer or person without consent.","Learners must not share home addresses, children's images or personal numbers in their projects.","If a footprint search reveals harassment or abusive content about a learner, follow up privately and use the DIRA LEARN safeguarding process if needed.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Review the briefs in the portfolio folder and flag any that are too vague or unsafe before session 4.","Record attendance in DIRA LEARN and upload the slides and templates to the session resources."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 4,
   $cv13${"title":"Target audiences, social media marketing and WhatsApp Business","description":"Define target audiences and customer personas, use Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn for marketing, and set up WhatsApp Business to serve and sell to customers.","objectives":["Explain target audience, segmentation and customer persona with examples","Build a customer persona from real customer answers (demographics, needs, problems, habits, where they spend time online)","Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn by audience, content formats and best uses for marketing","Explain in simple terms how feeds decide what people see (relevance, engagement, consistency)","Set up or plan a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting, away message, quick replies and labels","Use broadcast lists and status responsibly, with customer consent","Choose the platforms and posting frequency for their campaign"],"activities":["Recap and customer answers","Target audiences and customer personas","Build your customer persona","Social media marketing on four platforms","Stretch break","Demonstration and practice - WhatsApp Business","Platform plan for your campaign","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Customer persona based on real customer information; WhatsApp Business plan (or a set-up profile on a business number); Platform plan with formats, posting times and reasons","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners stop marketing to 'everyone'. They define a target audience and build a customer persona from real information, learn what each major social platform is best for and how its content is shown to people, and set up a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting and quick replies. They leave with a persona and a platform plan for their campaign.","objectives":["Explain target audience, segmentation and customer persona with examples","Build a customer persona from real customer answers (demographics, needs, problems, habits, where they spend time online)","Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn by audience, content formats and best uses for marketing","Explain in simple terms how feeds decide what people see (relevance, engagement, consistency)","Set up or plan a WhatsApp Business profile with a catalogue, greeting, away message, quick replies and labels","Use broadcast lists and status responsibly, with customer consent","Choose the platforms and posting frequency for their campaign"],"topics":[{"title":"Target audiences and segmentation","subtopics":["Who is your customer (not everyone)","Segmenting by age, location, income, interests and needs","Primary and secondary audiences","Using real customer conversations"]},{"title":"Customer personas","subtopics":["Name, age and situation","Goals and problems","Buying habits and budget","Online habits","Objections and what builds trust"]},{"title":"Social media marketing on Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn","subtopics":["Facebook - pages, groups and Marketplace","Instagram - posts, reels, stories, highlights","TikTok - short video, trends, sounds and lives","LinkedIn - professional services, B2B and careers","Business and creator accounts and their insights","How feeds choose content"]},{"title":"WhatsApp Business","subtopics":["Business profile","Catalogue and prices","Greeting and away messages","Quick replies","Labels for orders","Status for updates","Broadcast lists and consent","Professional response times"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and customer answers","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share what customers said about how they found the business."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Target audiences and customer personas","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to choose a specific audience and describe them as a persona."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Build your customer persona","kind":"exercise","summary":"Create a persona for your campaign using the template and real customer answers."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Social media marketing on four platforms","kind":"lesson","summary":"Compare Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn and how each shows content to people."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Demonstration and practice - WhatsApp Business","kind":"demonstration","summary":"See a WhatsApp Business profile set up step by step, then plan or set up your own."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Platform plan for your campaign","kind":"group","summary":"Choose your campaign platforms, formats and posting times based on your persona."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer short questions on personas, platforms and WhatsApp Business."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Segment and choose","instructions":"List three customer segments for your brief and choose one primary segment with a reason."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Customer persona","instructions":"Complete the persona template with at least two facts from real customer answers."},{"title":"WhatsApp Business plan","instructions":"Write the profile description, three catalogue items with prices, greeting and away messages, three quick replies and three labels."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Persona questions","instructions":"Swap personas with a partner who asks two questions it does not answer yet; update your persona."},{"title":"Platform plan challenge","instructions":"Present your platform plan to two peers, who check it against your persona."}],"examples":["Shiru's Bakes persona 'Mercy, 32, accountant in Juja'.","A Kisumu photographer who books graduation shoots through Instagram highlights and a WhatsApp link.","A Nairobi CV-writing freelancer who finds clients on LinkedIn by posting before-and-after CV tips.","A Kakamega poultry farmer who sends weekly egg prices to a broadcast list of 80 customers who saved his number.","A mitumba trader in Gikomba who sells through TikTok lives in the evening."],"tools":["Persona template and platform plan template","WhatsApp Business app on a test phone, or prepared screenshots","Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and LinkedIn (viewing examples)","Homework answers from customers"],"assessment":[{"question":"Why is 'everyone' not a good target audience for a small business?"},{"question":"Name four parts of a customer persona."},{"question":"Which platform would you choose for a B2B cleaning company and why?"},{"question":"Name three WhatsApp Business features that save time."},{"question":"What must you do before adding a customer to a broadcast list or group?"}],"expected_outputs":["Customer persona based on real customer information","WhatsApp Business plan (or a set-up profile on a business number)","Platform plan with formats, posting times and reasons"],"homework":["Interview two more real customers or potential customers to test your persona and update it.","Follow three Kenyan businesses on your main platform and note what they post, when, and what gets the most engagement.","If the business owner agrees, set up or improve their WhatsApp Business profile."],"cross_track_notes":["Entrepreneurship & Business also defines customers and target markets, to decide whether a business idea is worth building. Digital Marketing builds personas to decide channels, messages and timing for an existing offer."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and customer answers","steps":["Ask four learners to share what customers told them about how they found the business. Write the channels on the board and count them.","Point out that word of mouth and WhatsApp usually appear often for small Kenyan businesses; this is evidence, not guesswork, and it should shape the campaign."]},{"title":"Target audiences and customer personas","steps":["Explain why 'everyone' is not a target audience: a small budget spread over everyone reaches no one well. A clear audience makes it easier to choose channels, words and images.","Teach segmentation with Shiru's Bakes. Segment 1 - working mothers in Ruiru and Juja ordering birthday cakes. Segment 2 - small offices ordering bread and snacks weekly. Segment 3 - university students at nearby campuses buying cupcakes for events. Choose one primary segment for the campaign.","Build a persona live: 'Mercy, 32, accountant in Juja, two children, orders a cake 4 times a year, budget KES 2,000 to 3,500, scrolls Facebook and WhatsApp status in the evening, worried about late delivery and cakes that look different from the photo, trusts recommendations from other mothers.'","Explain each persona part and how it changes marketing: her worry about late delivery means the campaign must show on-time delivery proof; her evening scrolling means posting at 7 to 9 pm.","Warn against stereotypes; personas must come from real conversations and data (the homework answers), not assumptions."]},{"title":"Build your customer persona","steps":["Learners fill the persona template for their brief - name, age, location, situation, goals, problems, budget, buying habits, online habits, objections, what builds trust.","Every persona must include at least two facts from real customer answers. Learners mark them with a star.","Swap with a partner, who asks two questions the persona does not yet answer. Learners update it."]},{"title":"Social media marketing on four platforms","steps":["Facebook: broad adult audience across Kenya, strong for local community groups, Marketplace and events. Formats - posts, photos, videos, reels, groups, Marketplace listings. Good for - local services, second-hand goods, community organisations, older customers. Tip - join and contribute to local groups (estate, town, university) and follow each group's rules on promotion.","Instagram: visual, younger urban audience. Formats - feed posts, carousels, reels, stories, highlights. Good for - fashion, food, beauty, photography, events. Tip - highlights for prices, how to order and reviews; a clear bio with a WhatsApp link.","TikTok: short video and discovery, people can find you even with zero followers. Formats - short videos, trends, sounds, duets, lives. Good for - showing products being made or used, behind the scenes, personality-led brands. Tip - the first 2 seconds decide if people keep watching.","LinkedIn: professional audience. Formats - posts, articles, company pages. Good for - professional services, B2B, recruitment, NGOs, a learner's own career. Tip - share lessons and results, not hard selling.","How feeds decide (simplified): platforms show content people are likely to engage with - watch time, saves, shares, comments and how quickly people react. Consistency and relevance beat posting a lot. Explain business or professional accounts and their insights (reach, engagement, audience age and location).","Quick discussion: which two platforms fit Mercy (the persona)? Facebook and WhatsApp status, maybe Instagram. Which fit a youth photographer in Kisumu? Instagram and TikTok."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Demonstration and practice - WhatsApp Business","steps":["Explain that WhatsApp Business is a separate free app for businesses and that it should run on a business number. Do not ask learners to switch their personal number in class.","Demonstrate on a test phone (or screenshots): business profile (name, category, description, address or service area, hours, email, website or Instagram), catalogue (item photo, name, price in KES, short description), greeting message ('Karibu Shiru's Bakes! Send us the cake size and date and we will reply within 30 minutes'), away message, quick replies (/prices, /delivery, /mpesa), and labels (New order, Paid, Delivered).","Status and broadcast lists: status is good for daily updates (today's fresh bread); broadcast lists only reach people who have saved your number, which is a good consent signal. Never add people to groups or lists without their agreement, and always offer a way to stop messages ('Reply STOP to stop updates').","Learners write their own WhatsApp Business plan - profile description, three catalogue items with KES prices, greeting message, away message, three quick replies and three labels. Learners who already run a business number can set it up now."]},{"title":"Platform plan for your campaign","steps":["In groups of three, each learner completes the platform plan: main platform, support platform, WhatsApp Business role, content formats, posting days and times, and why (linked to the persona).","Peers check each plan against the persona and challenge anything that does not match (for example TikTok-only for a persona who never uses TikTok).","Learners add the persona and platform plan to their campaign folder."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the assessment questions and discuss the answers."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Each learner says their persona's name and main platform in one sentence.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Building a persona from real answers","steps":["Read three real or realistic customer quotes, extract facts and fill the persona template live."]},{"title":"WhatsApp Business set-up","steps":["Walk through profile, catalogue, greeting, away message, quick replies and labels on a test device or screenshots.","Show status and a broadcast list with an opt-out line."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Why is 'everyone' not a good target audience for a small business?","guide":"Limited budget and time; a specific audience makes messages, channels and timing more effective."},{"question":"Name four parts of a customer persona.","guide":"Any four of demographics, situation, goals, problems, budget, buying habits, online habits, objections, trust triggers."},{"question":"Which platform would you choose for a B2B cleaning company and why?","guide":"LinkedIn (professional decision-makers), possibly Facebook and WhatsApp for local reach. Reason must refer to the audience."},{"question":"Name three WhatsApp Business features that save time.","guide":"Quick replies, greeting and away messages, catalogue, labels."},{"question":"What must you do before adding a customer to a broadcast list or group?","guide":"Get their agreement (they saved your number or asked for updates) and give a way to stop messages."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Install WhatsApp Business on a test phone with a test number, or prepare clear screenshots of every step.","Prepare examples of real Kenyan business pages on each platform (public pages only) for the platform comparison.","Print or share the persona and platform plan templates."],"delivery":["Do not teach every platform feature; focus on what each platform is for and what works for small businesses.","Check that personas are based on real answers. If a learner did not do the homework, they can interview a classmate who fits the persona as a stopgap and must complete real interviews before session 5."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners must not add people to groups or broadcast lists without consent.","Remind learners not to share personal numbers publicly; recommend a separate business number where possible."],"after_session":["Review personas and platform plans; give written feedback in DIRA LEARN on at least one point per learner.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 5,
   $cv13${"title":"Branding, content planning and marketing graphics","description":"Build a simple brand identity, plan content with pillars and a two-week calendar, and design marketing graphics that communicate clearly.","objectives":["Explain branding and brand identity (name, promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, logo) with local examples","Create a brand mini-kit for their campaign business","Define three or four content pillars and a content mix for their persona","Build a two-week content calendar with dates, platforms, formats, captions ideas and goals","Apply visual communication principles (hierarchy, contrast, alignment, white space, readable text) to a marketing graphic","Design two marketing graphics in Canva (or a similar free tool) that follow the brand mini-kit"],"activities":["Recap and what businesses post","Branding and brand identity","Create your brand mini-kit","Content pillars and the content calendar","Stretch break","Build your two-week content calendar","Visual communication principles","Demonstration and practice - designing marketing graphics","Peer review and knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Brand mini-kit (promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, photo style); Content pillars and a two-week content calendar; Two branded marketing graphics (feed post and status or story)","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners give their campaign a consistent look and voice, turn their persona and platform plan into a two-week content calendar with clear content pillars, and design their first marketing graphics in a free design tool using visual communication principles.","objectives":["Explain branding and brand identity (name, promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, logo) with local examples","Create a brand mini-kit for their campaign business","Define three or four content pillars and a content mix for their persona","Build a two-week content calendar with dates, platforms, formats, captions ideas and goals","Apply visual communication principles (hierarchy, contrast, alignment, white space, readable text) to a marketing graphic","Design two marketing graphics in Canva (or a similar free tool) that follow the brand mini-kit"],"topics":[{"title":"Branding and brand identity","subtopics":["Brand versus logo","Brand promise","Brand personality and voice","Colours, fonts and imagery","Consistency builds recognition and trust"]},{"title":"Content planning","subtopics":["Content pillars","Content mix (educate, entertain, inspire, sell, community)","Posting frequency that is realistic","Using the persona to choose topics"]},{"title":"Content calendars","subtopics":["Calendar columns (date, platform, format, pillar, idea, caption hook, call to action, status)","Planning around local dates (paydays, holidays, back to school, exams, festive season)","Batching content"]},{"title":"Marketing graphics and visual communication","subtopics":["Visual hierarchy","Contrast and readability on small phone screens","Alignment and white space","Using real product photos","Templates without looking generic","Image sizes for each platform"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and what businesses post","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share what you noticed about the businesses you followed."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Branding and brand identity","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn what a brand is and the parts of a simple brand identity."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Create your brand mini-kit","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write a brand promise, personality, voice and choose colours and fonts for your campaign business."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Content pillars and the content calendar","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to plan content around pillars and organise it in a calendar."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Build your two-week content calendar","kind":"exercise","summary":"Fill a two-week calendar for your campaign with at least six planned posts."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Visual communication principles","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the design principles that make marketing graphics clear on a phone screen."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Demonstration and practice - designing marketing graphics","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Watch a marketing graphic designed in Canva, then design two of your own."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Peer review and knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Test each other's graphics with the 3-second test and answer the knowledge check."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Content pillars","instructions":"Write three or four content pillars for your business, each linked to a persona need."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Brand mini-kit","instructions":"Complete the brand promise, personality, voice examples, colours, fonts and photo style for your campaign business."},{"title":"Two-week content calendar","instructions":"Plan at least six posts and two WhatsApp status or broadcast items with pillar, format, idea, hook and call to action."},{"title":"Two marketing graphics","instructions":"Design one feed post and one status or story graphic that follow your brand kit and pass the 3-second test."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"3-second test","instructions":"Show your graphic to a partner for three seconds; they say what it means; improve it if they miss the message."}],"examples":["Shiru's Bakes brand mini-kit and content pillars.","A Mombasa juice vendor planning posts around hot afternoons and Friday prayers footfall.","A school uniform seller in Nyeri planning a content calendar around school opening dates.","A Nakuru youth football club using consistent colours and logo on all match-day posters."],"tools":["Canva (free) or a similar free design tool; paper and coloured pens as a backup","Brand mini-kit template and content calendar template (Google Sheets or paper)","Meta Business Suite (for showing scheduling)","Real product photos from the business (with permission)"],"assessment":[{"question":"What is the difference between a brand and a logo?"},{"question":"Write a brand promise for your business in one sentence."},{"question":"What are content pillars and why use them?"},{"question":"Name three visual communication principles."},{"question":"Your calendar has 10 posts and 8 are sales posts. What is the problem?"}],"expected_outputs":["Brand mini-kit (promise, personality, voice, colours, fonts, photo style)","Content pillars and a two-week content calendar","Two branded marketing graphics (feed post and status or story)"],"homework":["Finish both graphics and design one more for your calendar.","Take at least five real photos of the business or product (with permission) for the next session's posts. Ask before including any person's face, and do not photograph children. On the simulated option, use your own product photos or free-licence images.","Show your brand kit and one graphic to the business owner and note their feedback."],"cross_track_notes":["Content Creation teaches graphic design and visual storytelling in depth (layout, typography, carousels, video). Digital Marketing uses design only as far as needed to communicate an offer and a call to action that serve the campaign goal.","AI Tools covers AI-generated images; learners in Digital Marketing may use AI help only with disclosure and must prefer real product photos."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and what businesses post","steps":["Ask learners what kinds of posts got the most engagement for the businesses they followed. Group the answers (behind the scenes, offers, tips, customer photos, humour).","Link to today - those patterns become content pillars."]},{"title":"Branding and brand identity","steps":["Define brand: what people feel and expect from a business. The logo is only a symbol. Ask learners to describe the feeling of two well-known Kenyan brands (for example a telecom company and a local dairy) in three words.","Teach the parts of brand identity: name; brand promise (one sentence on what customers can always expect, for example 'Fresh cakes, on time, exactly as in the photo'); personality (three adjectives - warm, reliable, playful); voice (how it writes - friendly English with a little Sheng or Kiswahili, or formal); visual identity (two or three colours, one or two fonts, photo style, logo).","Show the Shiru's Bakes mini-kit: promise, personality 'warm, reliable, celebratory', voice examples ('Karibu!', 'Your celebration, our pleasure'), colours (a warm pink, cream, chocolate brown with their codes), fonts (one bold heading font, one clean body font), photo style (natural light, real cakes, customers' smiles with consent).","Consistency: the same name, profile image, colours and voice across all platforms make a small business look trustworthy. Show an inconsistent example (different names and logos on each platform) and ask what customers might think.","Ethics: do not copy another brand's logo, name or designs; this can confuse customers and infringe trademarks or copyright."]},{"title":"Create your brand mini-kit","steps":["Learners complete the brand mini-kit template - promise, three personality words, voice (three example phrases), two or three colours with codes, fonts and photo style. If the business already has a brand, they document it and suggest one improvement.","In Canva (or similar), learners create a simple brand kit page or a screenshot of colours and fonts. Learners without a device do it on paper with coloured pens.","Two volunteers show their kits; the group checks whether the visuals match the personality words."]},{"title":"Content pillars and the content calendar","steps":["Explain content pillars - three or four themes the business always posts about, linked to persona needs. Shiru's Bakes - (1) Celebration ideas, (2) Behind the scenes (baking, delivery), (3) Happy customers (reviews and photos with consent), (4) Offers and how to order.","Content mix: not every post should sell. A simple rule - for every sales post, share two or three posts that help, entertain or build trust.","Show the calendar template: date, platform, format (photo, carousel, reel, status, broadcast), pillar, idea, hook (first line), call to action, owner, status (idea, drafted, designed, scheduled, posted).","Plan around Kenyan dates: month-end paydays, school opening and closing, Madaraka, Mashujaa and Jamhuri holidays, the festive season, exam periods, local events. Say: a realistic calendar (three to four posts a week) that is followed beats an ambitious one that is abandoned.","Explain batching - create several posts in one sitting and schedule them (Meta Business Suite can schedule Facebook and Instagram posts)."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Build your two-week content calendar","steps":["Learners write their three or four pillars, then fill a two-week calendar with at least six posts and two WhatsApp status or broadcast items, across their chosen platforms.","Each entry needs a pillar, format, idea, hook and call to action.","Check that the calendar includes at least one post for each pillar and no more than one third sales posts."]},{"title":"Visual communication principles","steps":["Teach five principles with good and bad examples: (1) hierarchy - one main message biggest, then detail, then call to action; (2) contrast - dark text on light backgrounds or the reverse, never yellow on white; (3) alignment - line elements up, do not scatter; (4) white space - leave breathing room, fewer words; (5) readability - test the design at phone size, use at most two fonts.","Show the 3-second test: if someone scrolling cannot understand the post in 3 seconds, simplify it.","Mention sizes: square 1080 x 1080 for feed posts, vertical 1080 x 1920 for stories, reels and status, 1080 x 1350 portrait for Instagram feed.","Prefer real product photos over stock photos where possible - customers trust real images."]},{"title":"Demonstration and practice - designing marketing graphics","steps":["Live in Canva: create a 1080 x 1080 design, upload a real product photo, apply brand colours and fonts, write a headline (for example 'Birthday cakes delivered on time in Ruiru'), add price or offer, and a call to action ('Order on WhatsApp 07XX XXX XXX'). Show how to remove clutter.","Show how to resize the design for status or stories and how to download it as PNG.","Learners design two graphics from their calendar (one feed post and one status or story) using their brand mini-kit. Remind them to use their own photos, the business's photos with permission, or images with a licence that allows use."]},{"title":"Peer review and knowledge check","steps":["In pairs, learners show their graphic for 3 seconds; the partner says what the message is. If they get it wrong, the designer notes one change.","Run two or three quick assessment questions aloud."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners that brand kit, calendar and graphics all go into the campaign folder.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Brand mini-kit","steps":["Show a complete mini-kit for Shiru's Bakes and explain each choice."]},{"title":"Designing a marketing graphic in Canva","steps":["Create, apply brand kit, write hierarchy, add CTA, resize for status, download."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What is the difference between a brand and a logo?","guide":"The brand is what people feel and expect; the logo is a visual symbol of it."},{"question":"Write a brand promise for your business in one sentence.","guide":"Specific, believable, customer-focused (for example 'Fresh, on time, exactly as shown')."},{"question":"What are content pillars and why use them?","guide":"Repeating themes linked to audience needs; they make planning easier and keep content consistent."},{"question":"Name three visual communication principles.","guide":"Hierarchy, contrast, alignment, white space, readability."},{"question":"Your calendar has 10 posts and 8 are sales posts. What is the problem?","guide":"Too much selling; audiences disengage. Add posts that help, entertain or build trust."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the Shiru's Bakes mini-kit, a good and bad graphic for each principle, and the calendar template.","Test Canva login and screen sharing; prepare a downloaded template in case of connectivity issues.","Prepare a list of Kenyan calendar dates for the next two months that learners can plan around."],"delivery":["Learners often spend too long choosing colours; give a time limit and allow them to refine later.","Push for real photos of the business; offer tips for taking them with a phone (natural light near a window, clean background)."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Remind learners to get consent before using photos of customers or staff, and never to use images of children without their parent's or guardian's consent.","Learners must not copy other brands' logos or designs.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Give feedback on each calendar in DIRA LEARN (balance of pillars, realistic frequency).","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 6,
   $cv13${"title":"Copywriting, customer engagement and search engine optimisation","description":"Write persuasive, honest marketing copy, turn engagement into customers on WhatsApp and social media, and use search engine optimisation basics including Google Business Profile.","objectives":["Write captions using the AIDA and Problem-Agitate-Solution structures, with a clear call to action","Turn product features into customer benefits","Write honest marketing copy that avoids false or misleading claims","Use engagement and conversion techniques (fast replies, social proof, clear offers, easy ordering and payment)","Handle an enquiry from first message to confirmed order in a role-play","Explain how search engines rank results and the basics of keywords and local SEO","Draft a Google Business Profile and a keyword list for their business"],"activities":["Recap and caption hunt","Copywriting and persuasive messaging","Write your campaign captions","Customer engagement and conversion","Stretch break","Role-play - from enquiry to order","Search engine optimisation fundamentals and Google Business Profile","Keyword list and Google Business Profile draft","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Three finished captions with hooks and calls to action; Enquiry-to-order conversation script (from the role-play); Keyword list (ten phrases, five priority); Google Business Profile draft","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners write captions and messages that make people act, handle enquiries so they become sales, and make the business easier to find on Google Search and Maps. They leave with finished captions, a customer conversation script and a local search plan.","objectives":["Write captions using the AIDA and Problem-Agitate-Solution structures, with a clear call to action","Turn product features into customer benefits","Write honest marketing copy that avoids false or misleading claims","Use engagement and conversion techniques (fast replies, social proof, clear offers, easy ordering and payment)","Handle an enquiry from first message to confirmed order in a role-play","Explain how search engines rank results and the basics of keywords and local SEO","Draft a Google Business Profile and a keyword list for their business"],"topics":[{"title":"Copywriting and persuasive messaging","subtopics":["Hooks and first lines","Features versus benefits","AIDA (Attention, Interest, Desire, Action)","Problem-Agitate-Solution","Calls to action","Tone and language (English, Kiswahili, Sheng)","Honesty and avoiding misleading claims"]},{"title":"Customer engagement and conversion","subtopics":["Reply speed and tone","From comment to DM to order","Social proof (reviews, testimonials with consent, numbers)","Offers and urgency without pressure","Reducing friction (prices, delivery, M-Pesa till or paybill)","Follow-up and repeat customers"]},{"title":"Search engine optimisation fundamentals","subtopics":["How search works (crawling, indexing, ranking)","Keywords and search intent","Local SEO and 'near me' searches","Google Business Profile (category, hours, photos, reviews, posts)","Simple on-page SEO for a website or blog","Consistent name, address and phone across the web"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and caption hunt","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share a caption that made you want to buy something."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Copywriting and persuasive messaging","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to write hooks, benefits, calls to action and honest persuasive captions."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Write your campaign captions","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write three captions for your calendar posts using AIDA or PAS."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Customer engagement and conversion","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to turn likes, comments and messages into orders and repeat customers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Role-play - from enquiry to order","kind":"exercise","summary":"Practise handling a customer message from the first question to a confirmed order."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Search engine optimisation fundamentals and Google Business Profile","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how search works and how a small business gets found on Google Search and Maps."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Keyword list and Google Business Profile draft","kind":"exercise","summary":"Find keywords for your business and draft its Google Business Profile."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer questions on copywriting, conversion and search."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Features to benefits","instructions":"Turn three product features into customer benefits for your persona."},{"title":"Keyword hunt","instructions":"Use Google autocomplete to find ten real search phrases and pick the five most relevant."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Three campaign captions","instructions":"Write one AIDA, one PAS and one short status caption for your calendar, each with a hook and one CTA."},{"title":"Google Business Profile draft","instructions":"Complete the profile template with a keyword-rich but natural description."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Enquiry-to-order role-play","instructions":"In pairs, handle a scenario-card customer message from first question to confirmed order, then swap."}],"examples":["Shiru's Bakes captions in AIDA and PAS.","A Thika boda boda delivery business that ranks for 'parcel delivery Thika' after completing its Google Business Profile and collecting reviews.","A kinyozi in Kahawa West replying to every Google review with the customer's first name and a thank-you.","An Eldoret shoe seller who lost sales by replying 'inbox' to price questions, then gained orders by posting prices in the catalogue."],"tools":["Caption template, scenario cards, Google Business Profile template","Google Search (autocomplete, People also ask) and Google Maps","WhatsApp Business (from session 4)"],"assessment":[{"question":"What does AIDA stand for?"},{"question":"Turn the feature 'delivery within 2 hours' into a benefit."},{"question":"A customer asks 'how much?' in a comment. What should you do?"},{"question":"Name three parts of a Google Business Profile that help a business get found."},{"question":"Why should you never buy fake reviews?"}],"expected_outputs":["Three finished captions with hooks and calls to action","Enquiry-to-order conversation script (from the role-play)","Keyword list (ten phrases, five priority)","Google Business Profile draft"],"homework":["Publish one of your posts with its caption on the business's account (with the owner's permission) or your own account, and note the reach and engagement after 48 hours. Private option: share it with a small group of people who agreed to take part, or test it with three peers, and record their reactions instead of public reach.","Bring all campaign parts to session 7."],"cross_track_notes":["Content Creation teaches captions as creative writing and storytelling for audiences. Digital Marketing teaches copy as persuasion with a measurable action (an enquiry, an order, a booking).","Entrepreneurship & Business covers sales and negotiation face to face and in business deals; here selling happens in online conversations."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and caption hunt","steps":["Ask three learners to read a caption from a Kenyan business that worked on them and say why.","Note common patterns (a question, a problem they recognise, a clear price, urgency)."]},{"title":"Copywriting and persuasive messaging","steps":["Hooks: the first line must stop the scroll. Types - a question ('Late cake again?'), a bold promise ('Birthday cakes delivered on time, every time'), a number ('3 ways to save on your child's party'), a story start ('At 6 am on Saturday...').","Features versus benefits. Feature - 'eggless sponge'. Benefit - 'everyone at the party can enjoy it, even guests who don't eat eggs'. Practise turning two features into benefits together.","AIDA: Attention (hook), Interest (relevant detail), Desire (benefit, proof), Action (call to action). Write one AIDA caption live for Shiru's Bakes.","PAS: Problem ('Your office tea break needs snacks but the shop is far'), Agitate ('so people leave work and come back late'), Solution ('Shiru's delivers fresh bread and mandazi to your office every morning - order by 8 pm').","Calls to action must be one clear step: 'WhatsApp us on 07XX', 'Tap the link to order', 'Save this post for your next party'.","Tone: match the persona. Many Kenyan brands mix English with Kiswahili or Sheng, but it must sound natural to the audience. Honesty: do not claim 'best in Kenya' without proof, do not invent reviews, do not hide prices or conditions, and label paid partnerships."]},{"title":"Write your campaign captions","steps":["Learners write three captions for posts from their calendar - one AIDA, one PAS and one short caption for a status or story.","Each caption must include a hook, a benefit, proof or detail, and one call to action.","Partners swap captions and underline the hook and CTA. If they cannot find them, the writer rewrites."]},{"title":"Customer engagement and conversion","steps":["Explain conversion: the moment someone takes the action you want (a message, an order, a booking, a sign-up). Engagement without conversion does not pay the rent.","Speed: reply to enquiries quickly during business hours; many customers buy from whoever answers first. Use quick replies from session 4.","The enquiry-to-order flow: greet, answer the question with the price, ask one question to understand the need, recommend, confirm details (size, date, location), give payment details (M-Pesa till or paybill, never a personal number for a registered business), confirm the order in writing, thank and follow up after delivery.","Social proof: share reviews and customer photos with their consent, numbers ('120 cakes delivered this month'), before-and-after.","Offers and urgency: a genuine limited offer can help ('10% off orders before Friday'), but fake countdowns and pressure damage trust.","Reduce friction: prices in posts or a catalogue (customers who have to ask 'how much?' often leave), delivery areas and costs, clear payment steps.","Retention: a thank-you message, asking for a review, and a reason to come back (a loyalty stamp on WhatsApp, a birthday message with permission)."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Role-play - from enquiry to order","steps":["In pairs, one learner is the customer (using a scenario card - for example 'asks the price, says it is expensive, wants delivery tomorrow in Juja'), the other replies as the business using the enquiry-to-order flow. Swap after 4 minutes.","Debrief - which reply moved the conversation forward? How did you handle 'it is expensive' (explain value, offer a smaller option, never insult the customer)?"]},{"title":"Search engine optimisation fundamentals and Google Business Profile","steps":["Explain search in three steps: search engines crawl the web, index (store) pages, then rank results by relevance to the search, quality and trust, and for local searches, distance and prominence.","Keywords and intent: the words people actually type. 'cake shop Ruiru', 'birthday cake delivery Juja', 'eggless cake near me'. Informational intent (how to) versus commercial intent (where to buy). Use Google autocomplete and 'People also ask' to find real phrases.","Local SEO is the most useful part for small Kenyan businesses: a complete Google Business Profile shows on Google Maps and local results. Demonstrate the parts - business name (real name, no keyword stuffing), category, service area or address, hours, phone, website or social link, photos, products or services, posts, and reviews and replies.","Reviews: ask happy customers for Google reviews with a short link; never buy fake reviews or offer payment for positive ones.","On-page basics if the business has a website or blog: a clear page title and description with the main keyword, a heading that says what and where, fast pages, mobile-friendly, contact details on every page.","Consistency: the same business name, phone and location on Google, Facebook, Instagram and directories helps search engines and customers trust the business."]},{"title":"Keyword list and Google Business Profile draft","steps":["Learners use Google autocomplete to list ten search phrases real customers might use, marking the five most relevant.","Learners draft the Google Business Profile using the template - name, category, area, hours, a 250-word description using keywords naturally, five product or service entries and three photo ideas.","If the owner agrees and the business does not have a profile, the owner can create or verify it later; learners must not create profiles for businesses without the owner's permission."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the assessment questions; discuss two answers in depth."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners that session 7 is the campaign build and they must bring all parts (brief, persona, platform plan, brand kit, calendar, graphics, captions).","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Writing an AIDA caption live","steps":["Start from a feature list, find the benefit, write hook, interest, desire and CTA, then cut unnecessary words."]},{"title":"Google Business Profile walkthrough","steps":["Show each field of a fictional or consenting business profile and how reviews and replies appear on Maps."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What does AIDA stand for?","guide":"Attention, Interest, Desire, Action."},{"question":"Turn the feature 'delivery within 2 hours' into a benefit.","guide":"For example 'last-minute party? You still get a fresh cake on time'."},{"question":"A customer asks 'how much?' in a comment. What should you do?","guide":"Reply quickly with the price or a clear range and the next step; ideally prices are already in the post or catalogue."},{"question":"Name three parts of a Google Business Profile that help a business get found.","guide":"Correct category, complete details (area, hours, phone), photos, products or services, reviews and replies, posts."},{"question":"Why should you never buy fake reviews?","guide":"It misleads customers, breaks platform rules and can lead to removal; trust is lost if discovered."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare scenario cards for the role-play and a fictional Google Business Profile walkthrough.","Collect examples of good and weak captions from public Kenyan business pages."],"delivery":["Keep copywriting practical; learners should write more than they listen.","Stress honesty and consent - persuasive does not mean misleading."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners must not create or edit profiles for businesses without the owner's permission.","Testimonials and customer photos need the customer's consent."],"after_session":["Check every learner has the parts needed for the session 7 build; message those missing pieces.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 7,
   $cv13${"title":"Online advertising, campaign budgeting, analytics and campaign build","description":"Understand online advertising, plan a small KES budget, choose measures of success, and build the full campaign for the final project.","objectives":["Explain the difference between boosting a post and creating an ad in Meta Ads Manager, and give an overview of Google Ads","Choose an ad objective, audience, creative and budget for a small campaign","Build a campaign budget in KES covering content, data, ads and other costs, with a cost-per-result estimate","Explain reach, impressions, engagement rate, clicks, messages, conversions, cost per result and return on ad spend","Read basic insights on Facebook, Instagram or WhatsApp Business and track offline results with simple codes","Assemble a complete campaign plan with a SMART objective, KPIs and a timeline","Improve the campaign based on peer testing"],"activities":["Recap and results from your first post","Introduction to online advertising","Campaign planning and budgeting in KES","Marketing analytics and measuring results","Stretch break","Campaign build","Peer testing and revision","Showcase preparation and homework"],"expected_output":"Complete campaign plan (all twelve sections); Campaign budget in KES with cost-per-result estimate (or zero-budget time plan); KPI list with targets and a tracking sheet; Peer-test feedback and a revision list","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners understand how paid online advertising works and when a small business should use it, plan a realistic KES campaign budget, choose key performance indicators and how to track them, and assemble every part of their campaign into one presentable plan that they test with peers.","objectives":["Explain the difference between boosting a post and creating an ad in Meta Ads Manager, and give an overview of Google Ads","Choose an ad objective, audience, creative and budget for a small campaign","Build a campaign budget in KES covering content, data, ads and other costs, with a cost-per-result estimate","Explain reach, impressions, engagement rate, clicks, messages, conversions, cost per result and return on ad spend","Read basic insights on Facebook, Instagram or WhatsApp Business and track offline results with simple codes","Assemble a complete campaign plan with a SMART objective, KPIs and a timeline","Improve the campaign based on peer testing"],"topics":[{"title":"Introduction to online advertising","subtopics":["Organic versus paid reach","Boosted posts versus Ads Manager","Objectives (awareness, traffic, engagement, messages, sales)","Audience targeting (location, age, interests, custom audiences)","Ad creative and copy","Ad policies and honest advertising","Overview of Google Search ads","Micro-influencers and paid partnerships (disclosure)"]},{"title":"Campaign planning and budgeting","subtopics":["SMART campaign objectives","Campaign timeline","Budget lines in KES (content, data, ads, printing, delivery, incentives)","Daily versus lifetime budget","Estimating cost per result","Starting small and testing"]},{"title":"Marketing analytics and performance measurement","subtopics":["Key performance indicators linked to objectives","Reach, impressions, engagement rate","Clicks and messages","Conversions and cost per conversion","Return on ad spend","Platform insights","Tracking offline sales (codes, asking 'how did you hear about us?')","Simple tracking sheet"]},{"title":"Practical campaign development","subtopics":["Putting the parts together","Checking consistency","Peer testing","Revising"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and results from your first post","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share the reach and engagement from the post you published."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Introduction to online advertising","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how paid ads work on social media and search, and when a small business should use them."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Campaign planning and budgeting in KES","kind":"lesson","summary":"Plan a campaign timeline and a realistic budget with an estimated cost per result."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Marketing analytics and measuring results","kind":"lesson","summary":"Choose KPIs for your objective and learn how to read and track the numbers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Campaign build","kind":"exercise","summary":"Assemble your complete campaign plan using the campaign template."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Peer testing and revision","kind":"group","summary":"Test your campaign with peers using the checklist and improve it."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase preparation and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Learn how session 8 works and what to prepare."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Cost-per-result calculation","instructions":"Given ad spend, conversations and orders, calculate cost per conversation and cost per order and decide if the ad was worth it."},{"title":"Match KPIs to objectives","instructions":"For three objectives (awareness, messages, sales), choose the right two KPIs each."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Campaign budget","instructions":"Build your campaign budget in KES with at least five lines and an estimated cost per result (or a zero-budget time plan)."},{"title":"Complete campaign plan","instructions":"Fill all twelve sections of the campaign template."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Campaign peer test","instructions":"Present your campaign in three minutes; peers use the checklist to give two strengths and two improvements."}],"examples":["Shiru's Bakes KES 3,000 campaign with KES 1,500 ads, aiming for 20 orders in two weeks.","A Kisumu fish seller using the code 'SAMAKI5' on WhatsApp status to see which customers came from the campaign.","A Nairobi youth tech hub boosting an event post to people aged 18 to 30 within 15 km.","A community clean-up group in Kitale running a zero-budget campaign through church and school WhatsApp groups (with admins' permission)."],"tools":["Campaign template, budget sheet and tracking sheet (Google Sheets or paper)","Screenshots of Meta boost and Ads Manager, and page insights","Calculator","Campaign checklist and showcase rubric"],"assessment":[{"question":"What is the difference between reach and impressions?"},{"question":"You spent KES 1,000 on ads, got 25 messages and 5 orders. What is the cost per order?"},{"question":"Which KPI fits the objective 'get 20 WhatsApp orders'?"},{"question":"Why should a business fix its offer and WhatsApp replies before paying for ads?"},{"question":"How can you measure sales that happen offline or on WhatsApp?"}],"expected_outputs":["Complete campaign plan (all twelve sections)","Campaign budget in KES with cost-per-result estimate (or zero-budget time plan)","KPI list with targets and a tracking sheet","Peer-test feedback and a revision list"],"homework":["Finish the campaign and run at least one part of it for real (posts, status or broadcast with consent) and record the results in the tracking sheet. Private option: run it as a private test (peers, or a WhatsApp group that agreed) or present it as a simulation with clearly labelled estimates.","Prepare a five-slide presentation or one-page visual for the showcase.","Practise your three-minute presentation twice, including your self-pitch introduction.","If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute campaign presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."],"cross_track_notes":["Entrepreneurship & Business covers business budgeting and cash flow for the whole business; Digital Marketing budgets only the campaign and calculates its cost per result.","AI Tools may be used to draft campaign copy or analyse results, with disclosure and human checking."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and results from your first post","steps":["Learners share the reach and engagement of the post they published. Note the range and ask what might explain the differences (time, hook, image, audience).","Say that today is about measuring and improving, not just posting."]},{"title":"Introduction to online advertising","steps":["Organic versus paid: organic posts reach a part of your followers; paid ads reach people chosen by targeting. Paid works best when the offer, content and WhatsApp follow-up are already good - ads amplify, they do not fix a weak offer.","Boost versus Ads Manager: boosting a post is quick (choose audience, budget, duration); Meta Ads Manager gives more control (objective, placements, multiple ads, testing). Show the boost screen and the Ads Manager campaign structure (campaign - objective; ad set - audience, budget, schedule; ad - creative and copy) using screenshots.","Objectives: choose the one that matches the campaign goal - for a WhatsApp business, a 'messages' or 'engagement with WhatsApp' style objective is often better than reach. Names change on the platforms, so check the current options in Ads Manager.","Targeting: location radius (for example 10 km around Ruiru), age, interests, and custom audiences (people who engaged with your page). Too narrow costs more; too broad wastes money.","Budget: platforms allow small daily budgets; check the current minimum in Ads Manager. Start small (for example a few hundred shillings a day for a few days), test two versions, keep the better one.","Google Search ads appear for searches with commercial intent ('cake delivery Juja'); you pay per click. Explain briefly; most learners will start with Meta.","Policies and ethics: ads are reviewed and can be rejected; no misleading claims, before-and-after body images, or restricted products. Paid influencer posts must be disclosed (for example 'ad' or 'paid partnership').","Payment: ad accounts are paid by card or other methods the platform accepts; the business owner must authorise any spending. Learners must never spend the owner's money without written agreement."]},{"title":"Campaign planning and budgeting in KES","steps":["SMART campaign objective: 'Get 20 new cake orders through WhatsApp from Ruiru and Juja between 1 and 14 December' rather than 'increase sales'.","Show the budget sheet (Google Sheets or paper): lines for content (printing, props, sample products for photos), internet data, ads, delivery promotion, incentives (discount cost), and contingency. Use the Shiru's Bakes example totalling about KES 3,000, including KES 1,500 for ads.","Cost per result: if KES 1,500 of ads brings 30 WhatsApp conversations and 10 orders, cost per conversation = KES 50, cost per order = KES 150. Compare with the profit per order (for example KES 600). If the profit is higher than the cost per order, the ads pay for themselves.","Zero-budget campaigns are valid - use organic posts, WhatsApp status, broadcast lists, community groups and referral offers. Learners with no ad budget plan time instead of money."]},{"title":"Marketing analytics and measuring results","steps":["Teach the measures: reach (unique people who saw it), impressions (times shown), engagement rate (likes, comments, shares, saves divided by reach), clicks, messages started, conversions (orders, bookings), cost per result, return on ad spend (revenue from ads divided by ad cost).","Link KPIs to objectives: an awareness campaign tracks reach and video views; a sales campaign tracks messages, orders and cost per order. Vanity metrics (likes alone) can mislead.","Show where to find numbers: Facebook and Instagram professional dashboards and insights, Meta Business Suite, WhatsApp Business (message statistics where available), TikTok analytics for business or creator accounts.","Track offline and WhatsApp sales: ask every customer 'how did you hear about us?', use a campaign code ('mention CAKE10 for 10% off'), and record orders in a simple tracking sheet (date, customer first name or code, source, amount, campaign code).","Show the tracking sheet and how to calculate weekly totals. Explain that learning from results ('reels brought more messages than photos, so do more reels') is the real point."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Campaign build","steps":["Learners open the campaign template and fill every section: 1 brief; 2 SMART objective; 3 persona; 4 channels and platform plan; 5 brand mini-kit; 6 key message and offer; 7 two-week content calendar; 8 three finished posts with captions and one WhatsApp broadcast or status message; 9 optional ad plan (objective, audience, creative, budget, duration); 10 budget in KES; 11 KPIs, targets and tracking method; 12 timeline and who does what.","The facilitator circulates and checks each campaign for consistency - does the persona match the platforms, the message match the brand, the KPI match the objective?","Learners who finish early create one more asset or a short reel for their campaign."]},{"title":"Peer testing and revision","steps":["In groups of three, each learner presents the campaign in three minutes. Peers use the campaign checklist (clear objective, specific persona, fitting channels, consistent brand, strong hooks, clear CTA, realistic budget, measurable KPIs, consent and honesty) and give two strengths and two improvements.","Learners make at least one improvement immediately and note the others to finish before session 8."]},{"title":"Showcase preparation and homework","steps":["Explain the showcase format: three minutes to present (introduce yourself with your self-pitch, the brief, the campaign, any results so far and what you would do next), two minutes of questions. Share the showcase rubric.","Homework: finish the campaign, run at least one part of it in real life (publish posts or a status campaign with permission) and record the results, prepare a five-slide presentation or a one-page visual."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Ads Manager structure and boosting a post","steps":["Using screenshots, walk through boost options and the campaign, ad set and ad levels. Do not spend money live."]},{"title":"Budget and cost-per-result calculation","steps":["Fill the budget sheet for Shiru's Bakes and calculate cost per message and per order, and compare with profit per order."]},{"title":"Reading insights","steps":["Show a page's insights (prepared screenshots) and explain reach, engagement and messages for one post."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What is the difference between reach and impressions?","guide":"Reach counts unique people; impressions count every time it was shown."},{"question":"You spent KES 1,000 on ads, got 25 messages and 5 orders. What is the cost per order?","guide":"KES 200 per order (1,000 / 5). Cost per message is KES 40."},{"question":"Which KPI fits the objective 'get 20 WhatsApp orders'?","guide":"Orders (conversions) and cost per order; messages started as a leading indicator."},{"question":"Why should a business fix its offer and WhatsApp replies before paying for ads?","guide":"Ads only amplify; if the offer or follow-up is weak, more people see a weak experience and money is wasted."},{"question":"How can you measure sales that happen offline or on WhatsApp?","guide":"Ask how they heard, campaign codes, tracking sheet, unique offers per channel."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare Ads Manager and boost screenshots (platform screens change; check them shortly before the session).","Prepare the campaign template, budget sheet, tracking sheet, checklist and showcase rubric.","Set the session 8 room plan from the track group size (up to 12 - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus a recorded-questions room) and line up co-facilitators and peer panels."],"delivery":["Do not spend money or ask learners to spend money in class. Advertising is optional; zero-budget campaigns get the same marks if well planned.","Spend most of your time circulating during the build; this is where learning is consolidated."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners must have the business owner's written agreement (a WhatsApp message is enough) before any spending or posting on the business's behalf.","Campaigns must not target minors or use content involving children without parental consent.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Review campaigns submitted to DIRA LEARN and flag any that are incomplete before session 8.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('digital', 8,
   $cv13${"title":"Digital marketing campaign showcase","description":"Present your digital marketing campaign, answer questions, give and receive feedback, pitch yourself professionally and plan your next 30 days.","objectives":["Present a basic digital marketing campaign clearly in three minutes, explaining objective, audience, channels, content, budget and measures","Report real results or test findings honestly and say what they would improve","Answer questions about their choices with evidence","Give and receive feedback professionally using the showcase rubric","Pitch themselves and their campaign as a portfolio piece to an employer or client","Write a 30-day action plan for continuing to market the business or to find marketing work"],"activities":["Showcase set-up and expectations","Campaign presentations and questions - block 1","Stretch break","Campaign presentations and questions - block 2","Feedback round","Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","30-day action plan","Celebration and close"],"expected_output":"Presented digital marketing campaign (final project) with rubric score and feedback; One-page campaign case study for the portfolio; 30-day action plan; Written feedback given to two peers","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 3 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners demonstrate their practical digital marketing capability by presenting the campaign they built, explaining their choices and results, and answering questions. They learn to present their work as a portfolio piece to employers and clients, practise professional behaviour in a showcase setting and leave with a 30-day action plan.","objectives":["Present a basic digital marketing campaign clearly in three minutes, explaining objective, audience, channels, content, budget and measures","Report real results or test findings honestly and say what they would improve","Answer questions about their choices with evidence","Give and receive feedback professionally using the showcase rubric","Pitch themselves and their campaign as a portfolio piece to an employer or client","Write a 30-day action plan for continuing to market the business or to find marketing work"],"topics":[{"title":"Practical digital marketing campaign presentation","subtopics":["Structure of a campaign presentation","Showing results honestly","Explaining choices with evidence","Answering questions"]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and your work","subtopics":["Self-pitch plus project","Turning the campaign into a portfolio case study","Talking about your work in interviews and with clients"]},{"title":"Professional behaviour and workplace readiness","subtopics":["Showcase etiquette (time, respect, listening)","Receiving critical feedback","Following up after an event","Pathways - internships, freelance and online work, running your own business"]},{"title":"Next steps","subtopics":["30-day action plan","Ongoing learning","Staying connected with DIRA alumni"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","kind":"introduction","summary":"Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Campaign presentations and questions - block 1","kind":"assessment","summary":"The first six presenters in each room present and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Campaign presentations and questions - block 2","kind":"assessment","summary":"The next six presenters in each room present; recorded presenters answer their live questions."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Feedback round","kind":"group","summary":"Share feedback with peers and reflect on what you heard."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","kind":"lesson","summary":"Turn your campaign into a portfolio piece and practise pitching yourself to an employer or client."},{"minutes":15,"title":"30-day action plan","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write a 30-day plan for continuing the campaign or finding marketing work."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Celebration and close","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Celebrate the work and learn what happens next."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Campaign case study","instructions":"Turn your campaign into a one-page case study with challenge, actions, results, learning and visuals."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Campaign presentation","instructions":"Present your campaign in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."},{"title":"30-day action plan","instructions":"Complete the plan with a goal, four weekly actions, a measure, an accountability partner and one risk."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer feedback","instructions":"Give rubric-based written feedback (one strength, one improvement) to the two presenters assigned to you."},{"title":"Pitch practice","instructions":"Practise your self-pitch with your campaign as proof, in pairs."}],"examples":["A learner who presents a two-week campaign for a Ruiru bakery with 14 new orders and a cost per order of about KES 85.","A learner who ran a zero-budget campaign for a Kibera youth recycling group and recruited 25 volunteers through WhatsApp groups.","A learner who uses the campaign as proof in an application for a marketing assistant internship at a Nairobi SME."],"tools":["Learners' presentations (slides, one-page visual or phone screen sharing)","Showcase rubric and feedback cards (or DIRA LEARN)","Timer","30-day action plan template and case study template"],"assessment":[{"question":"Presentation scored with the showcase rubric (objective and audience, content quality, channel and budget choices, measurement and results, presentation and answers)."},{"question":"What would you change in your campaign if you ran it again, and why?"},{"question":"Deliver your pitch using the campaign as proof."}],"expected_outputs":["Presented digital marketing campaign (final project) with rubric score and feedback","One-page campaign case study for the portfolio","30-day action plan","Written feedback given to two peers"],"homework":["Submit the final campaign, case study and 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.","Send a thank-you message and results summary to the business owner within two days."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track ends with a showcase using the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment, so all learners leave with a portfolio piece and a 30-day plan; the rubric differs by track."],"track_project":"A basic digital marketing campaign for a real brief (a local business, youth enterprise, community organisation or the learner's own idea). It contains a SMART objective, a customer persona, a channel plan, a brand mini-kit, a two-week content calendar, at least three finished posts with captions, a WhatsApp Business plan, a small KES budget, the measures of success and a short results or test report, presented in session 8. Privacy option: a learner who cannot or does not wish to publish publicly may run the campaign as a private test (peers, or a small WhatsApp group whose members agreed to take part) or build it for the simulated brief provided by the facilitator, with mock-up posts and clearly labelled estimated results; it is assessed on the same rubric. A real business's name, logo, photos, prices or customer information are used only with the owner's written permission."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","steps":["Welcome learners and any guests (business owners, DIRA staff, mentors, invited employers). Thank business owners who allowed learners to work with them.","Explain the format: three minutes to present, two minutes of questions, then the next presenter. Share the rubric: clarity of objective and audience (20%), quality and consistency of content (25%), channel and budget choices (20%), measurement and results (20%), presentation and answers (15%).","Etiquette: phones on silent, cameras on if online, respectful questions, applause after each presenter.","Assign each learner to give written feedback on two specific presenters (so every presenter gets at least two pieces of peer feedback)."]},{"title":"Campaign presentations and questions - block 1","steps":["Run the campaigns with the session 8 room plan in the facilitator instructions: the first six presenters in each room go in this block, each with 3 minutes plus 2 minutes of questions.","Learners present in the published order. Keep strict time with a visible timer; give a 30-second warning.","Each presentation covers: self-pitch introduction; the brief; SMART objective; persona; channels; brand and three posts; budget; KPIs and results or test findings; what they learned and would do next.","Ask at least one question per presenter, such as: 'Why did you choose this platform for this persona?', 'What would you change if you had KES 5,000 more?', 'What does your cost per result tell you?', 'How did you get consent for these photos?'","Score each presenter on the rubric and note one strength and one improvement."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Campaign presentations and questions - block 2","steps":["The remaining presenters in each live room (7 to 12) go now, with the same timer, rubric and question approach as block 1.","In a recorded-questions room, do not replay the recordings (the panel scored them before the session); give each recorded presenter 2 minutes of live questions about their campaign.","Each room's panel hands its completed campaign rubric sheets to the room facilitator before the feedback round."]},{"title":"Feedback round","steps":["Learners give their written feedback to the two presenters they were assigned (in DIRA LEARN or on cards).","Facilitator summarises patterns: what strong campaigns had in common (specific persona, consistent brand, clear CTA, honest measurement) and the most common improvements.","Model receiving feedback - thank, ask for an example, decide what to act on."]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","steps":["Show how to turn the campaign into a one-page case study: the challenge, what you did, results, what you learned, and visuals. Add it to LinkedIn (Featured section), a Google Drive portfolio folder or a simple online portfolio.","Pitch formula for interviews and clients: who you are, what you can do, proof (this campaign and its numbers), what you are looking for. Example: 'I am Brian, a digital marketer from Thika. I recently ran a two-week WhatsApp and Facebook campaign for a bakery that brought 14 new orders for KES 1,200 in ads. I am looking for an internship or small businesses that need help with social media.'","Pathways: internships and entry-level jobs (marketing assistant, social media assistant, customer service), freelance and online work (Kenya's Ajira Digital programme provides training and information on online work), and supporting or running a business. Warn again about job scams that ask for fees.","Professional follow-up: within two days, thank the business owner, share the results summary and ask for a short testimonial or reference; connect with guests on LinkedIn with a personal note.","Learners practise their pitch once in pairs."]},{"title":"30-day action plan","steps":["Learners complete the 30-day action plan: one main goal, four weekly actions (for example week 1 finish the case study, week 2 offer services to three businesses, week 3 publish two portfolio posts, week 4 apply to two internships), one measure of success, one person who will hold them accountable and one risk with a plan.","Learners share their main goal with their accountability partner from core session 1.","Remind learners that the plan is part of their final project submission in DIRA LEARN."]},{"title":"Celebration and close","steps":["Celebrate - a short recognition of every learner's campaign (for example one word of praise each).","Explain next steps - submitting the final project, how results and certificates work in DIRA LEARN according to the programme's rules, and how to stay connected with DIRA."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Model presentation","steps":["Before the session, prepare a 3-minute example presentation (Shiru's Bakes) and share it as a reference recording or slide set for learners who want an example. Do not use showcase time for it."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Presentation scored with the showcase rubric (objective and audience, content quality, channel and budget choices, measurement and results, presentation and answers).","guide":"Use the rubric descriptors. Results do not need to be large; honest measurement and good reasoning score well."},{"question":"What would you change in your campaign if you ran it again, and why?","guide":"Look for a change based on evidence from results or feedback."},{"question":"Deliver your pitch using the campaign as proof.","guide":"Who, what, proof with a number, ask."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Publish the presentation order in advance and confirm each learner's materials work (screen sharing or slides).","Invite business owners and guests at least a week before; brief guests on respectful questioning.","Confirm the room plan: count the learners in the track group, book the rooms and co-facilitators, brief the peer panels, and publish each room's presenter order and the list of recorded presenters by the end of session 7."],"delivery":["Be strict on time but warm; nervous presenters can use notes.","Celebrate honest reporting of weak results - the learning is what matters.","Session 8 room plan - Room plan (set in session 7 from the track group size; the session is never extended): up to 12 learners - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms in parallel; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus one recorded-questions room. Each live room takes at most 12 presenters: 6 in each 30-minute block, 3 minutes to present and 2 minutes of questions each.","Every room has a facilitator or briefed co-facilitator (another trainer, a mentor or DIRA staff member who knows the rubric and the safeguarding expectations) and a peer panel of two learners who present in the other block. All rooms use the same rubric.","Recorded option: learners in a recorded-questions room, and anyone who cannot present live (absence, connection problems, anxiety, or the privacy option), submit a 3-minute recording - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - by the day before session 8. The panel scores it in advance with the same rubric; in session 8 each recorded presenter answers 2 minutes of live questions (12 per block). If more learners need the recorded option than one room can take, the panel sends written questions and the learner answers them in DIRA LEARN.","Timekeeping: a visible timer with a 30-second warning; stop at 3 minutes. If a block runs late, cut questions to 1 minute - do not extend the session."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners who cannot present live (connectivity, illness) can submit a recorded presentation according to the programme's rules.","Guests must not contact learners privately without going through the programme; remind them of the safeguarding expectations."],"after_session":["Enter rubric scores and feedback in DIRA LEARN grading for the final project.","Share the case study template and collect business owners' feedback where offered.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 3,
   $cv13${"title":"Understanding AI, AI tools and responsible use","description":"What artificial intelligence is, the main types of AI tools and what they are used for, and the principles of responsible and ethical AI use.","objectives":["Explain in plain words what AI, machine learning and generative AI are, and how a chat assistant produces an answer","Identify AI already used in everyday Kenyan life (spam filters, maps, fraud alerts, translation, customer-care chatbots)","Name at least six types of AI tools and a practical use for each","Complete three AI-assisted tasks and record what the learner checked or changed","Explain five principles of responsible AI use (honesty, privacy, accuracy, fairness, human responsibility)","Agree and sign a group AI use code"],"activities":["Welcome to AI Tools and track overview","What AI is and how it works","Types and uses of AI tools","Demonstration - three AI tasks and what needed checking","Stretch break","Your three AI tasks","Responsible AI scenarios and group AI use code","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"AI task log with three completed tasks and checks; Signed group AI use code with the disclosure line format","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners build an accurate mental model of AI - what it is, how today's generative tools produce answers and why they make mistakes - and survey the main types of AI tools. They try three practical tasks, record what needed human checking, and agree a group code for responsible and ethical AI use that applies for the rest of the track.","objectives":["Explain in plain words what AI, machine learning and generative AI are, and how a chat assistant produces an answer","Identify AI already used in everyday Kenyan life (spam filters, maps, fraud alerts, translation, customer-care chatbots)","Name at least six types of AI tools and a practical use for each","Complete three AI-assisted tasks and record what the learner checked or changed","Explain five principles of responsible AI use (honesty, privacy, accuracy, fairness, human responsibility)","Agree and sign a group AI use code"],"topics":[{"title":"Understanding artificial intelligence","subtopics":["What AI is and is not","Machine learning - learning patterns from examples","Generative AI and large language models in simple terms","Why AI can be confidently wrong (hallucinations)","AI already around us in Kenya"]},{"title":"Types and uses of AI tools","subtopics":["Chat assistants (ChatGPT, Gemini, Copilot, Claude, Meta AI)","Image and design tools","Voice, transcription and translation","Research tools that show sources","AI inside everyday apps (email, documents, WhatsApp, Canva)","Coding and data assistants","Free versus paid versions and data limits"]},{"title":"Responsible and ethical AI use","subtopics":["Honesty and disclosure","Academic and workplace integrity","Privacy and personal data","Accuracy and verification","Fairness and bias","Deepfakes and misuse","Humans stay responsible for decisions"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Welcome to AI Tools and track overview","kind":"introduction","summary":"Meet your track group and see the six sessions and final AI project."},{"minutes":20,"title":"What AI is and how it works","kind":"lesson","summary":"Understand AI, machine learning and generative AI in plain language, and why AI makes mistakes."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Types and uses of AI tools","kind":"lesson","summary":"Survey the main families of AI tools and what each is good for."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Demonstration - three AI tasks and what needed checking","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Watch an AI assistant explain, draft and summarise, and see the facilitator check each output."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Your three AI tasks","kind":"exercise","summary":"Complete three AI-assisted tasks of your own and fill in the AI task log."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Responsible AI scenarios and group AI use code","kind":"group","summary":"Discuss real ethical dilemmas and agree how the group will use AI responsibly."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer quick questions on how AI works and responsible use."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Tool matching","instructions":"Match five everyday tasks to the right family of AI tool and say why."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"AI task log","instructions":"Complete three real AI-assisted tasks (explain, draft, summarise) and record prompt, quality, checks, changes and decision."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Responsible AI scenarios","instructions":"Discuss an ethical scenario, identify harms and the responsible choice, and report back."},{"title":"Group AI use code","instructions":"Agree six to eight rules for responsible AI use, including a disclosure line, and sign it."}],"examples":["Fraud alerts on mobile money and bank transactions as everyday machine learning.","A Kitengela hardware shop using an AI-drafted, owner-checked set of replies to common customer questions.","A university student in Eldoret using AI to explain a statistics concept and then checking it against lecture notes.","Viral AI-generated fake images and voice notes shared on Kenyan social media, and why checking matters."],"tools":["At least one free AI chat assistant available in Kenya","AI task log template and scenario cards","A public, non-personal article or notice for the summarising demo","Shared document or flip chart for the group AI use code","Free and low-data options: the free tier of an AI assistant in a phone browser (no paid plan or app needed); text chat uses little data; one shared phone or laptop per pair or three; printed scenario cards."],"assessment":[{"question":"In simple words, how does a chat assistant produce an answer?"},{"question":"What is a hallucination?"},{"question":"Name three types of AI tools and one use for each."},{"question":"Give two examples of information you must never put into an AI tool."},{"question":"Write your disclosure line for a piece of work AI helped with."}],"expected_outputs":["AI task log with three completed tasks and checks","Signed group AI use code with the disclosure line format"],"homework":["Use AI for one more real task this week and add it to your task log with your checks. Do not paste personal data (ID numbers, phone numbers, health details) or a business's private information into an AI tool without permission.","Find one example of AI in a Kenyan service you use and explain in two sentences what it does.","Think of a real person, business or group you could help with an AI-assisted solution for your final project."],"cross_track_notes":["Core session 2 introduced AI awareness for everyone. Here learners study how AI works and the full range of tools, which the rest of the track builds on."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Welcome to AI Tools and track overview","steps":["Show the track journey: S3 understanding AI and responsible use, S4 prompting, research and fact-checking, S5 writing, images and presentations, S6 productivity, business, automation, privacy and bias, S7 project build and testing, S8 showcase.","Show one example final project (for example a 'Customer reply kit' made with AI for a Kitengela hardware shop - prompts, checked answers, a test with the owner). Say that by session 8 each learner will demonstrate one like it.","Quick poll: which AI tools have you used, and for what? Collect answers in the chat or on the board."]},{"title":"What AI is and how it works","steps":["Define AI: computer systems that do tasks that usually need human intelligence - recognising images, understanding language, making predictions, generating text or pictures.","Explain machine learning with an example: an M-Pesa-style fraud system learns from millions of past transactions what normal and suspicious patterns look like, then flags unusual ones. Nobody writes a rule for every case; the system learns patterns from examples.","Explain generative AI and large language models: they were trained on enormous amounts of text and learned to predict the next word. When you ask a question, they produce a likely-sounding answer word by word. That is why they write fluently - and why they can invent facts, quotes, references and numbers that sound right but are false. This is called a hallucination.","Everyday AI in Kenya: email spam filters, Google Maps traffic estimates, phone camera face detection, translation apps, bank and mobile money fraud alerts, customer-care chatbots on company websites and WhatsApp, social media feeds choosing what you see.","Three key limits to remember: AI does not know your situation unless you tell it; its information may be out of date; it can repeat biases from its training data. AI is a powerful assistant, not an authority."]},{"title":"Types and uses of AI tools","steps":["Walk through the tool families with one use each: chat assistants (draft, explain, brainstorm); image tools (create illustrations, edit photos); voice and transcription (turn a recorded meeting into notes); translation (English to Kiswahili, with checking); research tools that cite sources; AI inside apps (Canva's AI features, writing help in Google Docs or Microsoft tools, Meta AI in WhatsApp); coding and spreadsheet assistants (explain a formula).","Free versus paid: many tools have free tiers with limits; some features need payment or a specific country; terms and availability change, so check the tool's current terms. Data use: free tools may use your inputs to improve their models unless you change settings.","Ask learners to match five everyday tasks (writing a CV summary, transcribing a voice note, designing a poster, translating a notice, checking a fact) to a tool family."]},{"title":"Demonstration - three AI tasks and what needed checking","steps":["Task 1, explain: 'Explain what a KRA PIN is and why a young person starting work needs one, in simple English, 100 words.' Read the answer and point out what you would confirm on the official KRA website.","Task 2, draft: 'Draft a polite WhatsApp message to a client asking for payment of an overdue invoice of KES 4,500 for a logo design.' Show edits to tone and details.","Task 3, summarise: paste a public, non-personal article or notice (for example a county youth-fund announcement) and ask for five bullet points. Compare with the original; find anything missed or wrong.","Model the AI task log - task, tool, prompt, what was good, what I checked or changed, final decision."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Your three AI tasks","steps":["Learners choose three real tasks from their life or work - one explain, one draft, one summarise (for example explain a topic from their course, draft a message to a landlord or customer, summarise a public notice).","For each, they fill the AI task log: tool, prompt, output quality (1 to 5), what they checked and how (source, own knowledge, asking someone), what they changed, and whether they would use the output.","Remind learners of the privacy rule - no personal data, passwords, ID numbers, other people's private messages or confidential work information in prompts.","Learners without a device pair up; one types, both evaluate."]},{"title":"Responsible AI scenarios and group AI use code","steps":["Groups of four discuss one scenario each: (1) a college student submits an AI-written assignment as their own; (2) a job seeker asks AI to add experience they do not have to a CV; (3) someone makes an AI image of a classmate in an embarrassing situation; (4) a small business pastes customers' names and phone numbers into an AI tool to write personalised messages; (5) a youth group shares an AI-written health claim on WhatsApp without checking.","Each group answers: what is the harm, who is affected, what would be the responsible choice? 8 minutes. Groups report back in one minute each.","Draw out five principles: honesty and disclosure (say when AI helped); privacy (protect personal data - Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019 gives people rights over their personal data); accuracy (verify before sharing); fairness (watch for bias and stereotypes); human responsibility (you are accountable for what you submit, send or publish).","Agree the group AI use code: six to eight short rules written by the group, including a disclosure line format ('AI-assisted: drafted with [tool], edited and checked by me'). Everyone agrees in the chat or by signing.","Note that making or sharing sexual or humiliating fake images of a real person can cause serious harm, can be a crime and must be reported."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the assessment questions as a quick quiz and discuss the answers."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Each learner shares one surprising thing they learned about AI today.","Set the homework and post the group AI use code in the cohort space."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Three AI tasks with checking","steps":["Run explain, draft and summarise tasks live; for each, show the checking step and the task log entry."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"In simple words, how does a chat assistant produce an answer?","guide":"It predicts likely next words based on patterns learned from huge amounts of text; it does not look facts up unless connected to search."},{"question":"What is a hallucination?","guide":"A confident but false or invented output (fake facts, references, quotes or numbers)."},{"question":"Name three types of AI tools and one use for each.","guide":"Any three tool families with a sensible use."},{"question":"Give two examples of information you must never put into an AI tool.","guide":"Passwords and PINs, ID numbers, other people's personal data, health information, confidential work data."},{"question":"Write your disclosure line for a piece of work AI helped with.","guide":"Names the tool and what the learner did (edited, checked)."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Test the AI tools on the venue internet; prepare screenshots of the three demo tasks as a backup.","Choose a public, non-personal text for the summarising demo.","Print or share the task log template and scenario cards."],"delivery":["Keep explanations concrete; avoid technical jargon unless a learner asks.","Model checking every single time you show an AI output."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Some scenarios (fake images) may relate to learners' experiences. Allow learners to pass, and follow the DIRA LEARN safeguarding process if anyone discloses image-based abuse.","Do not require learners to create accounts that need personal data they are not comfortable sharing; use pairs or the facilitator's demo instead.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Post the group AI use code in the cohort space and upload slides and templates.","Review task logs and note common checking gaps to address in session 4.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 4,
   $cv13${"title":"Prompt engineering, AI for research and learning, and fact-checking","description":"Write clear and effective prompts, use AI to research and learn, and evaluate and fact-check AI outputs before using them.","objectives":["Use the RTCF prompt framework (Role, Task, Context, Format) plus constraints and examples","Apply at least four prompt patterns (step by step, ask me questions first, critique and improve, give examples)","Iterate a prompt and explain why the output improved","Use AI to learn a topic (explain, quiz, summarise, compare) and to plan research","Fact-check AI outputs using lateral reading and official or reliable sources","Produce a short research brief in which every key claim has a verified source"],"activities":["Recap and task log review","Prompt engineering - framework and patterns","Prompt lab","AI for research and learning","Stretch break","Demonstration and practice - fact-checking AI outputs","Verified research brief","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Prompt library v1 (three tested prompts); Fact-check record for one AI answer; Verified research brief with sources and disclosure","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners move from casual questions to deliberate prompting. They learn a prompt framework and advanced patterns, use AI as a study and research partner, and apply a fact-checking routine to AI outputs. They produce the first version of their personal prompt library and a verified research brief.","objectives":["Use the RTCF prompt framework (Role, Task, Context, Format) plus constraints and examples","Apply at least four prompt patterns (step by step, ask me questions first, critique and improve, give examples)","Iterate a prompt and explain why the output improved","Use AI to learn a topic (explain, quiz, summarise, compare) and to plan research","Fact-check AI outputs using lateral reading and official or reliable sources","Produce a short research brief in which every key claim has a verified source"],"topics":[{"title":"Prompt engineering","subtopics":["The RTCF framework - Role, Task, Context, Format","Constraints (length, tone, audience, language)","Giving examples (few-shot prompting)","Prompt patterns - step by step, ask me questions first, critique and improve, compare options","Iterating and follow-up prompts","Building a reusable prompt library"]},{"title":"AI for research and learning","subtopics":["AI as a tutor - explain, simplify, quiz me","Summarising long texts you are allowed to use","Planning research questions","AI research tools that show sources","Limits - invented references and outdated facts"]},{"title":"Fact-checking and evaluating AI outputs","subtopics":["Stop - do not share yet","Lateral reading (check what other sources say)","Official and reliable sources in Kenya (government, KNBS, Kenya Law, recognised media, academic sources)","Checking numbers, dates, names, quotes and references","Accuracy, relevance, completeness and bias checklist"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and task log review","kind":"introduction","summary":"Review what needed checking in your AI tasks."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Prompt engineering - framework and patterns","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the RTCF framework and prompt patterns that produce better outputs."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Prompt lab","kind":"exercise","summary":"Rewrite weak prompts into strong ones, compare outputs and start your prompt library."},{"minutes":15,"title":"AI for research and learning","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn to use AI as a tutor and research planner without trusting it blindly."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Demonstration and practice - fact-checking AI outputs","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Watch the fact-checking routine applied to an AI answer, then use it yourself."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Verified research brief","kind":"exercise","summary":"Use AI to plan and draft a short research brief, then verify every key claim."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer questions on prompting, research and fact-checking."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Prompt rewrite","instructions":"Rewrite four weak prompts with RTCF for a specific Kenyan situation and compare outputs."},{"title":"Claim check","instructions":"Mark each claim in an AI answer as confirmed, wrong or not found, with the source used."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Prompt library v1","instructions":"Save your three best prompts with their purpose, the prompt text and tips for using them."},{"title":"Verified research brief","instructions":"Draft a 200-word brief with AI help, verify at least five claims and list sources beside them."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Prompt swap","instructions":"Test a partner's prompt and suggest one improvement."}],"examples":["A Mombasa hospitality graduate using RTCF to get CV bullet points for hotel jobs.","Checking business-name registration steps against the official eCitizen Business Registration Service pages instead of trusting an AI answer.","An AI tool inventing academic references on youth unemployment in Kenya that do not exist.","A KCSE leaver using AI to quiz themselves on a topic before a college entry test."],"tools":["AI chat assistant (and, if available, one that shows sources)","Search engine and official websites (eCitizen, KRA, Kenya Law, KNBS)","Prompt library template, evaluation checklist and research brief template","Low-data option: text prompts only; keep the prompt library as a phone note or on paper; use the text pages of official sites rather than videos."],"assessment":[{"question":"What do the letters RTCF stand for?"},{"question":"Improve this prompt - 'make me a business plan'."},{"question":"What is lateral reading?"},{"question":"An AI gives you a reference to a report. How do you check it?"},{"question":"Name four things to check in an AI output."}],"expected_outputs":["Prompt library v1 (three tested prompts)","Fact-check record for one AI answer","Verified research brief with sources and disclosure"],"homework":["Add two more prompts to your prompt library and test them on real tasks.","Choose your final project brief and write one paragraph - who it helps, what problem it solves, and how AI will help."],"cross_track_notes":["Other tracks use AI only as an optional helper. The AI Tools track teaches prompting and verification as core skills that learners can then apply to marketing, content or business work."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and task log review","steps":["Ask learners what they had to check or change in their task logs. Group the answers (wrong facts, generic tone, missing local detail, too long).","Say that today they learn to get better outputs (prompting) and to catch problems (fact-checking)."]},{"title":"Prompt engineering - framework and patterns","steps":["Teach RTCF. Role - who the AI should act as ('You are a careers adviser in Kenya'). Task - exactly what to do ('Write three bullet points for a CV'). Context - the details it needs ('for Amina, 22, diploma in hospitality from Mombasa, two years of part-time waitressing'). Format - how the answer should look ('three bullets, each under 15 words, British English').","Add constraints and examples: tone, audience, length, language (English, Kiswahili), what to avoid. Give an example of the style you want; AI copies examples well.","Prompt patterns: (1) Step by step - 'Explain step by step'; (2) Ask me questions first - 'Before you answer, ask me up to five questions you need'; (3) Critique and improve - 'Here is my draft; list three weaknesses, then rewrite it'; (4) Compare options - 'Give three options with pros and cons in a table'; (5) Role-play - 'Act as a tough interviewer for a bank teller job and ask me one question at a time'.","Iteration: the first answer is a draft. Follow up - 'shorter', 'more local examples', 'simpler words', 'make it sound like a 20-year-old wrote it, not a robot'.","Show a weak and a strong prompt side by side with their outputs."]},{"title":"Prompt lab","steps":["Learners get four weak prompts ('write about farming', 'make a CV', 'business ideas', 'explain taxes') and rewrite each using RTCF for a specific Kenyan situation.","They run their weak and strong versions and rate both outputs from 1 to 5, noting why the strong one is better.","Learners save their best three prompts in a prompt library (document or note) with the purpose, the prompt text and tips.","Pairs swap one prompt and test each other's; they suggest one improvement."]},{"title":"AI for research and learning","steps":["AI as a tutor: 'Explain [topic] as if I am in Form Four, then give me five quiz questions, then mark my answers.' Show this live with a topic like compound interest or photosynthesis.","Summarising: only paste texts you are allowed to share (public documents, your own notes). Ask for a summary plus 'what is missing or uncertain?'.","Research planning: ask AI to suggest research questions, keywords and types of sources, then do the actual research with search engines and reliable sources.","Research tools that show sources (some assistants can search the web and cite pages) are more checkable, but the sources must still be opened and read - AI sometimes cites pages that do not say what it claims, or invents references entirely.","Learning integrity: using AI to understand is good; submitting AI work as your own understanding is not. Follow your institution's rules."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Demonstration and practice - fact-checking AI outputs","steps":["Ask AI a factual question with Kenyan specifics (for example 'What are the requirements to register a business name in Kenya and how much does it cost?'). Read the answer.","Apply the routine. Stop - do not share yet. Identify the claims (requirements, costs, process). Lateral reading - search each claim and open official sources (eCitizen Business Registration Service pages for business names; KRA for tax; Kenya Law for laws; KNBS for statistics). Compare. Mark each claim as confirmed, wrong or not found.","Show a fake reference: ask the AI for 'three academic references on youth unemployment in Kenya' and check whether each actually exists (search the exact title). Explain that invented references are common.","Show the evaluation checklist - accuracy, relevance, completeness, up to date, bias or one-sidedness, appropriate for the audience.","Learners apply the routine to one AI answer from their own task log or prompt lab and record the result."]},{"title":"Verified research brief","steps":["Learners choose a question linked to their final project idea (for example 'What support exists for young farmers in Nakuru County?' or 'What do small shops in my area struggle with online?').","They use AI to plan the question and draft a 200-word brief, then verify at least five claims with real sources, correcting or removing anything unverified.","The final brief lists sources (title and link) beside each claim and ends with the AI disclosure line."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the assessment questions and discuss answers."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask learners to name their best prompt pattern so far.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Weak versus strong prompt","steps":["Run a weak prompt, then the RTCF version, and compare outputs aloud."]},{"title":"Fact-checking routine and fake references","steps":["Verify an AI answer claim by claim against official sources and test whether AI-provided references exist."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What do the letters RTCF stand for?","guide":"Role, Task, Context, Format."},{"question":"Improve this prompt - 'make me a business plan'.","guide":"Should add role, specific business, location, audience, length and format."},{"question":"What is lateral reading?","guide":"Checking a claim by opening other reliable sources rather than relying on the one answer."},{"question":"An AI gives you a reference to a report. How do you check it?","guide":"Search the exact title and author, find the original, confirm it exists and says what was claimed."},{"question":"Name four things to check in an AI output.","guide":"Accuracy, relevance, completeness, currency, bias, suitability for audience."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare weak prompts, a factual question with Kenyan specifics and the fact-checking routine slide.","Check that the official websites load on the venue internet; prepare screenshots as a backup."],"delivery":["Have learners type and test, not just listen; the prompt lab is the core of the session.","When fact-checking, verify live even if it is slow - learners must see the effort it takes."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Remind learners to paste only public or their own content when summarising.","Health, legal and financial AI answers must always be checked with qualified sources; say so explicitly.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Give feedback on research briefs, especially the quality of sources.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 5,
   $cv13${"title":"AI-assisted writing, images, creative work and presentations","description":"Use AI responsibly to draft and improve writing, create images and creative work, and build presentations, keeping your own voice and checking every output.","objectives":["Use the human-first-and-last workflow (plan, prompt, edit, check, disclose) for writing","Use AI to draft, edit, shorten, change tone and translate, while keeping their own voice and facts","Write image prompts with subject, setting, style, lighting and composition, and evaluate results","Explain the risks of AI images (deepfakes, consent, bias, copyright uncertainty) and label AI images honestly","Plan and build a five-slide presentation with AI support and check it for accuracy and clarity","Disclose AI assistance appropriately"],"activities":["Recap and project brief check","AI-assisted writing","Write with AI in your own voice","AI images and creative work","Stretch break","Create an AI-assisted image","AI for presentations - demonstration and build","Gallery review and knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"AI-assisted writing piece (draft and edited version); Labelled AI-assisted image with prompt notes; Five-slide presentation with speaker notes and disclosure","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners apply prompting to three common outputs - writing, images and presentations - with a 'human first and last' workflow. They produce an AI-assisted piece of writing in their own voice, an AI-assisted image with an honest label, and a short presentation, all checked and disclosed.","objectives":["Use the human-first-and-last workflow (plan, prompt, edit, check, disclose) for writing","Use AI to draft, edit, shorten, change tone and translate, while keeping their own voice and facts","Write image prompts with subject, setting, style, lighting and composition, and evaluate results","Explain the risks of AI images (deepfakes, consent, bias, copyright uncertainty) and label AI images honestly","Plan and build a five-slide presentation with AI support and check it for accuracy and clarity","Disclose AI assistance appropriately"],"topics":[{"title":"AI-assisted writing","subtopics":["Human first and last workflow","Drafting, editing, shortening and changing tone","Keeping your own voice","Translation and checking Kiswahili or other languages","CVs, cover letters and emails - truthfully","Academic integrity and plagiarism"]},{"title":"AI-generated images and creative work","subtopics":["How image prompts work - subject, setting, style, lighting, composition","Editing and variations","Common image problems (hands, text, odd details)","Bias in generated people and places","Deepfakes, consent and real people","Copyright uncertainty and labelling AI images"]},{"title":"AI for presentations","subtopics":["Outlining a presentation with AI","One idea per slide","AI features in slide and design tools","Checking facts, numbers and images","Speaker notes and rehearsal"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and project brief check","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share your final project idea in one sentence."},{"minutes":20,"title":"AI-assisted writing","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn a responsible workflow for writing with AI while keeping your own voice."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Write with AI in your own voice","kind":"exercise","summary":"Produce a short piece of real writing with AI help and edit it into your own voice."},{"minutes":20,"title":"AI images and creative work","kind":"demonstration","summary":"See how image prompts work, what goes wrong, and how to use AI images responsibly."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Create an AI-assisted image","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write an image prompt, improve it twice and label the final image."},{"minutes":20,"title":"AI for presentations - demonstration and build","kind":"exercise","summary":"Outline and build a five-slide presentation with AI support and check it."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Gallery review and knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Review each other's work and answer quick questions."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Remove the robot","instructions":"Take a generic AI paragraph and rewrite it with your own voice, a personal detail and a local example."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"AI-assisted writing piece","instructions":"Produce a real piece of writing using the five-step workflow, showing the AI draft and your edited version."},{"title":"AI-assisted image","instructions":"Create and label an image for your project using a five-part prompt improved twice. Do not create images of real, identifiable people without their consent."},{"title":"Five-slide presentation","instructions":"Build a five-slide presentation with speaker notes, checked facts and a disclosure line."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Voice check","instructions":"A partner reads your final writing and says whether it sounds like you."},{"title":"Gallery review","instructions":"Comment on two classmates' images and slides with one strength and one fix."}],"examples":["A hospitality graduate improving a cover letter for a hotel in Diani without adding false experience.","A youth group in Machakos preparing a talk on safe AI use for job seekers.","AI images that wrongly show Kenyan markets or put the wrong currency on price tags.","A Kakamega farmers' group using an AI illustration for a training poster, labelled as AI-generated."],"tools":["AI chat assistant; a free AI image tool available in Kenya","Google Slides, PowerPoint or Canva","Writing workflow card and image prompt template","Free or low-cost alternatives: free tiers of image tools (daily limits apply) - or, where image generation is not possible, write and improve the image prompt and sketch the result on paper; Google Slides or Canva free, or the phone's built-in office app offline. At most two generated images per learner on mobile data."],"assessment":[{"question":"What are the five steps of the human-first-and-last workflow?"},{"question":"Why must you never let AI add experience to your CV?"},{"question":"Name the five parts of a good image prompt."},{"question":"When should a business use a real photo instead of an AI image?"},{"question":"What should the last slide of an AI-assisted presentation include?"}],"expected_outputs":["AI-assisted writing piece (draft and edited version)","Labelled AI-assisted image with prompt notes","Five-slide presentation with speaker notes and disclosure"],"homework":["Improve your presentation using the gallery feedback.","Write the project plan for session 7 - problem, user, AI tools you will use, human checks, and how you will test it with a real user."],"cross_track_notes":["Content Creation teaches photography, video and design by hand; AI Tools teaches when and how AI can assist creative work and its limits. Digital Marketing uses captions and graphics for a campaign; AI Tools focuses on the AI workflow and checking."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and project brief check","steps":["Each learner shares their final project brief in one sentence. Note them for session 7.","Introduce the human-first-and-last idea - you decide the purpose and facts first, and you edit and check last."]},{"title":"AI-assisted writing","steps":["Workflow: (1) plan - write your purpose, audience and key points yourself; (2) prompt - give AI your points and ask for a draft; (3) edit - rewrite in your voice, cut generic phrases; (4) check - facts, names, numbers, tone; (5) disclose.","Common uses: improve a cover letter, shorten a long message, change tone (formal for a bank, friendly for customers), turn notes into a report, fix grammar, translate.","Your voice: AI writing often sounds generic ('In today's fast-paced world...'). Show how to remove filler and add personal detail and local examples.","Truthfulness: AI must never add skills, experience, qualifications or achievements you do not have to a CV or application. Employers check, and false claims can cost the job.","Translation: AI translation into Kiswahili is useful but can be unnatural or wrong; ask a fluent speaker to check important messages.","Integrity: in college and at work, follow the rules on AI use; when allowed, disclose."]},{"title":"Write with AI in your own voice","steps":["Learners choose a real piece: a cover letter paragraph, a message to a client, a post for a community group, or a section of their project.","They follow the workflow, keeping the AI draft and their edited version side by side and highlighting what they changed.","Partners read the final version and say whether it sounds like the writer."]},{"title":"AI images and creative work","steps":["Explain image prompts: subject (a young woman selling fresh fruit), setting (a busy market in Kisumu in the morning), style (realistic photo, or bright flat illustration), lighting (soft morning light), composition (close-up, eye level, space on the right for text).","Demonstrate in an available free image tool (for example the image features in Canva, Copilot, Gemini or Meta AI, depending on what works in Kenya at the time). Show two variations and an edit.","Show common problems: distorted hands, unreadable text, wrong local details (wrong currency, unrealistic markets), and stereotyped depictions of Africans or women. Discuss how to correct them with better prompts or by using real photos instead.","Responsible rules: never create images of real people without their consent; never create fake images meant to deceive (fake events, fake products, fake evidence); label AI images ('AI-generated image'); for real products and businesses, prefer real photos because customers expect accuracy. Copyright rules for AI images are still developing in many countries, so do not claim exclusive ownership without checking the tool's terms."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Create an AI-assisted image","steps":["Learners write an image prompt for their project (an illustration for a poster, a concept image for a presentation) using the five parts.","They improve the prompt twice and keep the best result, noting problems they fixed.","They label the image and write one sentence on why AI was (or was not) the right choice for it."]},{"title":"AI for presentations - demonstration and build","steps":["Demonstrate: prompt 'Act as a presentation coach. Create a five-slide outline for a 3-minute talk to a youth group in Machakos about using AI safely for job searching. One key idea per slide, with a title and three short points and speaker notes.' Review and edit the outline.","Show how to build slides quickly in Google Slides, PowerPoint or Canva (some have AI design features), using big text, one idea per slide and real or labelled images.","Learners create a five-slide presentation for their final project idea or a topic of their choice, with speaker notes.","They check every fact and number on the slides and add a disclosure line on the last slide."]},{"title":"Gallery review and knowledge check","steps":["Learners post their image and first slide in the cohort space; each learner comments on two others (one strength, one fix).","Ask two assessment questions aloud."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners to save all outputs in their project folder.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Human-first-and-last writing","steps":["Write your key points, prompt for a draft, then visibly edit it into your own voice and check facts."]},{"title":"Image prompt anatomy and fixes","steps":["Build an image prompt part by part, generate variations, fix a problem with a better prompt."]},{"title":"AI-assisted presentation","steps":["Generate an outline, edit it, build two slides live and add speaker notes."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What are the five steps of the human-first-and-last workflow?","guide":"Plan, prompt, edit, check, disclose."},{"question":"Why must you never let AI add experience to your CV?","guide":"It is dishonest, employers verify, it can cost the job and damage reputation."},{"question":"Name the five parts of a good image prompt.","guide":"Subject, setting, style, lighting, composition."},{"question":"When should a business use a real photo instead of an AI image?","guide":"When showing real products, people or places customers expect to be accurate."},{"question":"What should the last slide of an AI-assisted presentation include?","guide":"A disclosure line and, where facts were used, sources."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Check which free image tools currently work in Kenya on the venue internet; prepare backups.","Prepare a generic AI paragraph for the 'remove the robot' exercise."],"delivery":["Time-box the image activity; image tools can absorb a whole session.","Stress that learners' own judgement is what makes the output valuable."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["No images of real people (including classmates) without consent; no sexual, violent or humiliating content.","If a learner creates harmful content, stop the activity, address it privately and follow the programme's conduct and safeguarding processes.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Review the gallery and give feedback on disclosure and checking.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 6,
   $cv13${"title":"AI for productivity, business and automation, with privacy, bias and limits","description":"Use AI for everyday tasks and small business activities, understand simple automation, and manage privacy, bias and the limitations of AI.","objectives":["Use AI for at least four productivity tasks (planning, summarising, organising, drafting, spreadsheet help)","Use AI for small business activities (customer replies, product descriptions, simple market research, social posts) with checks","Explain automation (trigger, action, human check) and identify three automation opportunities in a workflow","Explain how personal data must be protected when using AI, with reference to Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019","Identify bias and limitations in AI outputs, including language and local-context gaps","Map a routine workflow and build a reusable prompt set with human checkpoints"],"activities":["Recap and time audit","AI for productivity and everyday tasks","AI-assisted business activities","Introduction to automation","Stretch break","Privacy, bias and limitations","Workflow map and reusable prompt set","Knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Workflow map with H, AI and A steps, data points and human checks; Reusable prompt set (tested); Updated prompt library","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners apply AI to routine work - planning, summarising, organising, customer communication and business tasks - and learn how simple automations save time. They study privacy, bias and the limits of AI in depth, and design a workflow with reusable prompts and clear human checks.","objectives":["Use AI for at least four productivity tasks (planning, summarising, organising, drafting, spreadsheet help)","Use AI for small business activities (customer replies, product descriptions, simple market research, social posts) with checks","Explain automation (trigger, action, human check) and identify three automation opportunities in a workflow","Explain how personal data must be protected when using AI, with reference to Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019","Identify bias and limitations in AI outputs, including language and local-context gaps","Map a routine workflow and build a reusable prompt set with human checkpoints"],"topics":[{"title":"AI for productivity and everyday tasks","subtopics":["Planning a week or a project","Summarising notes and meetings you have permission to record","To-do lists and priorities","Emails and messages","Spreadsheet formulas and simple data analysis","Studying and job searching"]},{"title":"AI-assisted business activities","subtopics":["Customer replies and FAQs","Product descriptions","Simple market and competitor research (verified)","Social media post ideas","Drafting business documents (quotes, invoices, simple plans)","Where AI should not decide (pricing without data, hiring, credit)"]},{"title":"Introduction to automation","subtopics":["Trigger, action and human check","Automation already in everyday tools (auto-replies, filters, scheduled posts, forms to sheets)","No-code automation tools - overview","When automation goes wrong","Keeping a human in the loop"]},{"title":"Privacy, bias and limitations of AI","subtopics":["Personal data and consent","Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 - fair, lawful, limited use","Tool settings and data retention","Bias - where it comes from and examples","Language and local-context gaps (Kiswahili, Sheng, local languages)","Over-reliance and skills","When not to use AI"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and time audit","kind":"introduction","summary":"List the routine tasks that take up your time each week."},{"minutes":15,"title":"AI for productivity and everyday tasks","kind":"lesson","summary":"See practical ways AI saves time on planning, summarising, organising and spreadsheets."},{"minutes":15,"title":"AI-assisted business activities","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how small businesses use AI for customer replies, descriptions, research and content, and where AI should not decide."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Introduction to automation","kind":"lesson","summary":"Understand trigger-action automation and spot safe opportunities in a workflow."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Privacy, bias and limitations","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to protect personal data, recognise bias and know the limits of AI."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Workflow map and reusable prompt set","kind":"exercise","summary":"Map a routine workflow, mark AI, automation and human steps, and build a reusable prompt set."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Answer questions on productivity, automation, privacy and bias."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Bias spotting","instructions":"Ask an image or text tool for 'a picture/description of a successful entrepreneur' and discuss whose image appears and who is missing."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Workflow map","instructions":"Map a routine workflow with H, AI and A labels, personal data points and human checks."},{"title":"Reusable prompt set","instructions":"Write three to five placeholder prompts for the workflow and test at least two."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Privacy review","instructions":"Review a partner's workflow map for privacy risks and missing human checks."}],"examples":["A Thika salon using AI-drafted, owner-approved replies for booking questions and a WhatsApp away message after hours.","A Nairobi youth group using a Google Form and Sheet to register 200 participants for a career fair.","Image tools showing stereotyped pictures of 'a doctor' or 'a farmer in Africa'.","An AI chatbot that promised a free delivery the business does not offer."],"tools":["AI chat assistant, Google Sheets, Google Forms","Screenshots of a no-code automation tool (optional)","Workflow map template and prompt set template","Free tools only: Google Sheets and Forms (free with a Google account); workflow maps on paper; automation shown through screenshots - no paid tool is needed."],"assessment":[{"question":"What are the three parts of an automation?"},{"question":"Name two tasks where AI should not make the final decision."},{"question":"What does the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 require when handling personal data?"},{"question":"Give one example of AI bias and how to reduce it."},{"question":"How do you anonymise data before using AI?"}],"expected_outputs":["Workflow map with H, AI and A steps, data points and human checks","Reusable prompt set (tested)","Updated prompt library"],"homework":["Finalise your project plan and gather everything you need for the build (content, information from your user, prompt library).","Arrange for one real user to test your solution during or right after session 7. If no real user is available, or testing would involve their personal data, test with a classmate using invented sample data."],"cross_track_notes":["Entrepreneurship & Business teaches business processes and decisions; AI Tools shows where AI and automation can support them and where humans must decide.","Digital Marketing uses WhatsApp Business features as marketing tools; AI Tools uses them as examples of simple automation."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and time audit","steps":["Learners list three routine tasks they do every week and estimate the time each takes (for example replying to customer questions, planning a study timetable, writing reports).","Say that today they will decide which parts AI or automation can help with, and which parts need a human."]},{"title":"AI for productivity and everyday tasks","steps":["Planning: 'I have these tasks and deadlines; make a realistic plan for the week with two hours a day.' Then adjust it to your real life.","Summarising and organising: turn messy notes into a structured summary with actions; ask for a to-do list sorted by priority.","Spreadsheets: ask AI to explain or write a formula ('A Google Sheets formula to total column C where column B says Paid'), then test it on sample data.","Job searching: tailor a CV summary to a job advert (truthfully), prepare interview practice questions, draft follow-up emails.","Remind learners - always review, adjust and own the final result."]},{"title":"AI-assisted business activities","steps":["Customer replies: build an FAQ answer set (prices, delivery, opening hours) with AI from the owner's real information; the owner checks every answer before use.","Product descriptions: give AI the real features and ask for short, honest descriptions for WhatsApp catalogue and Jumia-style listings.","Market research: ask AI for questions to ask customers and competitors to look at, then verify with real observation and conversations; AI does not know your local market prices.","Business documents: quotes, invoices, simple plans and letters - check numbers carefully.","Where AI should not decide alone: setting prices without real cost data, hiring decisions, deciding who gets credit, medical or legal advice. AI can support thinking; a responsible person decides."]},{"title":"Introduction to automation","steps":["Define automation: a rule that does a task automatically. Every automation has a trigger (something happens), an action (what is done) and should have a human check where mistakes matter.","Everyday examples: WhatsApp Business greeting and away messages (trigger - message received; action - auto-reply); email filters; scheduled social posts; a Google Form that saves answers to a Google Sheet and sends a confirmation email.","No-code tools such as Zapier, Make or built-in automation in Google and Microsoft tools can connect apps (for example 'new form response, then add a row and notify me'). Explain conceptually; free tiers and features change.","Demonstrate (or show screenshots of) a Google Form connected to a Sheet for event registrations, and explain the human check (someone reviews registrations daily).","When automation goes wrong: auto-replies giving wrong prices, messages sent to the wrong list, AI chatbots promising things the business cannot deliver. Rule - automate the routine, keep a human for exceptions and anything involving money, complaints or personal data."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Privacy, bias and limitations","steps":["Privacy: personal data is any information that identifies a person (name, phone, ID, location, photos, health). Kenya's Data Protection Act 2019 requires personal data to be processed lawfully, fairly and transparently, for clear purposes, and kept secure; people have rights such as being informed and objecting. The Office of the Data Protection Commissioner oversees it.","In practice: do not paste customers' or classmates' personal data into AI tools; remove names and numbers first (anonymise); check tool settings for chat history and training; use business or education versions with stronger protection if your organisation provides them.","Bias: AI learns from data that reflects existing inequalities. Examples - image tools showing doctors as men and nurses as women; CV-screening tools that disadvantage certain names or schools; poorer performance in Kiswahili, Sheng and Kenyan local languages than in English. Ask AI for diverse examples and check outputs for stereotypes.","Limitations: out-of-date knowledge, invented facts, no real understanding of local context, inconsistent answers, and over-reliance (if AI does all your thinking, your skills weaken).","When not to use AI: confidential matters, emergencies, decisions that seriously affect someone's life, tasks your school or employer forbids, and anything you cannot check."]},{"title":"Workflow map and reusable prompt set","steps":["Learners choose one routine workflow (their own, or one from their project user - for example a salon's booking enquiries, a youth group's event registration, a student's weekly study routine).","They map each step and label it: H (human only), AI (AI-assisted with human check), A (automated with human check). They mark where personal data appears and how it is protected.","They write a reusable prompt set - three to five prompts with placeholders (for example [customer question], [price list]) - and test at least two.","Partners review the map for privacy risks and missing human checks."]},{"title":"Knowledge check","steps":["Run the assessment questions and discuss answers."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners that session 7 is the project build - bring the project plan, prompt library and any materials.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"AI spreadsheet formula","steps":["Ask AI for a formula, paste it into a sheet with sample data and test it, fixing any error."]},{"title":"Form-to-sheet automation","steps":["Show a Google Form linked to a Sheet with an email notification, and the human review step."]},{"title":"Anonymising data","steps":["Take a sample customer message list and remove names and numbers before using AI to categorise the questions."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What are the three parts of an automation?","guide":"Trigger, action, human check."},{"question":"Name two tasks where AI should not make the final decision.","guide":"Hiring, credit, pricing without data, medical, legal, safeguarding decisions."},{"question":"What does the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 require when handling personal data?","guide":"Lawful, fair and transparent processing for a clear purpose, limited to what is needed, kept secure; people have rights over their data."},{"question":"Give one example of AI bias and how to reduce it.","guide":"Stereotyped images or unfair screening; ask for diversity, check outputs, use human review, use better data."},{"question":"How do you anonymise data before using AI?","guide":"Remove or replace names, numbers, IDs, addresses and any detail that identifies a person."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare a sample spreadsheet, a sample anonymised customer message list and a working Google Form and Sheet.","Read the summary of the Kenya Data Protection Act 2019 from the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner so you can answer basic questions; do not give legal advice."],"delivery":["Keep automation conceptual and safe - learners should not connect real customer accounts or business data in class.","Encourage learners to choose workflows from their real lives or project users."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners must not use real personal data in exercises; use anonymised or invented data.","Bias discussions can touch on identity; keep them respectful and evidence-based.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Review workflow maps for privacy issues before learners use them in their projects.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 7,
   $cv13${"title":"AI project build and testing","description":"Build an AI-assisted solution for a real brief, evaluate it with the AI quality checklist, test it with a real user and prepare a demonstration.","objectives":["Define the problem, user, AI role and success test for the project","Build a working AI-assisted solution using a documented prompt set and tools","Evaluate the solution with the AI quality checklist and fix problems","Test the solution with a real user and record their feedback","Prepare a three-minute demonstration with an AI-use disclosure"],"activities":["Project check-in","Designing your solution","Build","Stretch break","Evaluate and user-test","Revise and prepare the demonstration","Showcase briefing and homework"],"expected_output":"Completed project design canvas; Working AI-assisted solution with documented prompt set and user instructions; AI quality checklist and user test record; Draft three-minute demonstration","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 3 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners combine everything from the track into one practical AI-assisted solution for a real person, business or group. They build it, evaluate it for accuracy, usefulness, safety, privacy and bias, test it with a real user, revise it and prepare to demonstrate it in the showcase.","objectives":["Define the problem, user, AI role and success test for the project","Build a working AI-assisted solution using a documented prompt set and tools","Evaluate the solution with the AI quality checklist and fix problems","Test the solution with a real user and record their feedback","Prepare a three-minute demonstration with an AI-use disclosure"],"topics":[{"title":"Designing an AI-assisted solution","subtopics":["Problem and user","What AI does and what humans do","Tools and prompts","Success test - how you will know it works"]},{"title":"Building and documenting","subtopics":["Prompt set with placeholders","Example inputs and outputs","Instructions for the user"]},{"title":"Testing and evaluating AI outputs","subtopics":["AI quality checklist - accuracy, usefulness, safety, privacy, bias, disclosure","User testing","Revising based on evidence"]},{"title":"Preparing a demonstration","subtopics":["Demo structure","Showing before and after","Explaining responsible choices"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Project check-in","kind":"introduction","summary":"Confirm your project brief and success test."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Designing your solution","kind":"lesson","summary":"Plan the solution using the project design canvas."},{"minutes":40,"title":"Build","kind":"exercise","summary":"Build your AI-assisted solution and document the prompts and outputs."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Evaluate and user-test","kind":"assessment","summary":"Score your solution with the AI quality checklist and test it with a real user or a peer."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Revise and prepare the demonstration","kind":"exercise","summary":"Fix the main problems and prepare a three-minute demo."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase briefing and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Learn how the showcase works and what to submit."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Project design canvas","instructions":"Complete the canvas - user, problem, AI role, human role, tools, prompts, data protection, risks and checks, success test."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Build the solution","instructions":"Build and document the solution - prompt set, example inputs and outputs, user instructions."},{"title":"Quality checklist","instructions":"Score the solution on each checklist item and fix anything scoring 1."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Demo run-through","instructions":"Run a 1-minute version of your demo for a partner and get one improvement."}],"examples":["A customer reply kit for a Kitengela hardware shop.","A KCSE revision pack with AI-generated quizzes checked against the syllabus by a teacher.","An AI-assisted information pack for a Kisumu community health volunteer group, checked by a health worker before use.","A registration and reminder workflow for a youth sports tournament in Nyeri."],"tools":["AI tools from the track, Google Forms and Sheets, WhatsApp Business (as needed)","Project design canvas, AI quality checklist and demo template","User feedback form","No paid subscription needed: a solution can be a prompt set with instructions in a free document, a Google Form, or a WhatsApp message flow; build in pairs on a shared device if needed."],"assessment":[{"question":"AI quality checklist completed for the solution."},{"question":"What is your success test, and did the solution pass?"},{"question":"Where is the human check in your solution, and why there?"}],"expected_outputs":["Completed project design canvas","Working AI-assisted solution with documented prompt set and user instructions","AI quality checklist and user test record","Draft three-minute demonstration"],"homework":["Complete the real user test and record the feedback.","Finish your demonstration and practise it twice within three minutes.","Upload your solution files, prompt set, checklist and user feedback to DIRA LEARN.","If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute solution presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."],"cross_track_notes":["All tracks build their final project in session 7. The AI Tools project is judged on usefulness, responsible AI use and evaluation, not on design polish."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Project check-in","steps":["Learners state their brief in one sentence: 'I am building [solution] for [user] to [solve problem], and it works if [success test].'","Project types to choose from: a study resource pack (explanations, quizzes, summaries) for a group of learners; a customer-service prompt kit and FAQ for a small business; an AI-assisted information or marketing pack for a community group; a simple automated workflow (form, sheet, AI-drafted replies with human review)."]},{"title":"Designing your solution","steps":["Explain the project design canvas: user and problem; what AI does; what the human does; tools; prompts; data used and how it is protected; risks and checks; success test.","Walk through a worked example - 'Customer reply kit for a Kitengela hardware shop': AI drafts answers from the owner's price list and policies; the owner approves answers; quick replies are saved in WhatsApp Business; success test - the owner answers 10 common questions in under 10 minutes with no wrong information.","Learners complete their canvas in 8 minutes; the facilitator checks the success tests are specific."]},{"title":"Build","steps":["Learners build the solution using their tools and prompt library. They document the final prompt set, example inputs and outputs, and short instructions for the user.","Checkpoint at 20 minutes: each learner shows the facilitator one working output and one check they have done.","The facilitator circulates, unblocking technical problems and asking 'how do you know this is correct?' and 'where is the human check?'.","Learners who finish early add a second feature or improve the user instructions."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Evaluate and user-test","steps":["Learners score their solution against the AI quality checklist (1 to 3 each): accuracy, usefulness, clarity, safety and appropriateness, privacy, bias and fairness, disclosure. Anything scoring 1 must be fixed.","Learners test the solution with a real user (by phone, in person or via WhatsApp) or, if not possible during the session, with a peer playing the user, and record what worked, what confused them and what they would change. Real user testing must be completed before session 8."]},{"title":"Revise and prepare the demonstration","steps":["Learners fix the top two problems from the checklist and test.","They prepare the demo: self-pitch introduction (15 seconds), the problem and user, a live or recorded demonstration of the solution, before and after (time saved or quality improved), checks and responsible choices, user feedback, what next. Ends with the disclosure line.","Partners watch a 1-minute run-through and give one improvement."]},{"title":"Showcase briefing and homework","steps":["Explain the showcase format (3 minutes demo, 2 minutes questions), the rubric and the submission (solution files, prompt set, checklist, user feedback, disclosure).","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Worked project example","steps":["Walk through the completed design canvas for the hardware shop reply kit and show two outputs with the owner's corrections."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"AI quality checklist completed for the solution.","guide":"Each criterion scored; items scored 1 fixed or explained."},{"question":"What is your success test, and did the solution pass?","guide":"A specific, observable test with an honest result."},{"question":"Where is the human check in your solution, and why there?","guide":"At points involving facts, money, personal data or decisions affecting people."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Review project plans before the session and prepare to unblock common technical issues.","Prepare the design canvas, checklist and demo template.","Set the session 8 room plan from the track group size (up to 12 - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus a recorded-questions room) and line up co-facilitators and peer panels."],"delivery":["Protect the 45-minute build time; keep instructions short.","Push for real users and real problems; a polished solution nobody needs scores lower than a simple useful one."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Projects must not use real personal data without consent; anonymise or use invented data.","Projects giving health, legal or financial information must be checked by a qualified person before anyone relies on them, and must say so.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Check every learner has arranged a real user test before session 8.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('ai', 8,
   $cv13${"title":"AI tools showcase","description":"Demonstrate your AI-assisted solution, explain your responsible choices, give and receive feedback, pitch yourself and plan your next 30 days.","objectives":["Demonstrate an AI-assisted solution clearly in three minutes","Explain the human checks, privacy protections and disclosure in the solution","Report user feedback and evaluation results honestly","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves and the project as portfolio evidence","Write a 30-day plan for using AI skills responsibly"],"activities":["Showcase set-up and expectations","Demonstrations and questions - block 1","Stretch break","Demonstrations and questions - block 2","Feedback round and responsible AI reflection","Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","30-day action plan","Celebration and close"],"expected_output":"Demonstrated AI-assisted solution (final project) with rubric score and feedback; One-page AI project case study with disclosure; 30-day action plan; Written feedback given to two peers","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 3 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners demonstrate practical AI capability by showing a working AI-assisted solution, explaining how they checked it and used AI responsibly, and answering questions. They learn to present the project as portfolio evidence, practise professional behaviour and leave with a 30-day plan for using AI skills in study, work or business.","objectives":["Demonstrate an AI-assisted solution clearly in three minutes","Explain the human checks, privacy protections and disclosure in the solution","Report user feedback and evaluation results honestly","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves and the project as portfolio evidence","Write a 30-day plan for using AI skills responsibly"],"topics":[{"title":"Demonstrating an AI-assisted solution","subtopics":["Demo structure","Live versus recorded demos","Answering questions about accuracy and responsibility"]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and your work","subtopics":["AI skills employers value (prompting, verification, workflow design)","Turning the project into a case study","Talking honestly about AI use"]},{"title":"Professional behaviour and workplace readiness","subtopics":["Showcase etiquette","Receiving critical feedback","Following up with your user","Pathways - jobs, freelancing, using AI in your own business"]},{"title":"Next steps","subtopics":["30-day plan","Keeping up with AI changes safely"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","kind":"introduction","summary":"Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Demonstrations and questions - block 1","kind":"assessment","summary":"The first six presenters in each room demonstrate and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Demonstrations and questions - block 2","kind":"assessment","summary":"The next six presenters in each room demonstrate; recorded presenters answer their live questions."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Feedback round and responsible AI reflection","kind":"group","summary":"Share feedback and reflect on what responsible AI use looked like in practice."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","kind":"lesson","summary":"Turn your project into a portfolio case study and practise pitching your AI skills."},{"minutes":15,"title":"30-day action plan","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write your 30-day plan for using AI skills."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Celebration and close","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Celebrate the AI solutions you built and learn what happens after the programme."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"AI project case study","instructions":"Write a one-page case study - problem, user, solution, checks, results, learning, disclosure."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Demonstration","instructions":"Demonstrate your solution in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."},{"title":"30-day plan","instructions":"Complete the plan with goal, weekly actions, measure, accountability partner and risk."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer feedback","instructions":"Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."},{"title":"Pitch practice","instructions":"Practise your self-pitch with your project as proof."}],"examples":["A learner demonstrating a revision quiz pack used by 15 Form Four students in Murang'a.","A learner showing how a youth tournament registration workflow saved organisers hours of manual entry.","A learner using the project as proof in an application for an administrative assistant role."],"tools":["Learners' solutions (live or recorded), showcase rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN","Timer, 30-day plan template and case study template","Low-data showcase: a recorded demo can be a screen recording or a voice note with screenshots; play recordings from downloaded files, not streams."],"assessment":[{"question":"Demonstration scored with the AI showcase rubric."},{"question":"Show one AI output that was wrong and explain how you caught and fixed it."},{"question":"Deliver your pitch with your project as proof."}],"expected_outputs":["Demonstrated AI-assisted solution (final project) with rubric score and feedback","One-page AI project case study with disclosure","30-day action plan","Written feedback given to two peers"],"homework":["Submit the final solution, case study and 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.","Thank your user and share what you changed based on their feedback."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the AI rubric weights evaluation and responsible use."],"track_project":"An AI-assisted solution or demonstration for a real brief (a study resource pack, a customer-service prompt kit for a small business, an AI-assisted marketing or information pack for a community group, or a simple automated workflow). It includes the problem and user, the AI tools and prompts used, the human checks, an evaluation against the AI quality checklist, a user test and a live demonstration in session 8 with an AI-use disclosure. Privacy option: the solution can be tested with a classmate or with invented sample data instead of a real user. No personal or business information goes into an AI tool without permission, and no AI image shows an identifiable real person without their consent."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","steps":["Welcome learners and guests (users, DIRA staff, mentors, employers). Thank users who tested solutions.","Share the rubric: problem and user (15%), solution works and is useful (25%), evaluation and human checks (25%), privacy, fairness and disclosure (20%), presentation and answers (15%).","Etiquette and peer-feedback assignments: each learner gives written feedback to two presenters."]},{"title":"Demonstrations and questions - block 1","steps":["Run the demonstrations with the session 8 room plan in the facilitator instructions: the first six demonstrators in each room go in this block, each with 3 minutes plus 2 minutes of questions.","Learners demonstrate in order, 3 minutes each plus 2 minutes of questions. Recorded demos are allowed if the internet fails.","Ask at least one question per presenter, such as: 'Show me an output that was wrong and how you caught it', 'What personal data does this touch and how is it protected?', 'What would happen if the AI tool changed or became paid?', 'Would your user keep using it? Why?'","Score with the rubric and note one strength and one improvement."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Demonstrations and questions - block 2","steps":["The remaining demonstrators in each live room (7 to 12) go now, with the same timer, rubric and question approach as block 1.","In a recorded-questions room, do not replay the recordings (the panel scored them before the session); give each recorded presenter 2 minutes of live questions about their AI solution.","Each room's panel hands its completed AI solution rubric sheets to the room facilitator before the feedback round."]},{"title":"Feedback round and responsible AI reflection","steps":["Learners give their written feedback to their two assigned presenters.","Reflection: learners revisit the group AI use code from session 3. Which rules were easy to follow, which were hard, and would they change any?","Facilitator summarises patterns in strong projects (real user, clear human checks, honest evaluation)."]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","steps":["Case study format: problem, user, what I built, how I checked it, results and feedback, what I learned. Add screenshots and the disclosure.","Pitch formula with proof: 'I am Faith from Kericho. I use AI tools responsibly to save time on routine work. I built a reply kit for a hardware shop that cut answering time from 30 to 10 minutes with zero wrong prices. I am looking for an administrative or digital assistant role.'","Employers value people who can use AI and check it - prompting, verification, workflow design and honesty about AI use. Never claim AI work as entirely your own; explain your role.","Pathways: office and administrative roles, customer service, digital assistants, freelance support work (Ajira Digital provides information on online work), and using AI in your own business. Avoid scams promising easy money from 'AI jobs' for a fee.","Learners practise their pitch once in pairs."]},{"title":"30-day action plan","steps":["Learners complete the 30-day plan: main goal, four weekly actions (for example week 1 finish the case study, week 2 offer the solution to two more users, week 3 learn one new AI tool safely, week 4 apply for two roles), a measure of success, an accountability partner and one risk with a plan.","Learners share their goal with their accountability partner."]},{"title":"Celebration and close","steps":["Celebrate each learner's solution in one sentence.","Explain how to submit the final AI solution and its verification note in DIRA LEARN, how results and certificates follow the programme's rules, and how alumni can keep sharing useful prompts and tools."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Model demonstration","steps":["Before the session, share a recorded 3-minute example demo (the hardware shop reply kit) as a reference. Do not use showcase time for it."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Demonstration scored with the AI showcase rubric.","guide":"Use rubric descriptors; honest evaluation and responsible choices score highly even if the solution is simple."},{"question":"Show one AI output that was wrong and explain how you caught and fixed it.","guide":"A concrete example with the checking method."},{"question":"Deliver your pitch with your project as proof.","guide":"Who, skill, proof with a number or result, ask; honest about AI's role."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Publish the demo order and test screen sharing; ask learners to have a recorded backup.","Invite users and guests; brief guests on respectful questions and safeguarding expectations.","Confirm the room plan: count the learners in the track group, book the rooms and co-facilitators, brief the peer panels, and publish each room's presenter order and the list of recorded presenters by the end of session 7."],"delivery":["Keep strict time; prioritise questions about checking and responsibility.","Session 8 room plan - Room plan (set in session 7 from the track group size; the session is never extended): up to 12 learners - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms in parallel; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus one recorded-questions room. Each live room takes at most 12 presenters: 6 in each 30-minute block, 3 minutes to present and 2 minutes of questions each.","Every room has a facilitator or briefed co-facilitator (another trainer, a mentor or DIRA staff member who knows the rubric and the safeguarding expectations) and a peer panel of two learners who present in the other block. All rooms use the same rubric.","Recorded option: learners in a recorded-questions room, and anyone who cannot present live (absence, connection problems, anxiety, or the privacy option), submit a 3-minute recording - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - by the day before session 8. The panel scores it in advance with the same rubric; in session 8 each recorded presenter answers 2 minutes of live questions (12 per block). If more learners need the recorded option than one room can take, the panel sends written questions and the learner answers them in DIRA LEARN.","Timekeeping: a visible timer with a 30-second warning; stop at 3 minutes. If a block runs late, cut questions to 1 minute - do not extend the session."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners unable to present live can submit a recorded demo according to the programme's rules.","Guests must not contact learners privately outside programme channels.","Shared devices and low data: pair or group learners on one device and rotate the typing role each task; learners without a device or data follow the shared screen and record their work on paper or later on programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid tool or plan, or an account that needs personal data the learner does not want to give."],"after_session":["Enter rubric scores and feedback in DIRA LEARN grading.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 3,
   $cv13${"title":"Content strategy, storytelling and platform formats","description":"Plan content with a clear purpose, audience and niche, tell stories with a hook, a middle and an ending, and choose the right formats for each platform.","objectives":["Write a content strategy one-pager (purpose, audience, niche, goals, pillars, platforms, posting rhythm)","Explain the difference between creator content and brand content","Apply a story structure (hook, build, payoff, call to action) and the 'show, don't tell' principle","Compare content formats and specifications for TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, WhatsApp status, LinkedIn and podcasts","Choose a real brief for the creative project","Storyboard a 30 to 60-second piece"],"activities":["Welcome to Content Creation and track overview","Content strategy and planning","Storytelling","Content formats for different platforms","Stretch break","Demonstration - from idea to storyboard","Strategy one-pager, brief and storyboard","Peer pitch and knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Content strategy one-pager; Creative project brief; Storyboard for a 30 to 60-second piece","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners start the track as planners, not just posters. They define the purpose, audience and niche for their content, learn storytelling structures that hold attention, understand formats and specifications for each platform, and storyboard the first piece of their creative project.","objectives":["Write a content strategy one-pager (purpose, audience, niche, goals, pillars, platforms, posting rhythm)","Explain the difference between creator content and brand content","Apply a story structure (hook, build, payoff, call to action) and the 'show, don't tell' principle","Compare content formats and specifications for TikTok, Instagram, YouTube, Facebook, WhatsApp status, LinkedIn and podcasts","Choose a real brief for the creative project","Storyboard a 30 to 60-second piece"],"topics":[{"title":"Content strategy and planning","subtopics":["Purpose - why you create","Audience - who it is for","Niche - what you are known for","Goals and measures","Content pillars","Consistency and a realistic rhythm","Creator content versus brand content"]},{"title":"Storytelling","subtopics":["The hook in the first 2 seconds","Story arc - setup, tension, payoff","Character, conflict and change","Show, don't tell","Emotion and authenticity","Local stories and languages"]},{"title":"Content formats for different platforms","subtopics":["Short vertical video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) 9:16","Feed photos and carousels (1:1 and 4:5)","Stories and WhatsApp status","Long-form video (YouTube 16:9)","Audio and podcasts","Text-led platforms (LinkedIn, X)"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Welcome to Content Creation and track overview","kind":"introduction","summary":"Meet your track group and see the six sessions and the final portfolio."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Content strategy and planning","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to define purpose, audience, niche, pillars and rhythm for content."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Storytelling","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn story structures that hold attention, with local examples."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Content formats for different platforms","kind":"lesson","summary":"Match formats and specifications to platforms and audiences."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Demonstration - from idea to storyboard","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Watch a story idea turned into a six-frame storyboard."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Strategy one-pager, brief and storyboard","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write your content strategy one-pager, choose your project brief and storyboard your first piece."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Peer pitch and knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Pitch your storyboard in 30 seconds and answer quick questions."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Story anatomy","instructions":"Watch two short videos and identify the hook, tension, payoff and call to action."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Content strategy one-pager","instructions":"Write your purpose, audience, niche, goals, pillars, platforms and posting rhythm."},{"title":"Storyboard","instructions":"Storyboard a 30 to 60-second piece for your brief with six to eight frames."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Storyboard pitch","instructions":"Pitch your storyboard in 30 seconds; peers judge the hook and suggest one improvement."}],"examples":["A Kisumu creator reviewing affordable fish and ugali spots.","A mama mboga's 4 am start at Marikiti market told as a 40-second story.","A Nairobi tailor's 'fabric to fitting' transformation reel.","A community clean-up in Nakuru told as a before-and-after carousel."],"tools":["Phones, paper storyboard template, strategy one-pager template","Example videos (public, from Kenyan creators or prepared by the facilitator)","Free and low-data planning: paper storyboards and strategy templates need no device or data; example videos downloaded in advance on programme Wi-Fi and shown on one screen."],"assessment":[{"question":"What is the difference between creator content and brand content?"},{"question":"Write a hook for a video about cheap lunch spots in your town."},{"question":"What does 'show, don't tell' mean?"},{"question":"What is the aspect ratio and size for a TikTok or Reel?"},{"question":"Name your niche and two content pillars."}],"expected_outputs":["Content strategy one-pager","Creative project brief","Storyboard for a 30 to 60-second piece"],"homework":["Turn your storyboard into a shot list (every shot you need to film).","Visit or contact your brief (business or group) to plan filming and get consent. If you cannot get consent, or prefer not to work with a real brief, use the simulated brief from the facilitator and film objects, places or yourself.","Watch five videos in your niche and note the hooks used."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing plans content to reach a business goal through a campaign. Content Creation focuses on the craft - storytelling, production and the creator's portfolio - which can serve marketing but also education, causes and personal creator brands."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Welcome to Content Creation and track overview","steps":["Show the journey: S3 strategy, storytelling and formats, S4 photography, lighting, composition, mobile video and sound capture, S5 video editing, audio and captions, S6 design, branding, visual storytelling and copyright, S7 portfolio and project build, S8 showcase.","Show an example portfolio (fictional creator 'Otieno Shoots', a Kisumu creator making short videos for local food spots). Say that by session 8 each learner will have their own.","Ask learners to name a Kenyan creator they follow and why they keep watching."]},{"title":"Content strategy and planning","steps":["Purpose: why are you creating - to build a personal creator brand, to promote a business, to educate a community, or to tell stories for a cause? Purpose decides everything else.","Audience: who exactly? 'Students in Eldoret who want to eat well on a budget' is stronger than 'young people'.","Niche: what you want to be known for. A niche can be a topic (budget food), a style (funny skits in Sheng), or a place (Kisumu). Broad creators struggle to grow; focused creators get remembered.","Goals: followers are not the only goal. Others include enquiries for a business, sign-ups for an event, saves and shares (people found it useful), or paid briefs.","Pillars and rhythm: three or four pillars (for example 'cheap eats reviews', 'cook with me', 'campus food news'). A rhythm you can keep for three months (for example three short videos a week) beats a burst followed by silence.","Creator content versus brand content: creators speak as themselves; brand content speaks for a business and follows its brand. Many creators earn by making brand content for clients - the portfolio will show both."]},{"title":"Storytelling","steps":["The hook: the first 2 seconds decide whether people keep watching. Hooks - a surprising visual, a question ('Can you eat lunch for KES 100 in town?'), a bold statement, a result first ('This KES 500 room makeover...').","Story arc: setup (who and where), tension (a problem, a question or a challenge), payoff (the answer, the result, the emotion), call to action (follow, comment, visit).","Character, conflict and change: people connect with people. A mama mboga starting her day at 4 am at Marikiti market and selling out by noon is a story; 'we sell vegetables' is not.","Show, don't tell: instead of saying 'our tailor is skilled', show hands cutting fabric, a fitting and the client's smile.","Authenticity and language: speak like your audience - English, Kiswahili, Sheng or your local language - as long as your target audience understands. Add captions so more people can follow.","Play or describe two short examples and ask learners to identify the hook, tension and payoff."]},{"title":"Content formats for different platforms","steps":["Short vertical video (TikTok, Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, Facebook Reels): 9:16 (1080 x 1920), usually 7 to 60 seconds for discovery content, strong hook, captions, trending or original audio.","Feed photos and carousels (Instagram, Facebook, LinkedIn): 1:1 (1080 x 1080) or 4:5 (1080 x 1350); carousels tell step-by-step or before-and-after stories.","Stories and WhatsApp status: vertical, short-lived, informal - good for behind the scenes and daily updates.","Long-form video (YouTube): 16:9 (1920 x 1080), for tutorials, vlogs and interviews; thumbnail and title matter.","Audio and podcasts: interviews and conversations; good sound is essential.","Text-led platforms (LinkedIn, X): opinions, lessons and professional updates, often with an image or video.","Platform specifications and limits change; check each platform's current help pages before publishing."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Demonstration - from idea to storyboard","steps":["Take an idea: 'A day with a boda boda rider who delivers lunch in Thika'. Write the hook, tension and payoff.","Draw a six-frame storyboard: frame number, shot type (wide, medium, close-up), what we see, what we hear (voice, sound, music), on-screen text, duration. Total 30 to 45 seconds.","Show how the storyboard becomes a shot list for filming in session 4."]},{"title":"Strategy one-pager, brief and storyboard","steps":["Learners choose the creative project brief: (a) a local business (with the owner's agreement), (b) a cause or community group, or (c) their own creator brand.","Learners complete the strategy one-pager - purpose, audience, niche, goals, three or four pillars, main platforms, posting rhythm.","Learners storyboard one 30 to 60-second piece for the brief (six to eight frames) with a clear hook and payoff.","Learners without a device can draw the storyboard on paper and photograph it later."]},{"title":"Peer pitch and knowledge check","steps":["In groups of three, each learner pitches the storyboard in 30 seconds. Peers say whether the hook would stop them scrolling and suggest one improvement.","Ask two or three assessment questions aloud."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners to bring a charged phone with free storage to session 4 (shooting session).","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Idea to storyboard","steps":["Turn an idea into a hook, tension and payoff, then draw a six-frame storyboard with shot types, audio and text."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What is the difference between creator content and brand content?","guide":"Creator content speaks as the creator; brand content speaks for a business and follows its brand."},{"question":"Write a hook for a video about cheap lunch spots in your town.","guide":"Grabs attention within 2 seconds - a question, bold claim, surprising visual or result first."},{"question":"What does 'show, don't tell' mean?","guide":"Use visuals and actions to communicate instead of stating claims."},{"question":"What is the aspect ratio and size for a TikTok or Reel?","guide":"9:16, 1080 x 1920."},{"question":"Name your niche and two content pillars.","guide":"Specific niche and pillars linked to the audience."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare two short example videos (public or your own) that show clear hooks and payoffs.","Prepare the strategy one-pager and storyboard templates."],"delivery":["Keep learners creating; theory segments should feel like inspiration, not lectures.","Push for specific audiences and niches."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners working with a business or group must get the owner's or leader's agreement.","Content involving children needs a parent's or guardian's consent; discourage filming minors at all for this project.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Review storyboards and give one improvement each in DIRA LEARN.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 4,
   $cv13${"title":"Smartphone photography, lighting, composition and mobile video","description":"Take clear, well-lit, well-composed photos and short videos on a phone, and capture clean sound.","objectives":["Apply composition techniques (rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, angles, clean backgrounds, symmetry)","Use phone camera features (gridlines, focus and exposure lock, portrait mode, HDR) and avoid digital zoom","Use natural light well (window light, golden hour, avoiding harsh midday sun, simple reflectors)","Film stable shots in the right orientation using wide, medium and close-up shots and B-roll","Capture clean audio (quiet location, mic close to the speaker, avoiding wind and echo)","Shoot a five-photo set and the clips for their storyboard, with consent"],"activities":["Recap and hooks you found","Composition and phone camera basics","Lighting","Mobile video production","Stretch break","Capturing clean sound","Practical shoot","Peer review of shots","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Five-photo set for the brief; Storyboard clips including B-roll and a clean audio clip (complete or in progress); Peer-reviewed shot checklist with a reshoot plan","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners master the phone as a camera. They learn composition rules, how to use natural light, camera settings that matter, shot types for video, stable filming and clean sound capture, then shoot a photo set and the clips for their storyboard.","objectives":["Apply composition techniques (rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, angles, clean backgrounds, symmetry)","Use phone camera features (gridlines, focus and exposure lock, portrait mode, HDR) and avoid digital zoom","Use natural light well (window light, golden hour, avoiding harsh midday sun, simple reflectors)","Film stable shots in the right orientation using wide, medium and close-up shots and B-roll","Capture clean audio (quiet location, mic close to the speaker, avoiding wind and echo)","Shoot a five-photo set and the clips for their storyboard, with consent"],"topics":[{"title":"Photography using smartphones","subtopics":["Clean the lens","Gridlines and focus/exposure lock","Portrait mode and HDR","Avoid digital zoom - move closer","Taking many shots and choosing the best"]},{"title":"Lighting and composition","subtopics":["Rule of thirds","Leading lines and framing","Angles - eye level, high, low","Clean backgrounds","Natural and window light","Golden hour and harsh midday light","DIY reflectors and diffusers"]},{"title":"Mobile video production","subtopics":["Vertical versus horizontal","Shot types - wide, medium, close-up","B-roll","Stable shots (two hands, lean on something, cheap tripod)","Hold each shot for 5 seconds","Simple movement - pan, push in, follow"]},{"title":"Audio and sound (capture)","subtopics":["Why audio matters more than picture","Quiet locations","Mic distance","Wired earphone and lapel mics","Wind, echo and background noise","Recording a test clip"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and hooks you found","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share the hooks you noticed and check your shot list."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Composition and phone camera basics","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn composition techniques and the camera settings that make the biggest difference."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Lighting","kind":"lesson","summary":"Use natural light to make photos and videos look professional."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Mobile video production","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn shot types, B-roll, stable filming and simple camera movement."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Capturing clean sound","kind":"lesson","summary":"Record clear voice and avoid the most common sound problems."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Practical shoot","kind":"exercise","summary":"Shoot a five-photo set and the clips for your storyboard."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Peer review of shots","kind":"assessment","summary":"Review each other's photos and clips with the shot checklist."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Composition hunt","instructions":"Take one photo for each technique - rule of thirds, leading lines, framing, low angle, top-down."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Five-photo set","instructions":"Shoot wide, medium, close-up, angle and portrait photos for your brief using good light and composition."},{"title":"Storyboard clips","instructions":"Film the clips on your shot list, including three B-roll shots and one clean audio clip."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Shoot pairs","instructions":"Work in pairs to shoot, hold reflectors and check each other's framing and sound."},{"title":"Shot checklist review","instructions":"Review your partner's photos and clip with the checklist and choose one shot to reshoot."}],"examples":["Food photos at a Kisumu fish spot using window light and a top-down angle.","A Nairobi tailor's hands as a close-up B-roll.","A Marikiti market wide shot using leading lines of the stalls.","Interviewing a youth group leader in a quiet church hall instead of beside a busy road."],"tools":["Smartphones with free storage, earphones with a mic, optional cheap tripod or lapel mic","White manila or foil reflector, a plain cloth background","Shot checklist","Low-cost kit: any smartphone camera; a stack of books or a cup as a tripod; white manila or foil as a reflector; a small room with curtains or clothes for clearer sound; learners without a camera phone shoot in pairs or threes."],"assessment":[{"question":"Explain the rule of thirds."},{"question":"Your subject is standing with a bright window behind them and their face is dark. What do you do?"},{"question":"Name the three basic shot types and what each shows."},{"question":"Why should you avoid digital zoom?"},{"question":"Name two ways to improve sound when filming on a phone."}],"expected_outputs":["Five-photo set for the brief","Storyboard clips including B-roll and a clean audio clip (complete or in progress)","Peer-reviewed shot checklist with a reshoot plan"],"homework":["Complete any missing storyboard clips and reshoot the shot you chose.","Back up your footage and install a free editor (for example CapCut, InShot or VN; check the app's current terms).","Take five more photos using golden-hour light."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing asks learners to take real product photos for campaigns; Content Creation teaches the full photography and video craft behind them."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and hooks you found","steps":["Learners share one hook they noticed in their niche.","Check that each learner has a shot list and consent from their brief."]},{"title":"Composition and phone camera basics","steps":["Demonstrate turning on gridlines (Android and iPhone camera settings). Teach the rule of thirds - place the subject on a line or intersection, not dead centre, unless symmetry is the point.","Leading lines (a road, a counter, a row of market stalls) draw the eye to the subject; framing (a doorway, a window, leaves) adds depth.","Angles: eye level for connection; low angle to make the subject look powerful; high angle (top-down) for food and products.","Clean backgrounds: move the subject or yourself to remove clutter; a plain wall or kanga cloth can make a product pop.","Camera basics: clean the lens with a soft cloth (phones in pockets get greasy); tap to focus and hold to lock focus and exposure; slide to adjust brightness; portrait mode for people and products; avoid digital zoom - move closer instead; take several shots and pick the best.","Show good and bad examples of each technique, using photos of Kenyan markets, food and people (with consent)."]},{"title":"Lighting","steps":["Light is the biggest difference between amateur and professional-looking content. Natural light is free.","Window light: place the subject facing or beside a window, not with the window behind them (that creates a silhouette).","Time of day: early morning and late afternoon (golden hour) give soft, warm light; midday sun in Kenya is harsh and creates strong shadows - move into open shade.","Backlight on purpose: for silhouettes or glowing hair, but expose for the face if you want it visible.","DIY tools: a white manila sheet or foil-covered cardboard as a reflector; a white curtain or bedsheet to soften harsh light; a cheap LED ring light for night filming.","Demonstrate moving a product from a dark corner to window light and show the difference."]},{"title":"Mobile video production","steps":["Orientation: vertical (9:16) for TikTok, Reels, Shorts and status; horizontal (16:9) for YouTube. Decide before filming.","Shot types: wide (where are we), medium (who and what are they doing), close-up (detail and emotion). A good sequence mixes them: wide market, medium vendor arranging tomatoes, close-up hands and a smile.","B-roll: extra footage that shows what is being talked about (hands working, the street outside, the product). Film more B-roll than you think you need.","Stability: two hands, elbows tucked in, lean on a wall, use a cheap tripod or a stack of books. Hold each shot steady for at least 5 seconds so you have room to edit.","Movement: slow pan, push in (walk slowly towards the subject), follow (walk behind someone). Move slowly; fast movement looks shaky.","Settings: film at 1080p and 30 frames per second as a safe default; check storage before you start."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Capturing clean sound","steps":["People forgive average video but not bad sound. Choose quiet places, away from matatu stages, generators and loud music.","Get the mic close: the phone or a wired earphone mic about 20 to 30 cm from the speaker's mouth; a cheap lapel mic improves sound a lot.","Avoid wind (shield the mic, record indoors or behind a wall) and echo (rooms with soft furnishings echo less than empty rooms).","Always record a 10-second test, listen with earphones, then record."]},{"title":"Practical shoot","steps":["In pairs (they help each other), learners complete two tasks. Task 1 - a five-photo set for their brief: one wide, one medium, one close-up detail, one top-down or low angle, one portrait of a person with consent - each using a different composition technique and good light.","Task 2 - film the clips for their storyboard using the shot list, including at least three B-roll shots and one clean audio clip (a short voice line or interview answer).","The facilitator circulates and coaches on light, stability and sound. Learners filming off-site for their brief can do task 2 as homework but must shoot practice clips now.","Remind learners to ask permission before photographing anyone and not to film people who decline, or children without their parent's or guardian's consent."]},{"title":"Peer review of shots","steps":["Pairs review each other's best three photos and one clip with the checklist - focus sharp, well lit, composition technique visible, clean background, steady video, clear sound.","Each learner chooses one shot to reshoot and notes why."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners to back up their footage (cloud or computer) and to install a free video editing app before session 5.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Light makes the difference","steps":["Photograph the same product in a dark corner, at a window and with a reflector, and compare."]},{"title":"Shot sequence","steps":["Film a wide, medium and close-up sequence plus B-roll of a simple action (making chai) and show it back."]},{"title":"Sound test","steps":["Record a voice line far away, close up and with a wired earphone mic, and play all three."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Explain the rule of thirds.","guide":"Divide the frame into thirds horizontally and vertically; place the subject on the lines or intersections."},{"question":"Your subject is standing with a bright window behind them and their face is dark. What do you do?","guide":"Turn them to face the window or move; expose for the face; use a reflector."},{"question":"Name the three basic shot types and what each shows.","guide":"Wide (context), medium (action), close-up (detail, emotion)."},{"question":"Why should you avoid digital zoom?","guide":"It reduces quality; move closer instead."},{"question":"Name two ways to improve sound when filming on a phone.","guide":"Quiet location, mic close, wired or lapel mic, shield from wind, test recording."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare a product and a simple action for the demonstrations; bring a reflector and a cheap tripod if possible.","Identify safe, quiet shooting spots near the venue."],"delivery":["Most of the session should be hands-on; keep each lesson segment practical with a live demonstration.","Coach individually during the shoot; praise good light and composition loudly so others learn."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners must ask permission before photographing or filming anyone; no filming of children without parental or guardian consent.","Safety when shooting outdoors - stay in groups, avoid dangerous roads and do not film police operations, military sites or restricted areas.","Learners with basic phones can still do composition and light exercises; pair them for video.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Check that every learner has footage to edit in session 5.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 5,
   $cv13${"title":"Video editing, audio and sound, captions and creative writing","description":"Edit a short video on a phone, improve audio and add music legally, write on-screen captions and post captions, and export for each platform.","objectives":["Apply editing principles (hook first, cut on action, pacing, jump cuts, sequences, minimal transitions)","Edit a 30 to 60-second video in a free mobile editor (trim, split, arrange, speed, text, audio, colour)","Improve audio (levels, noise reduction, voice-over) and use music legally (platform libraries, royalty-free, licensed)","Add accurate on-screen captions for accessibility and check auto-captions","Write a post caption using creative writing techniques with a hook and call to action","Export with the right settings for each platform"],"activities":["Recap and footage check","Editing principles and demonstration","Edit your video","Stretch break","Audio and sound - editing and legal music","Captions and creative writing","Finish and export","Screening and knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Edited 30 to 60-second video with captions and legal audio; Post caption with hook and call to action","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners turn raw footage into a finished short video. They learn editing principles and practise them in a free mobile editor, improve sound and use music legally, write on-screen captions for accessibility and post captions that connect, and export correctly for each platform.","objectives":["Apply editing principles (hook first, cut on action, pacing, jump cuts, sequences, minimal transitions)","Edit a 30 to 60-second video in a free mobile editor (trim, split, arrange, speed, text, audio, colour)","Improve audio (levels, noise reduction, voice-over) and use music legally (platform libraries, royalty-free, licensed)","Add accurate on-screen captions for accessibility and check auto-captions","Write a post caption using creative writing techniques with a hook and call to action","Export with the right settings for each platform"],"topics":[{"title":"Video editing","subtopics":["Hook first - put the best moment at the start","Cut on action and remove dead time","Pacing and rhythm","Jump cuts and sequences","Transitions - less is more","Speed changes","Colour and brightness basics","Aspect ratio and safe zones"]},{"title":"Audio and sound (editing)","subtopics":["Balancing voice and music levels","Noise reduction","Recording a voice-over","Sound effects","Music licensing - platform libraries, royalty-free and licensed music","Why using popular songs without permission can get content removed or muted"]},{"title":"Captions and creative writing","subtopics":["On-screen captions for accessibility and silent viewing","Checking auto-captions (names, Kiswahili, Sheng)","Writing post captions - hook, value, story, call to action","Hashtags and keywords","Voice and tone"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and footage check","kind":"introduction","summary":"Check your footage is ready and learn today's plan."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Editing principles and demonstration","kind":"demonstration","summary":"Learn the editing principles and watch a short video edited step by step."},{"minutes":25,"title":"Edit your video","kind":"exercise","summary":"Edit your 30 to 60-second video using the principles."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Audio and sound - editing and legal music","kind":"lesson","summary":"Improve sound, add a voice-over and use music legally."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Captions and creative writing","kind":"lesson","summary":"Add accurate on-screen captions and write a post caption that connects."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Finish and export","kind":"exercise","summary":"Finish your video and export it correctly for your platform."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Screening and knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Screen a few videos and give feedback."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Find the hook","instructions":"Look through your clips and choose the strongest 2-second moment to open your video."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Edited short video","instructions":"Edit a 30 to 60-second video with a strong hook, no dead time, balanced audio, corrected captions and a call to action."},{"title":"Post caption","instructions":"Write a caption with a hook, value or story, call to action and relevant hashtags."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Screening","instructions":"Watch volunteers' videos and give feedback on hook, pacing, sound and captions."}],"examples":["A 40-second 'mandazi at 6 am in Kariokor' video with a voice-over in Kiswahili and English captions.","A Mombasa creator whose video was muted for using a popular song, re-uploaded with platform-library music.","Auto-captions turning Kenyan place names into nonsense, corrected by hand."],"tools":["Free mobile video editor (for example CapCut, InShot or VN - check current terms and availability)","Earphones; learners' footage; facilitator's sample footage as a backup","Editing checklist and caption template","Free editors: CapCut, InShot or VN (check current terms) or the phone's built-in editor. Low storage: edit a 30-second version. Low data: download the editor and any music on programme Wi-Fi and export at 720p."],"assessment":[{"question":"Why should the best moment come first in a short video?"},{"question":"What does 'cut on action' mean?"},{"question":"Why add on-screen captions?"},{"question":"Where can you get music you are allowed to use?"},{"question":"What export settings are a safe default for short vertical video?"}],"expected_outputs":["Edited 30 to 60-second video with captions and legal audio","Post caption with hook and call to action"],"homework":["Improve your video based on the screening feedback.","Collect your logo, colours or style references for your brief (or start thinking about your creator brand).","Publish your video on your own account or the brief's account (with permission) and note views, watch time and comments after 48 hours. Private option: share it as an unlisted or private link, or submit it only in DIRA LEARN, and collect feedback from three people instead of public views."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing covers persuasive captions and copywriting tied to a sale. Content Creation covers captions as accessibility and creative writing that builds an audience."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and footage check","steps":["Learners confirm they have their clips and a free editor installed. Pair anyone without footage with a partner's clips or the facilitator's sample footage.","Show a before (raw clips) and after (finished 40-second video) to show what they will make today."]},{"title":"Editing principles and demonstration","steps":["Principles: (1) hook first - start with the most interesting moment, not 'Hi guys'; (2) cut dead time - remove pauses, setup and repeated shots; (3) cut on action - cut when something moves so the edit feels natural; (4) pacing - change shot every 2 to 4 seconds for short-form; (5) sequences - wide, medium, close-up; (6) transitions - simple cuts are best, flashy effects distract.","Live edit in a free mobile editor (for example CapCut, InShot or VN): import clips, put them in storyboard order, trim the start and end of each clip, split and delete a mistake, move the best shot to the start, adjust speed of one B-roll shot, add a title text, adjust brightness and contrast slightly.","Safe zones: keep text and faces away from the bottom and right edges where platform buttons and captions cover the screen."]},{"title":"Edit your video","steps":["Learners edit their storyboard footage into a 30 to 60-second video, following the principles and their storyboard (they may improve the story while editing).","Checkpoint at 15 minutes: the hook is in the first 2 seconds and there is no dead time.","The facilitator circulates and helps with the app."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Audio and sound - editing and legal music","steps":["Levels: voice must be clearly louder than music; lower the music under speech (many editors call this ducking or allow manual volume).","Noise reduction: use the editor's noise reduction feature if available; it cannot fix very bad recordings, so capture well.","Voice-over: record in a quiet room close to the phone, speak slowly and clearly, re-record mistakes.","Music licensing: songs belong to their creators and labels. Platforms such as TikTok and Instagram provide music libraries that are licensed for use on that platform within their terms; business accounts may have fewer choices. Royalty-free libraries (for example the YouTube Audio Library) provide music you can use under their terms. Using a popular Kenyan or international song from elsewhere without a licence can get your video muted or removed, or lead to a copyright claim.","Learners add music or a voice-over to their video and balance the levels."]},{"title":"Captions and creative writing","steps":["On-screen captions: many people watch without sound, and captions make content accessible to people who are deaf or hard of hearing. Use the editor's auto-captions, then correct every error - names, places, Kiswahili and Sheng words are often wrong. Keep two lines maximum, high contrast, inside the safe zone.","Post caption structure: hook (first line visible before 'more'), value or story (what the viewer gets, or the story behind the video), call to action (follow, comment, share, visit), and a few relevant hashtags or keywords that people actually search.","Creative writing techniques: specific details ('the smell of mandazi at 6 am in Kariokor'), a question to start conversation, humour that fits the audience, and short sentences.","Learners add on-screen captions and write the post caption."]},{"title":"Finish and export","steps":["Final check: hook, no dead time, sound levels, captions correct, text in safe zones, ending with a call to action or logo.","Export: 1080p, 30 fps for most platforms; 9:16 for short vertical video; save a copy to the cloud. Remove the editor's watermark only if the app's terms allow it.","Learners save the final video in their DIRA LEARN portfolio folder."]},{"title":"Screening and knowledge check","steps":["Screen three or four videos (volunteers). The group gives feedback on the hook, pacing, sound and captions.","Ask two or three assessment questions."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Set the homework and remind learners to bring their brand ideas to session 6."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Live mobile edit","steps":["Import, order, trim, split, reorder for the hook, speed change, title, brightness, export."]},{"title":"Audio balance and captions","steps":["Add music, lower it under the voice, generate auto-captions and correct errors."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Why should the best moment come first in a short video?","guide":"Viewers decide in the first 2 seconds whether to keep watching."},{"question":"What does 'cut on action' mean?","guide":"Cutting during a movement so the transition feels natural."},{"question":"Why add on-screen captions?","guide":"Silent viewing, accessibility for deaf and hard of hearing viewers, clarity."},{"question":"Where can you get music you are allowed to use?","guide":"Platform music libraries within their terms, royalty-free libraries under their licences, music you have permission or a licence for, your own music."},{"question":"What export settings are a safe default for short vertical video?","guide":"1080 x 1920 (9:16), 1080p, 30 fps."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Install and test the editor yourself; prepare sample footage for learners who have none.","Prepare a before-and-after example video."],"delivery":["Learners progress at different speeds; pair confident editors with beginners.","Do not try to teach every feature; teach the 20% that gives 80% of the result."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Phones with little storage struggle; let learners edit a shorter video or use a partner's phone.","Remind learners that content must respect the consent given by the people filmed.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Review the videos in the portfolio folder and give one improvement each.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 6,
   $cv13${"title":"Graphic design, visual storytelling, branding and responsible content","description":"Apply graphic design fundamentals, tell stories visually in carousels and posters, keep content consistent with a brand, and use content legally and responsibly.","objectives":["Apply the design principles contrast, repetition, alignment and proximity, plus typography and colour basics","Use grids and hierarchy to lay out a poster and a social post","Tell a story across a carousel (hook slide, steps or before-and-after, payoff, call to action)","Create a content brand kit (colours, fonts, logo or signature, templates, voice, recurring series)","Explain copyright basics in Kenya, licences, Creative Commons, consent and model releases, and platform rules","Keep a credit and consent log for all content"],"activities":["Recap and video results","Graphic design fundamentals","Visual storytelling","Content branding and consistency","Stretch break","Copyright and responsible content use","Design a branded post, poster and carousel","Peer critique and knowledge check","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Content brand kit with three templates; Branded post, poster and carousel; Credit and consent log (started)","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners design graphics that look professional and tell a story, create a content brand kit and templates that keep their content consistent, and learn the copyright, consent and responsibility rules every creator must follow. They make a branded post, poster and carousel and start a credit and consent log.","objectives":["Apply the design principles contrast, repetition, alignment and proximity, plus typography and colour basics","Use grids and hierarchy to lay out a poster and a social post","Tell a story across a carousel (hook slide, steps or before-and-after, payoff, call to action)","Create a content brand kit (colours, fonts, logo or signature, templates, voice, recurring series)","Explain copyright basics in Kenya, licences, Creative Commons, consent and model releases, and platform rules","Keep a credit and consent log for all content"],"topics":[{"title":"Graphic design fundamentals","subtopics":["Contrast","Repetition","Alignment","Proximity","Typography - two fonts, sizes and weights","Colour - palettes and meaning","Grids and hierarchy","Readable on a phone"]},{"title":"Visual storytelling","subtopics":["Carousels as stories","Before-and-after","Step-by-step","Simple infographics","Thumbnails and covers","Posters for events and causes"]},{"title":"Content branding and consistency","subtopics":["Creator or brand kit","Templates","Recurring series and formats","Consistent voice","Signature elements (intro, colours, sound)"]},{"title":"Copyright and responsible content use","subtopics":["What copyright protects","Kenya's Copyright Act and the Kenya Copyright Board (KECOBO)","Licences and permission","Creative Commons and stock sites","Fair dealing is limited - not a free pass","Consent and model releases","Children and vulnerable people","Misinformation, defamation and hate speech","Platform community guidelines","Disclosing paid partnerships and AI-generated content"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and video results","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share the results of the video you published."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Graphic design fundamentals","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the principles that make graphics look professional."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Visual storytelling","kind":"lesson","summary":"Tell stories across carousels, posters and covers."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Content branding and consistency","kind":"lesson","summary":"Build a brand kit and templates so your content is recognisable."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Copyright and responsible content use","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the legal and ethical rules for using and creating content."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Design a branded post, poster and carousel","kind":"exercise","summary":"Create your brand kit and three branded designs, and start your credit and consent log."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Peer critique and knowledge check","kind":"assessment","summary":"Give design feedback using the CRAP checklist and answer questions."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Licence check","instructions":"Find one image on a free stock site and one Creative Commons image, and record the licence conditions for each."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Content brand kit","instructions":"Create a brand kit with colours, fonts, signature, style, voice, a recurring series and three templates."},{"title":"Three branded designs","instructions":"Design a post, a poster and a carousel that use your kit and tell a clear story."},{"title":"Credit and consent log","instructions":"Record the source and licence of everything you did not create, and the consent for everyone who appears."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Design critique","instructions":"Critique a partner's designs with the CRAP checklist and the 3-second test."}],"examples":["Redesigning a Nakuru youth football tournament poster.","The 'Otieno Shoots' brand kit with a 'Cheap Eats Friday' series.","A Kibera recycling group carousel showing a dumpsite before and after a clean-up.","A creator whose video was removed after using a film clip without permission."],"tools":["Canva (free) or a similar design tool; paper and pens as a backup","CRAP checklist, brand kit template, credit and consent log template, simple model release template","Free stock and Creative Commons sources (follow their licence terms)","Free design options: Canva free, the phone's built-in markup tools, or a paper layout photographed in good light; design offline where possible."],"assessment":[{"question":"What do the letters in CRAP stand for?"},{"question":"You downloaded a photo from Google Images. Can you use it in a client's poster?"},{"question":"What is a model release and when do you need one?"},{"question":"Name three things in a content brand kit."},{"question":"What must you do when content is a paid partnership?"}],"expected_outputs":["Content brand kit with three templates","Branded post, poster and carousel","Credit and consent log (started)"],"homework":["Finish all three designs and fix the issues from the critique.","Complete your credit and consent log for everything made so far, including your video.","Choose the three to five best pieces for your portfolio."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing teaches a business brand identity to make a campaign consistent and persuasive. Content Creation teaches design and branding as a creative discipline and how a creator's own brand is built across content.","AI Tools covers AI images and disclosure; here learners learn traditional design and the legal rules that apply to all content."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and video results","steps":["Learners share views, watch time or comments from their published video and one thing they learned.","Introduce today - design, brand and the rules that protect creators and the people they feature."]},{"title":"Graphic design fundamentals","steps":["Teach CRAP with good and bad examples: Contrast (make the important thing different - size, colour, weight); Repetition (repeat colours, fonts and styles so it feels like one design); Alignment (everything lines up with something; use the grid); Proximity (group related items, separate unrelated ones).","Typography: maximum two fonts - one for headings, one for body; use size and weight for hierarchy; avoid decorative fonts for long text; check readability at phone size.","Colour: a palette of two or three main colours plus neutrals; colours carry meaning (green - fresh, growth; red - urgency, passion); ensure strong contrast between text and background.","Grids and hierarchy: decide the one thing viewers should notice first, second and third. Use margins and consistent spacing.","Redesign a cluttered event poster live (a youth football tournament poster with five fonts and no hierarchy) into a clean one."]},{"title":"Visual storytelling","steps":["Carousel structure: slide 1 hook (a bold question or promise), slides 2 to 6 the story (steps, before-and-after, tips), last slide payoff and call to action (save, share, follow, visit).","Before-and-after: a room makeover, a hairstyle, a cleaned-up river bank; consistent angle makes it powerful.","Simple infographics: one idea, few numbers, clear icons; always credit the data source.","Thumbnails and covers: a face with emotion, three to five words, high contrast.","Posters for events and causes: what, when, where, who, how to join - the 5Ws in hierarchy."]},{"title":"Content branding and consistency","steps":["Explain why consistency matters - people should recognise your content in a busy feed before seeing your name.","Brand kit contents: logo or signature mark, two or three colours with codes, two fonts, photo and video style (bright, moody, natural), voice (playful, calm, expert), recurring series ('Cheap Eats Friday'), intro or outro style, and three templates (post, carousel, story).","Show 'Otieno Shoots' brand kit as an example.","Demonstrate saving a template in Canva (or similar) and reusing it with new content."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Copyright and responsible content use","steps":["Copyright protects original works - photos, videos, music, designs, writing - from the moment they are created. In Kenya, the Copyright Act sets the rules and the Kenya Copyright Board (KECOBO) administers copyright matters. Owning a copy (downloading a photo) does not give you the right to reuse it.","To use others' work you need permission or a licence. Sources: your own content, the brief owner's content with permission, Creative Commons works (follow the licence - attribution, non-commercial, no derivatives), and free stock sites under their licence terms. Keep the licence link in your credit log.","Fair dealing (limited uses such as criticism, review or news reporting) exists in many laws, but it is narrow and not a free pass to use songs, films or photos in your content. When in doubt, do not use it or ask permission.","Consent: get clear permission before filming or photographing people for content, and written permission (a model release - a simple signed or WhatsApp-confirmed agreement) when the content is for a business or will be used widely. Never post children without their parent's or guardian's consent, and avoid identifying vulnerable people.","Responsibility: do not spread misinformation (check facts before posting), do not defame (false statements that damage someone's reputation), avoid hate speech and content that incites violence, follow platform community guidelines, disclose paid partnerships ('#ad', 'paid partnership') and AI-generated content.","Protecting your own work: add a subtle signature or watermark on photos, keep original files, and know you can report stolen content to platforms."]},{"title":"Design a branded post, poster and carousel","steps":["Learners create their content brand kit for their brief (or creator brand) in Canva or on paper.","They design three pieces using the kit: a social post, an event or promotional poster, and a three to five-slide carousel that tells a story (hook, story, payoff and CTA).","They start a credit and consent log - every image, music track, font or video not made by them (source, licence, link) and every person who appears (consent obtained, how, date)."]},{"title":"Peer critique and knowledge check","steps":["In pairs, learners critique each other's designs with the CRAP checklist and the 3-second test.","Ask two or three assessment questions, especially about copyright and consent."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Remind learners that session 7 is the portfolio build - all pieces and logs must be ready.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Poster redesign","steps":["Redesign a cluttered poster live by applying contrast, repetition, alignment and proximity."]},{"title":"Saving and reusing a template","steps":["Create a branded template and reuse it for a new post in under two minutes."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What do the letters in CRAP stand for?","guide":"Contrast, Repetition, Alignment, Proximity."},{"question":"You downloaded a photo from Google Images. Can you use it in a client's poster?","guide":"Not without permission or a licence that allows it; downloading does not give the right to reuse."},{"question":"What is a model release and when do you need one?","guide":"Written permission from a person to use their image; needed for business or widely used content."},{"question":"Name three things in a content brand kit.","guide":"Colours, fonts, logo or signature, style, voice, templates, recurring series."},{"question":"What must you do when content is a paid partnership?","guide":"Disclose it clearly (for example"}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the cluttered poster, the example brand kit and templates, and the model release template.","Read KECOBO's public guidance so you can answer basic questions accurately; do not give legal advice."],"delivery":["Keep design theory visual; show before-and-after every time.","Make copyright practical - ask learners to audit the content they used in their session 5 video."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Consent and images of children are safeguarding issues as well as legal ones; be firm.","Learners must not use content that sexualises, humiliates or targets anyone.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Check credit and consent logs in the portfolio folders.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 7,
   $cv13${"title":"Portfolio development and creative project build","description":"Build a content portfolio and complete the creative project for your brief, test it with peers and prepare the showcase.","objectives":["Explain what makes a strong content portfolio (best three to five pieces, variety, results, story behind each piece)","Choose a portfolio platform (Google Drive folder, Behance, Instagram highlights, a simple website)","Write a short case study for one piece (brief, idea, process, result, learning)","Complete the creative project to a publishable standard","Improve the project based on peer testing","Explain basic freelance terms (brief, deliverables, revisions, deadline, rate) in simple language"],"activities":["Project check-in","What makes a strong portfolio","Creative project production","Stretch break","Peer testing","Revise and prepare the showcase","Showcase briefing and homework"],"expected_output":"Completed creative project (video, photo set, branded designs); Content portfolio with three to five pieces, case-study notes and contact details; Completed credit and consent log; Showcase presentation draft","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 2 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners pull their work together into a professional content portfolio and complete their final creative project for a real brief. They learn what clients and employers look for, write short case-study notes, finish production, test with peers and prepare their showcase presentation.","objectives":["Explain what makes a strong content portfolio (best three to five pieces, variety, results, story behind each piece)","Choose a portfolio platform (Google Drive folder, Behance, Instagram highlights, a simple website)","Write a short case study for one piece (brief, idea, process, result, learning)","Complete the creative project to a publishable standard","Improve the project based on peer testing","Explain basic freelance terms (brief, deliverables, revisions, deadline, rate) in simple language"],"topics":[{"title":"Portfolio development","subtopics":["What clients and employers look for","Quality over quantity - three to five best pieces","Showing variety (video, photo, design)","Case-study notes","Portfolio platforms","Keeping the credit and consent log with the portfolio"]},{"title":"Working with clients (introduction)","subtopics":["Brief and deliverables","Revisions and deadlines","Simple pricing ideas and agreeing payment before work","Delivering files professionally"]},{"title":"Creative project completion","subtopics":["Finishing production","Quality check","Peer testing","Revising"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Project check-in","kind":"introduction","summary":"Confirm your brief, your best pieces and what is left to make."},{"minutes":15,"title":"What makes a strong portfolio","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to present your work so clients and employers take you seriously."},{"minutes":45,"title":"Creative project production","kind":"exercise","summary":"Finish every piece of your creative project and build your portfolio."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Peer testing","kind":"group","summary":"Test your portfolio and project with peers using the checklist."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Revise and prepare the showcase","kind":"exercise","summary":"Make improvements and prepare your three-minute showcase."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase briefing and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Learn the showcase format and what to submit."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Case study","instructions":"Write a case study for your best piece - brief, idea, process, result, learning."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Creative project completion","instructions":"Finish every piece of your creative project to a publishable standard."},{"title":"Portfolio build","instructions":"Build a portfolio with three to five best pieces, case-study notes, contact details and the credit and consent log. Private option: the portfolio can be a private \"anyone with the link\" folder or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Portfolio peer test","instructions":"Show your portfolio to two peers, who use the checklist to give two strengths and two improvements."}],"examples":["The 'Otieno Shoots' portfolio with a food video, a photo set and a carousel, each with a short case study.","A Nyeri creator who got a paid brief from a local cafe after sharing a Google Drive portfolio link.","A youth group media team in Kilifi documenting a beach clean-up for a conservation organisation."],"tools":["All tools from the track (phone, editor, design tool)","Portfolio checklist, case-study template, simple client brief template","Free portfolio platforms: a Google Drive folder, Behance (free) or a single PDF; compress videos to 720p to save data for viewers too."],"assessment":[{"question":"Portfolio checked with the portfolio checklist."},{"question":"What should you agree in writing with a client before starting work?"}],"expected_outputs":["Completed creative project (video, photo set, branded designs)","Content portfolio with three to five pieces, case-study notes and contact details","Completed credit and consent log","Showcase presentation draft"],"homework":["Finish your portfolio and project and upload them to DIRA LEARN.","Practise your showcase presentation twice within three minutes.","Ask your brief owner for a short comment on the work (a testimonial) if they are happy to give one.","If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute project and portfolio presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track builds its final project in session 7. The Content Creation project is judged on storytelling, production quality and responsible content use."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Project check-in","steps":["Each learner states their brief and what is left to finish in one sentence.","Remind learners of the final project contents (strategy one-pager, edited video, photo set, branded designs, credit and consent log, portfolio with case-study notes)."]},{"title":"What makes a strong portfolio","steps":["Quality over quantity: three to five best pieces beat twenty average ones. Show variety - at least one video, one photo set and one design.","Context: for each piece, add a short case study - the brief (who and what), the idea, your process (what you did and tools), the result (views, saves, client feedback, event turnout), and what you learned. Results can be small; be honest.","Platforms: a Google Drive folder with a clear index (easy and free), Behance (design), Instagram with highlights by category, or a simple free website. Use a professional username and add contact details.","Working with clients (for example a Nakuru cafe paying KES 3,000 for four short reels, half paid upfront by M-Pesa): agree the brief, deliverables, deadline, number of revisions and price in writing (WhatsApp is fine) before starting; agree a deposit for larger jobs; deliver files in the right format and size. Never pay someone for a 'job' - that is a scam.","Show the 'Otieno Shoots' portfolio index and one case study."]},{"title":"Creative project production","steps":["Learners finish production - final edits, reshoots if needed, designs, captions.","They build their portfolio (folder or platform) with three to five best pieces and write one full case study and short notes for the others.","Checkpoint at 25 minutes: the facilitator checks each learner has a finished video and design.","The facilitator circulates for one-to-one coaching on quality and on the credit and consent log."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Peer testing","steps":["In groups of three, each learner shows their portfolio for 3 minutes. Peers use the portfolio checklist: strong first piece, variety, case-study notes, quality (light, sound, design), consistency with brand, credits and consent recorded, contact details.","Each peer gives two strengths and two improvements."]},{"title":"Revise and prepare the showcase","steps":["Learners make at least one improvement from the feedback.","They prepare the showcase: self-pitch introduction, the brief, show the project (play the video, show designs), explain one creative choice and one result, what they learned, what next."]},{"title":"Showcase briefing and homework","steps":["Explain the showcase format (3 minutes plus 2 minutes of questions) and share the rubric.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Portfolio walkthrough","steps":["Show a complete example portfolio index and read one case study aloud."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Portfolio checked with the portfolio checklist.","guide":"All checklist items present; first piece strong; credits and consent recorded."},{"question":"What should you agree in writing with a client before starting work?","guide":"Brief, deliverables, deadline, revisions, price and payment terms."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the example portfolio and case study, the portfolio checklist and the client brief template.","Set the session 8 room plan from the track group size (up to 12 - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus a recorded-questions room) and line up co-facilitators and peer panels."],"delivery":["Protect the production time; this is where projects get finished.","Prioritise coaching learners who are behind."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Check consent logs before any content featuring people goes into a public portfolio.","Remind learners not to publish personal phone numbers; use a business number or email.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Check that every learner has a complete project; message those at risk.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('content', 8,
   $cv13${"title":"Content showcase","description":"Present your creative project and portfolio, explain your creative choices, give and receive feedback, pitch yourself and plan your next 30 days of content.","objectives":["Present the creative project and portfolio clearly in three minutes","Explain at least one creative choice and one responsible-content choice","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves as a creator with the portfolio as proof","Write a 30-day content plan"],"activities":["Showcase set-up and expectations","Project presentations and questions - block 1","Stretch break","Project presentations and questions - block 2","Feedback round","Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","30-day content plan","Celebration and close"],"expected_output":"Presented creative project and portfolio (final project) with rubric score and feedback; Portfolio message and creator pitch; 30-day content plan; Written feedback given to two peers","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 3 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners present their creative project and portfolio, explain their creative and ethical choices, and answer questions. They practise pitching themselves as creators to clients and employers, show professional behaviour in a showcase setting and leave with a 30-day content plan.","objectives":["Present the creative project and portfolio clearly in three minutes","Explain at least one creative choice and one responsible-content choice","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves as a creator with the portfolio as proof","Write a 30-day content plan"],"topics":[{"title":"Presenting creative work","subtopics":["Showcase structure","Explaining creative choices","Answering questions"]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and your work","subtopics":["Creator self-pitch","Sharing your portfolio link professionally","Talking about rates and briefs"]},{"title":"Professional behaviour and workplace readiness","subtopics":["Showcase etiquette","Receiving critical feedback","Following up with clients and guests","Pathways - employment, freelance, creator brand, media and NGO communications"]},{"title":"Next steps","subtopics":["30-day content plan","Staying consistent"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","kind":"introduction","summary":"Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Project presentations and questions - block 1","kind":"assessment","summary":"The first six presenters in each room present and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Project presentations and questions - block 2","kind":"assessment","summary":"The next six presenters in each room present; recorded presenters answer their live questions."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Feedback round","kind":"group","summary":"Share feedback and learn from patterns across projects."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","kind":"lesson","summary":"Practise pitching yourself as a creator and learn the pathways for creative work."},{"minutes":15,"title":"30-day content plan","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write your plan for the next 30 days of content."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Celebration and close","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Celebrate and learn what happens next."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Portfolio message","instructions":"Write a three-sentence message introducing yourself and your portfolio link to a potential client."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Showcase presentation","instructions":"Present your project and portfolio in three minutes and answer questions for two minutes."},{"title":"30-day content plan","instructions":"Complete the plan with goal, pillars, rhythm, weekly actions, measure, partner and risk."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer feedback","instructions":"Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."},{"title":"Pitch practice","instructions":"Practise your creator pitch in pairs."}],"examples":["A Nyeri creator pitching cafes with a 12,000-view video as proof.","A Kilifi youth media team showing a conservation clean-up documentary.","A Kisumu photographer using the portfolio to apply for an NGO communications internship."],"tools":["Projector or screen sharing with sound, learners' portfolios","Showcase rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN, timer, 30-day plan template","Low-data showcase: play every video from a downloaded file; online learners share their link or file in advance."],"assessment":[{"question":"Presentation scored with the content showcase rubric."},{"question":"Explain one creative choice and one responsible-content choice in your project."},{"question":"Deliver your creator pitch."}],"expected_outputs":["Presented creative project and portfolio (final project) with rubric score and feedback","Portfolio message and creator pitch","30-day content plan","Written feedback given to two peers"],"homework":["Submit the final project, portfolio link and 30-day plan in DIRA LEARN.","Thank your brief owner and deliver the final files."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the Content Creation rubric weights storytelling, production quality and responsible content."],"track_project":"A content portfolio built around one creative project for a real brief (a business, a cause, a community or the learner's own creator brand). It contains a content strategy one-pager, at least one edited short video with captions, a photo set, a branded graphic or carousel, a credit and consent log, and a portfolio page or folder with short case-study notes, presented in session 8 with a 30-day content plan. Privacy option: the portfolio can be a private link (an unlisted or \"anyone with the link\" folder) or files submitted only in DIRA LEARN, and the brief can be simulated. Identifiable people appear only with consent recorded in the credit and consent log."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","steps":["Welcome learners and guests (brief owners, DIRA staff, mentors, employers). Thank brief owners.","Share the rubric: storytelling and strategy (20%), production quality - photo, video, sound (25%), design and brand consistency (20%), responsible content - credits, consent, honesty (15%), presentation and answers (20%).","Assign each learner two presenters to give written feedback to."]},{"title":"Project presentations and questions - block 1","steps":["Run the screenings with the session 8 room plan in the facilitator instructions: the first six presenters in each room go in this block, each with 3 minutes plus 2 minutes of questions.","Learners present in order, 3 minutes each plus 2 minutes of questions. Play videos with sound checked in advance.","Ask at least one question per presenter, such as: 'Why did you open with that shot?', 'What would you change with more time?', 'How did you get consent?', 'What result did this piece get?'","Score with the rubric and note one strength and one improvement."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Project presentations and questions - block 2","steps":["Presenters 7 to 12 in each live room now show their projects and portfolios; check the sound before each video and keep the block 1 timer, rubric and questions.","In a recorded-questions room, do not replay the recordings (the panel scored them before the session); give each recorded presenter 2 minutes of live questions about their creative project and portfolio.","Each room's panel hands its completed creative project and portfolio rubric sheets to the room facilitator before the feedback round."]},{"title":"Feedback round","steps":["Learners give written feedback to their two assigned presenters.","Facilitator highlights patterns in strong projects - strong hooks, good light and sound, consistent brand, clear credits."]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","steps":["Creator pitch formula: who you are, what you make and for whom, proof (a piece and its result), the ask. Example: 'I am Wanjiku, a Nyeri content creator. I make short videos that help small cafes get noticed - my last video for a local cafe got 12,000 views and the owner says weekend visits went up. I am looking for two more cafes to work with this month.'","Sharing your portfolio: a short link, a clean index, and a message that introduces you and the value you offer - not 'please check my page'.","Pathways: employment (social media assistant, videographer, communications assistant at an NGO or company), freelance briefs, building a creator brand, media houses and production companies. Rates vary; start by agreeing clear deliverables and never paying to get work.","Professional follow-up: thank brief owners and guests within two days, deliver final files, ask for a testimonial.","Learners practise their pitch once in pairs."]},{"title":"30-day content plan","steps":["Learners complete the 30-day content plan: main goal, pillars, posting rhythm, four weekly actions (for example week 1 publish two portfolio pieces, week 2 pitch three local businesses, week 3 start a recurring series, week 4 review analytics), a measure, an accountability partner and one risk.","Learners share their goal with their accountability partner."]},{"title":"Celebration and close","steps":["Celebrate each learner's project in one sentence.","Explain how to submit the portfolio link, creative project and credit and consent log in DIRA LEARN, how results and certificates follow the programme's rules, and how alumni can share paid briefs with each other."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Model showcase","steps":["Before the session, share a recorded 3-minute example presentation as a reference. Do not use showcase time for it."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Presentation scored with the content showcase rubric.","guide":"Use rubric descriptors; honest results and responsible content choices count."},{"question":"Explain one creative choice and one responsible-content choice in your project.","guide":"A specific, reasoned example of each."},{"question":"Deliver your creator pitch.","guide":"Who, what for whom, proof, ask."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Test video playback with sound for every learner before the session; collect files in advance where possible.","Invite brief owners and guests; brief guests on respectful questions and safeguarding expectations.","Confirm the room plan: count the learners in the track group, book the rooms and co-facilitators, brief the peer panels, and publish each room's presenter order and the list of recorded presenters by the end of session 7."],"delivery":["Keep strict time; celebrate creativity and honest reflection.","Session 8 room plan - Room plan (set in session 7 from the track group size; the session is never extended): up to 12 learners - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms in parallel; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus one recorded-questions room. Each live room takes at most 12 presenters: 6 in each 30-minute block, 3 minutes to present and 2 minutes of questions each.","Every room has a facilitator or briefed co-facilitator (another trainer, a mentor or DIRA staff member who knows the rubric and the safeguarding expectations) and a peer panel of two learners who present in the other block. All rooms use the same rubric.","Recorded option: learners in a recorded-questions room, and anyone who cannot present live (absence, connection problems, anxiety, or the privacy option), submit a 3-minute recording - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - by the day before session 8. The panel scores it in advance with the same rubric; in session 8 each recorded presenter answers 2 minutes of live questions (12 per block). If more learners need the recorded option than one room can take, the panel sends written questions and the learner answers them in DIRA LEARN.","Timekeeping: a visible timer with a 30-second warning; stop at 3 minutes. If a block runs late, cut questions to 1 minute - do not extend the session."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners who cannot present live can submit a recorded presentation according to the programme's rules.","Guests must not contact learners privately outside programme channels.","Shared devices and low data: learners without a suitable phone shoot and edit in pairs or threes and take turns as camera operator, presenter and editor; plan uploads and downloads for programme Wi-Fi. Never require a paid app, premium template or paid music."],"after_session":["Enter rubric scores and feedback in DIRA LEARN grading.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 3,
   $cv13${"title":"Opportunity spotting","description":"Identify customer needs and business opportunities; distinguish assumptions from evidence.","objectives":["Identify business opportunities from problems, trends, skills and resources in their community","Describe a customer problem in terms of the job the customer is trying to do, their pains and the gains they want","Distinguish assumptions from evidence and list the riskiest assumptions in an idea","Plan simple market research using primary (observation, interviews, surveys) and secondary (reports, data) sources","Describe a target market and early adopters for an idea","Conduct a practice customer interview using good, non-leading questions","Write a one-page opportunity statement"],"activities":["Welcome to Entrepreneurship & Business and track overview","Identifying business opportunities","Understanding customer problems","Market research and target markets","Stretch break","Demonstration - good and bad customer interviews","Interview guide and practice interviews","Opportunity statement","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Three community problems and an opportunity chosen with the filter; Interview guide (eight to ten questions); One-page opportunity statement with top three assumptions","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners learn to find business opportunities in real problems around them, understand customers' problems deeply, do simple market research, and describe customer needs and target markets. They separate assumptions from evidence, prepare an interview guide and write a one-page opportunity statement.","objectives":["Identify business opportunities from problems, trends, skills and resources in their community","Describe a customer problem in terms of the job the customer is trying to do, their pains and the gains they want","Distinguish assumptions from evidence and list the riskiest assumptions in an idea","Plan simple market research using primary (observation, interviews, surveys) and secondary (reports, data) sources","Describe a target market and early adopters for an idea","Conduct a practice customer interview using good, non-leading questions","Write a one-page opportunity statement"],"topics":[{"title":"Identifying business opportunities","subtopics":["Problems as opportunities","Trends (mobile money, online shopping, climate, urbanisation)","Your skills, networks and resources","Gaps in local markets","Opportunity versus idea"]},{"title":"Understanding customer problems","subtopics":["The job the customer is trying to do","Pains and gains","How customers solve the problem today","Frequency and intensity of the problem","Willingness to pay"]},{"title":"Market research","subtopics":["Primary research - observation, interviews, short surveys","Secondary research - KNBS data, county information, industry reports, news","Asking good questions (past behaviour, not opinions about the future)","Avoiding leading questions"]},{"title":"Customer needs and target markets","subtopics":["Segments","Target market","Early adopters","Rough market size (how many, how often, how much)","Competition and alternatives"]},{"title":"Assumptions versus evidence","subtopics":["Listing assumptions","Riskiest assumptions first","What counts as evidence"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Welcome to Entrepreneurship & Business and track overview","kind":"introduction","summary":"Meet your track group and see the six sessions and final business pitch."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Identifying business opportunities","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn where business opportunities come from and how to spot them."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Understanding customer problems","kind":"lesson","summary":"Look at problems from the customer's point of view - the job, the pains and the gains."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Market research and target markets","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn simple ways to research a market and define your target customers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Demonstration - good and bad customer interviews","kind":"demonstration","summary":"See the difference between a leading interview and a good one that uncovers real behaviour."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Interview guide and practice interviews","kind":"group","summary":"Write your interview guide and practise interviewing in pairs."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Opportunity statement","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write a one-page opportunity statement for your idea."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Opportunity filter","instructions":"Run your three problems through the four opportunity filter questions and choose one."},{"title":"Job, pains and gains","instructions":"Map the job, pains, gains and current alternatives for your chosen problem."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Interview guide","instructions":"Write eight to ten non-leading questions about past behaviour, current solutions, pains and spending."},{"title":"Opportunity statement","instructions":"Complete the one-page opportunity statement including your top three assumptions."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Practice interviews","instructions":"Interview a partner for five minutes each and get feedback on leading questions."}],"examples":["A laundry pick-up service for university students in Nakuru.","Dried mango snacks from mango waste in Machakos.","A Kibera youth group collecting plastic for recycling companies.","A second-hand school uniform exchange in Kisii before school opening."],"tools":["Interview guide template, opportunity statement template","KNBS and county government websites (for secondary research)","Phone for notes or recording (with permission)"],"assessment":[{"question":"What is the difference between an idea and an opportunity?"},{"question":"Rewrite this leading question - 'Would you buy my cheap healthy juice?'"},{"question":"What is the riskiest assumption in your idea?"},{"question":"Name one primary and one secondary research method."},{"question":"Who are early adopters?"}],"expected_outputs":["Three community problems and an opportunity chosen with the filter","Interview guide (eight to ten questions)","One-page opportunity statement with top three assumptions"],"homework":["Conduct at least five real customer interviews using your guide and record what you learned (quotes, numbers, surprises). Tell each person why you are asking and that they can decline; record answers without names unless they agree.","Find one piece of secondary data about your market (with source and date)."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing also studies customers, but to plan channels and messages for an existing offer. Here learners study customers to decide whether a business is worth starting at all."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Welcome to Entrepreneurship & Business and track overview","steps":["Show the journey: S3 opportunity spotting, S4 value proposition and business model, S5 money - costing, pricing and cash flow, S6 marketing, customer experience and sales, S7 test and pitch, S8 pitch showcase.","Explain the final project - a tested business concept and a 3-minute pitch. Say that the goal is evidence, not a perfect plan.","Ask learners to share in one sentence a business they admire in their area and why."]},{"title":"Identifying business opportunities","steps":["Define an opportunity: a problem or need that enough people have, that they would pay to solve, and that you can realistically solve. An idea is a possible solution; an opportunity is the problem plus a market.","Sources of opportunities: (1) problems people complain about (water shortages, unreliable deliveries, expensive school uniforms); (2) trends (mobile money, online shopping, solar, recycling, urban farming, a growing youth population); (3) your skills and networks (you can cook, fix phones, know farmers); (4) gaps (no laundry service near a university hostel).","Kenyan examples: a young person in Nakuru who saw students struggling to dry clothes in the rainy season and started a laundry pick-up service; a group in Machakos turning mango waste into dried mango snacks; a Kibera youth group collecting plastic for recycling companies.","Opportunity filter - ask four questions: Is the problem real and frequent? Do people pay to solve it today? Can I reach these customers? Can I start small with what I have?","Learners list three problems they have seen this week in their community."]},{"title":"Understanding customer problems","steps":["The job: what the customer is trying to get done. A mother buying a school uniform is not buying cloth; she is getting her child ready for school on time, looking smart, within budget.","Pains: what annoys, costs or worries them (long queues, poor quality, prices going up, unreliable sellers). Gains: what would delight them (durability, delivery, paying in instalments with M-Pesa).","How they solve it today: the current alternative is your real competition - even if it is 'doing nothing' or 'asking a cousin'.","Frequency, intensity and willingness to pay: a problem that happens daily and hurts a lot is a better opportunity than one that happens once a year and is mildly annoying.","Assumptions versus evidence: 'students will pay KES 100 for laundry pick-up' is an assumption until real students say they have paid for similar services or actually pay. List assumptions and mark the riskiest - the one that would kill the idea if wrong.","Work through one learner's problem as a group, mapping job, pains, gains and assumptions on the board."]},{"title":"Market research and target markets","steps":["Primary research: observe (count customers at a competitor's stall at different times), interview (ten conversations teach more than a hundred opinions online), short surveys (a Google Form shared in a relevant WhatsApp group, with the admin's permission).","Secondary research: the Kenya National Bureau of Statistics (KNBS) publishes population and economic data; county governments publish plans and statistics; industry and news reports give trends. Always note the source and date.","Target market and early adopters: the target market is the specific group you serve first; early adopters are the people with the problem most urgently who are already trying to solve it - they will try something new first.","Rough market size: how many potential customers in your area, how often they buy, and how much they spend. Example - 2,000 students in nearby hostels, 30% send laundry out, twice a month, KES 150 each.","Competition and alternatives: who else solves the problem and how; what you could do differently."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Demonstration - good and bad customer interviews","steps":["Role-play a bad interview with a co-facilitator or volunteer: 'Would you buy my amazing juice? It is healthy and cheap, don't you think?' Point out leading questions, talking about the idea, and asking about the future.","Role-play a good interview: 'Tell me about the last time you bought juice or a snack in the afternoon. Where? How much? What did you like or dislike? What else did you consider?' Point out questions about past behaviour, listening more than talking, and not pitching.","Share the interview guide template: warm-up, the problem (last time it happened), current solution, pains, spending, what an ideal solution would do, permission to follow up. Ten questions maximum."]},{"title":"Interview guide and practice interviews","steps":["Learners choose the opportunity they will develop for the track (from their three problems or an existing idea) and write an interview guide of eight to ten questions.","In pairs, learners interview each other (5 minutes each) as if the partner were a potential customer. The interviewer notes what they learned; the partner gives feedback on any leading questions.","Learners revise their guide based on the feedback."]},{"title":"Opportunity statement","steps":["Learners complete the one-page opportunity statement: the problem, who has it (target market and early adopters), how they solve it today, why that is not good enough, the opportunity, rough market size, top three assumptions (riskiest first), and how they will gather evidence this week.","The facilitator checks a few statements and gives quick feedback on specificity."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask two learners to read their opportunity sentence.","Set the homework - at least five real interviews before session 4."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Bad versus good customer interview","steps":["Role-play a leading interview and a good one, then debrief the differences."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What is the difference between an idea and an opportunity?","guide":"An idea is a possible solution; an opportunity is a real problem with customers willing to pay that you can reach."},{"question":"Rewrite this leading question - 'Would you buy my cheap healthy juice?'","guide":"Ask about past behaviour, for example 'Tell me about the last time you bought a drink in the afternoon.'"},{"question":"What is the riskiest assumption in your idea?","guide":"The assumption that would end the idea if wrong (often willingness to pay or reaching customers)."},{"question":"Name one primary and one secondary research method.","guide":"Primary - interviews, observation, surveys. Secondary - KNBS data, county reports, news, industry reports."},{"question":"Who are early adopters?","guide":"Customers who feel the problem most and are already looking for solutions; they try new things first."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the interview guide and opportunity statement templates and the role-play script.","Arrange a co-facilitator or volunteer for the role-play."],"delivery":["Learners often fall in love with their idea; keep bringing them back to the customer's problem and evidence.","Encourage learners to work on ideas they can test cheaply in the next three weeks."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Interviews in the community must be safe - interview in public places or by phone, never alone at a stranger's home, and tell someone where you are.","Do not interview children for business research.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Check opportunity statements and give each learner one question to explore in interviews.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 4,
   $cv13${"title":"Value proposition and business model","description":"Describe customer value and map a simple business model.","objectives":["Summarise customer interview evidence into jobs, pains and gains","Design a value proposition that matches products and services to customer pains and gains","Write a one-sentence value proposition statement","Explain the parts of a simplified business model canvas and common business model types in Kenya","Choose how customers will be reached and kept (channels and relationships), including the role of digital marketing","Map a one-page business model and defend it in a peer challenge"],"activities":["Recap and interview findings","Value propositions","Your value proposition","Business models and the simplified canvas","Stretch break","Reaching and keeping customers - marketing in the business model","Map your one-page business model","Peer challenge","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Value proposition canvas and one-sentence statement; One-page business model (simplified canvas) with evidence and assumptions marked; Peer challenge notes and a revised model","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners turn interview evidence into a clear value proposition and map how their business would create, deliver and capture value on a simplified business model canvas. They see at business-model level how the approved digital marketing topics fit the 'channels' and 'customer relationships' parts of the model, and test their model through a peer challenge. This session follows the session record approved by DIRA on 8 Nov 2026.","objectives":["Summarise customer interview evidence into jobs, pains and gains","Design a value proposition that matches products and services to customer pains and gains","Write a one-sentence value proposition statement","Explain the parts of a simplified business model canvas and common business model types in Kenya","Choose how customers will be reached and kept (channels and relationships), including the role of digital marketing","Map a one-page business model and defend it in a peer challenge"],"topics":[{"title":"Value propositions","subtopics":["Customer profile - jobs, pains, gains","Value map - products and services, pain relievers, gain creators","Fit between customer and offer","Value proposition statement","Differentiation from alternatives"]},{"title":"Business models","subtopics":["Simplified business model canvas - customers, value proposition, channels, customer relationships, revenue streams, key activities, key resources, key partners, costs","Business model types - product sales, services, subscriptions, commissions and marketplaces, rental, freemium","Kenyan examples"]},{"title":"Reaching and keeping customers in the business model","subtopics":["Channels - how customers find, buy and receive","Digital marketing as a channel - social media, WhatsApp Business, content, advertising, search and measuring results (approved topics, business-model level)","Customer relationships - self-service, personal service, community"]},{"title":"Peer challenge","subtopics":["Testing the logic of a model","Asking hard questions respectfully","Revising the model"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and interview findings","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share what your customer interviews revealed."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Value propositions","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to match your offer to customers' jobs, pains and gains."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Your value proposition","kind":"exercise","summary":"Complete the value proposition canvas and statement for your idea using interview evidence."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Business models and the simplified canvas","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the parts of a business model and common model types in Kenya."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Reaching and keeping customers - marketing in the business model","kind":"lesson","summary":"See where the approved digital marketing topics fit in the channels and relationships boxes of your model."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Map your one-page business model","kind":"exercise","summary":"Complete the simplified business model canvas for your idea."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Peer challenge","kind":"group","summary":"Defend your business model against friendly but tough questions and improve it."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Model type match","instructions":"Decide which business model type fits your idea and consider one alternative model."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Value proposition canvas and statement","instructions":"Complete the canvas with interview evidence and write your one-sentence statement."},{"title":"One-page business model","instructions":"Complete the simplified business model canvas and mark each box as evidence or assumption."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer challenge","instructions":"Present your model for two minutes and answer challenge questions from two peers; then swap roles."}],"examples":["Fresh Fold laundry pick-up for students in Nakuru.","A juice vendor (product sales), a phone repair shop (service), a tent and chair hire business (rental).","A Kisumu fish seller who reaches office workers through WhatsApp status and delivers by boda boda."],"tools":["Value proposition canvas and simplified business model canvas templates","Challenge question cards","Interview notes from homework"],"assessment":[{"question":"Write your value proposition statement."},{"question":"Name five boxes of the simplified business model canvas."},{"question":"Which business model type is a weekly laundry service, and why?"},{"question":"What two channels will bring your first customers, and how will you measure them?"},{"question":"Which box of your model is the biggest assumption?"}],"expected_outputs":["Value proposition canvas and one-sentence statement","One-page business model (simplified canvas) with evidence and assumptions marked","Peer challenge notes and a revised model"],"homework":["Show your value proposition statement to two potential customers and record their reactions.","List every cost you think your business will have (start-up and monthly) and find real prices for at least five of them."],"cross_track_notes":["The digital marketing topics approved for this session are taught here only at business-model level (which channels, what message, how to measure). The Digital Marketing track teaches the same channels in depth; this avoids repeating the full content."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and interview findings","steps":["Ask four learners to share one surprise from their interviews (a quote, a number or a behaviour).","Highlight learners whose interviews changed their idea - that is a success, not a failure."]},{"title":"Value propositions","steps":["Draw the value proposition canvas. Customer profile (circle) - jobs, pains, gains from interviews. Value map (square) - products and services, pain relievers (how you reduce pains), gain creators (how you create gains). Fit = your pain relievers and gain creators address the most important pains and gains.","Worked example - laundry pick-up for students. Pains - no space to dry clothes, rain, time lost walking to the laundry. Pain relievers - pick-up from the hostel, dried and folded within 24 hours. Gains - more free time, clean clothes for events. Gain creators - weekly subscription, M-Pesa payment, WhatsApp reminders.","Value proposition statement formula: 'For [target customer] who [need or problem], [business name] is a [category] that [key benefit]. Unlike [alternative], we [key difference].' Example - 'For students in Nakuru hostels who have no time or space to wash and dry clothes, Fresh Fold is a laundry pick-up service that returns clothes clean and folded within 24 hours. Unlike hand-washing or walking to town, we collect from your hostel and you pay by M-Pesa.'","Differentiation: why customers would choose you over the current alternative - faster, cheaper, closer, more reliable, more convenient, better quality."]},{"title":"Your value proposition","steps":["Learners fill the customer profile with evidence from their interviews (mark each item with the number of interviewees who mentioned it).","They fill the value map and check that each top pain and gain has a pain reliever or gain creator.","They write the one-sentence value proposition statement and read it to a partner, who says whether it is clear and believable."]},{"title":"Business models and the simplified canvas","steps":["Explain the simplified business model canvas - nine boxes: customers (who); value proposition (what); channels (how they find, buy and receive); customer relationships (how you keep them); revenue streams (how money comes in); key activities (what you must do); key resources (what you need - people, equipment, money, skills); key partners (who helps - suppliers, riders, landlords); costs (what you spend).","Fill the canvas for Fresh Fold as a worked example, box by box.","Business model types with Kenyan examples: product sales (a juice vendor); services (a mobile phone repair shop); subscriptions (weekly laundry, gym membership); commissions and marketplaces (platforms that connect sellers and buyers and take a fee); rental (renting out tents and chairs for events); freemium (free basic service, paid premium).","Ask learners which model type fits their idea and whether a different one could work better."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Reaching and keeping customers - marketing in the business model","steps":["Explain that channels and customer relationships are where marketing lives in a business model. For most young Kenyan businesses, the main channels are digital: social media (Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, LinkedIn), WhatsApp Business (catalogue, quick replies, status), content (photos, graphics in tools like Canva, captions with calls to action), and sometimes advertising and search (a Google Business Profile so people find you on Maps).","At the business-model level, decide three things: which two channels will bring your first customers, what one message will make them act, and how you will measure whether the channel works (enquiries, orders, cost per customer).","Explain that session 6 turns this into a one-page marketing plan, and that the Digital Marketing track teaches these channels in depth. Here the question is only whether the channels fit the customers and the costs in the model."]},{"title":"Map your one-page business model","steps":["Learners complete the simplified canvas for their idea on one page, using their value proposition and interview evidence.","In the channels box, they choose two first channels and how they will measure them.","They mark each box as 'evidence' (from interviews or data) or 'assumption'.","The facilitator circulates and asks 'how do you know?' about key boxes (revenue and channels especially)."]},{"title":"Peer challenge","steps":["In groups of three, one learner presents their model in 2 minutes; the other two act as challengers with question cards: 'Why would customers switch?', 'How will the first 10 customers find you?', 'What does one customer cost you?', 'What happens if your key partner lets you down?', 'Which box is your biggest assumption?' Rotate.","Each learner notes the two hardest questions and revises their model or adds the question to their testing list for session 7."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask learners to name the box in their model with the weakest evidence.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Value proposition canvas worked example","steps":["Complete the canvas and statement for Fresh Fold laundry pick-up live."]},{"title":"Business model canvas worked example","steps":["Fill all nine boxes for Fresh Fold, marking evidence and assumptions."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Write your value proposition statement.","guide":"Names the target customer, need, category, key benefit and difference from alternatives."},{"question":"Name five boxes of the simplified business model canvas.","guide":"Any five of customers, value proposition, channels, relationships, revenue streams, key activities, key resources, key partners, costs."},{"question":"Which business model type is a weekly laundry service, and why?","guide":"Subscription (recurring payment for ongoing service); could also be a service model."},{"question":"What two channels will bring your first customers, and how will you measure them?","guide":"Specific channels linked to the target customer and a measurable indicator."},{"question":"Which box of your model is the biggest assumption?","guide":"Honest identification, usually revenue, channels or willingness to pay."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the canvases, the Fresh Fold worked example and the challenge question cards.","Note that this session's curriculum record was approved by DIRA on 8 Nov 2026; deliver its approved activities (simplified business model canvas and peer challenge) and output (one-page business model) as written."],"delivery":["Keep marketing content at the business-model level; learners who want more should be pointed to the Digital Marketing track resources.","Make learners show evidence; praise honest 'assumption' labels."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Keep the peer challenge respectful - challenge the idea, never the person."],"after_session":["Review business models and note the biggest assumption for each learner to test in session 7.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 5,
   $cv13${"title":"Basic venture finance","description":"Identify start-up and operating costs, price, revenue and simple cash flow.","objectives":["Explain why business and personal money must be separated and how to keep simple records (including M-Pesa statements)","Describe the risks and costs of borrowing (digital loans, Hustler Fund, chamas, SACCOs, banks)","Classify start-up and operating costs, and fixed and variable costs","Calculate variable cost per unit, contribution per unit, gross profit and fully allocated unit cost, and set a price using cost-plus, competitor and value methods","Calculate break-even in units using contribution per unit","Explain the difference between profit and cash flow, and build a one-month cash-flow estimate","Outline a lean one-page business plan"],"activities":["Recap and cost hunt results","Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs","Costing","Pricing and break-even","Stretch break","Revenue, expenses and cash flow","Start-up budget and one-month cash flow","Lean one-page business plan","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"Start-up budget with researched prices; Variable and fully allocated unit cost, price, contribution per unit, gross profit and break-even calculation; One-month cash-flow estimate; Lean one-page business plan outline (started)","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 6 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners build basic financial literacy for running a small business - separating business and personal money, keeping records, understanding credit - and learn to cost a product, set a price, calculate profit and break-even, and plan cash flow. They produce a start-up budget, a one-month cash-flow estimate and a lean one-page business plan outline.","objectives":["Explain why business and personal money must be separated and how to keep simple records (including M-Pesa statements)","Describe the risks and costs of borrowing (digital loans, Hustler Fund, chamas, SACCOs, banks)","Classify start-up and operating costs, and fixed and variable costs","Calculate variable cost per unit, contribution per unit, gross profit and fully allocated unit cost, and set a price using cost-plus, competitor and value methods","Calculate break-even in units using contribution per unit","Explain the difference between profit and cash flow, and build a one-month cash-flow estimate","Outline a lean one-page business plan"],"topics":[{"title":"Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs","subtopics":["Separating business and personal money","Simple record keeping (sales book, expense book, M-Pesa statements)","Paying yourself a fixed amount","Savings and emergency funds","Credit - digital loans, the Hustler Fund, chamas, SACCOs, banks, interest and the risk of debt","Tax basics - KRA PIN and why records matter"]},{"title":"Pricing and costing","subtopics":["Start-up versus operating costs","Fixed versus variable costs","Variable cost per unit and fully allocated unit cost","Cost-plus pricing","Competitor-based pricing","Value-based pricing","Discounts and their effect on profit"]},{"title":"Revenue and expenses","subtopics":["Revenue (sales)","Cost of goods sold","Contribution per unit and contribution margin","Gross profit and net profit","Simple monthly profit statement","Break-even"]},{"title":"Cash-flow fundamentals","subtopics":["Profit is not cash","Timing of money in and out","Credit sales and stock","One-month cash-flow estimate","Keeping a cash buffer"]},{"title":"Business planning","subtopics":["Lean one-page business plan","What to include","When a full business plan is needed (loans, investors, competitions)"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and cost hunt results","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share the real costs you found."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the money habits that keep a small business alive."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Costing","kind":"lesson","summary":"Classify your costs and calculate the cost of one unit."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pricing and break-even","kind":"lesson","summary":"Set a price using three methods, then calculate contribution per unit and break-even."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Revenue, expenses and cash flow","kind":"lesson","summary":"Understand profit and cash flow and why they differ."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Start-up budget and one-month cash flow","kind":"exercise","summary":"Build your start-up budget, unit cost, price, break-even and one-month cash-flow estimate."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Lean one-page business plan","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the outline of a lean business plan and when a full plan is needed."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Cost sorting","instructions":"Sort your costs into start-up or operating and fixed or variable."},{"title":"Discount effect","instructions":"Calculate how a 10% discount changes your contribution per unit and your break-even."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Finance template","instructions":"Complete the start-up budget, variable and fully allocated unit cost, price, contribution per unit, break-even, monthly gross and net profit and one-month cash flow, highlighting guesses."},{"title":"One-page plan outline","instructions":"Start the lean one-page business plan with what you have from sessions 3 to 5."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Number check","instructions":"Swap templates with a partner and check one calculation each."}],"examples":["A juice business with a KES 50 variable cost per bottle, a fully allocated cost of about KES 63 at 300 bottles a month, and a KES 80 price.","A Kericho mama mboga who separated her M-Pesa line and found she was losing money on transport.","A Nairobi phone repair technician who ran out of cash because customers collected phones on credit.","Comparing a digital loan with a chama loan for buying stock."],"tools":["Finance template (Google Sheets or paper) and calculator","Cost lists and price research from homework","Lean one-page business plan template"],"assessment":[{"question":"Classify rent and packaging as fixed or variable costs."},{"question":"Your variable cost per unit is KES 120, your fully allocated unit cost at expected sales is KES 150, and competitors charge KES 180 to 220. Suggest a price and explain."},{"question":"Fixed costs are KES 6,000 a month and contribution per unit is KES 40. What is break-even?"},{"question":"Variable cost per unit is KES 50, fixed costs are KES 4,000 a month and you expect to sell 200 units at KES 90. Work out the contribution per unit and the fully allocated unit cost."},{"question":"Why can a profitable business run out of cash?"},{"question":"Name two questions to ask before taking a loan."}],"expected_outputs":["Start-up budget with researched prices","Variable and fully allocated unit cost, price, contribution per unit, gross profit and break-even calculation","One-month cash-flow estimate","Lean one-page business plan outline (started)"],"homework":["Replace at least three guesses in your finance template with researched numbers.","Show your price to three potential customers and record their reactions."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing budgets only a campaign and its cost per result. Entrepreneurship & Business budgets the whole business - start-up costs, pricing, profit and cash flow."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and cost hunt results","steps":["Ask learners to share one cost they found that surprised them (rent, permits, packaging, transport).","Explain that many small businesses in Kenya fail not because nobody buys, but because they do not know their numbers. Today is about numbers."]},{"title":"Basic financial literacy for entrepreneurs","steps":["Separate business and personal money: use a separate M-Pesa line, a till number or a separate account for the business. Mixing them makes it impossible to know if the business makes money.","Records: write every sale and expense daily in a book or simple sheet; download M-Pesa statements monthly. Records help you make decisions, apply for loans and handle tax.","Pay yourself a fixed amount instead of taking money from the till whenever you need it.","Savings: set aside a little each week for emergencies and stock.","Credit: digital loans are quick but can be expensive and can lead to a cycle of borrowing; late repayment can affect your credit record with credit reference bureaus. Government funds such as the Hustler Fund, chamas and SACCOs are alternatives with different terms. Before borrowing, ask - what exactly will this money buy, how will it bring more income, and what is the total amount I will repay?","Tax basics: registering for a KRA PIN is required for many formal activities; keeping records makes tax obligations easier to meet. Check current requirements on the official KRA website."]},{"title":"Costing","steps":["Start-up costs (once, before opening): equipment, first stock, registration and permits, initial branding. Operating costs (every month): rent, stock, transport, data, wages, packaging.","Fixed costs (same every month regardless of sales): rent, a stall fee, a salary. Variable costs (change with each unit sold): ingredients, packaging, delivery per order.","Variable cost per unit - a juice business. Per 500 ml bottle: fruit KES 25, sugar and water KES 5, bottle and label KES 15, fruit transport share KES 5. Variable cost per bottle = KES 50 (these are also the direct costs of making the product). Fixed costs per month: stall fee KES 3,000, blender depreciation KES 500, data KES 500 = KES 4,000.","Fully allocated unit cost = variable cost per unit + (fixed costs for the month / units sold in the month). At 300 bottles a month: 50 + 4,000 / 300 = 50 + 13.33 = about KES 63 per bottle. At only 150 bottles it rises to 50 + 26.67 = about KES 77: fixed costs spread over fewer units, so each costs more.","Learners classify their own cost list into start-up or operating and fixed or variable."]},{"title":"Pricing and break-even","steps":["Cost-plus pricing: add a markup to cost. A 60% markup on the variable cost gives KES 50 + 30 = KES 80. Check it against the fully allocated unit cost at realistic sales (KES 63 at 300 bottles): KES 80 leaves about KES 17 per bottle after all costs.","Competitor-based pricing: what do similar juice sellers charge nearby? If they charge KES 70 to 100, KES 80 fits.","Value-based pricing: what is it worth to the customer? Fresh juice delivered to offices may be worth KES 100.","Two floors for any price: never below the variable cost per unit (every sale would lose money), and over the month the price must be above the fully allocated unit cost at your realistic sales volume, or the business makes a loss. Then check it fits the market and reflects the value.","Contribution per unit = price - variable cost per unit = KES 80 - 50 = KES 30. It is what each sale contributes towards fixed costs and then profit. Contribution margin = 30 / 80 = 37.5% of the price (a 60% markup on cost is a 37.5% margin on price - markup and margin are not the same).","Break-even (units) = fixed costs / contribution per unit = 4,000 / 30 = 133.3, so 134 bottles a month (round up) - about 5.2 a day over 26 trading days, so plan for at least 6. Check: at break-even the fully allocated unit cost (50 + 4,000 / 134 = about KES 80) equals the price.","Discounts: a 10% discount makes the price KES 72, so contribution falls from KES 30 to KES 22 per bottle - a 27% drop - and break-even rises from 134 to 182 bottles (4,000 / 22 = 181.8). Use discounts carefully."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Revenue, expenses and cash flow","steps":["Simple monthly profit at 300 bottles: revenue 300 x KES 80 = KES 24,000; cost of goods sold (the direct costs of the bottles sold) 300 x KES 50 = KES 15,000; gross profit = revenue - cost of goods sold = KES 9,000; minus fixed costs KES 4,000 = net profit KES 5,000 (before tax). Here every variable cost is a direct product cost, so gross profit equals total contribution (300 x KES 30). If a business also has variable selling costs, such as a delivery rider paid per order or an agent's commission, those reduce contribution but not gross profit, and the two figures differ. Check: fully allocated cost 300 x KES 63.33 = KES 19,000, and 24,000 - 19,000 = KES 5,000.","Formula card for the finance template: variable cost per unit = sum of the costs of one unit; contribution per unit = price - variable cost per unit; contribution margin = contribution per unit / price; gross profit = revenue - cost of goods sold; net profit = gross profit - fixed (and other operating) costs; fully allocated unit cost = variable cost per unit + fixed costs / units; break-even units = fixed costs / contribution per unit, rounded up.","Cash flow is the timing of money in and out. A business can be profitable on paper and still run out of cash - for example if an office pays at month-end but you must buy fruit every morning.","Show a one-month cash-flow table by week: opening cash, cash in (sales paid), cash out (stock, rent, transport), closing cash. Show a week where closing cash goes negative and discuss solutions (smaller stock purchases, deposits from customers, shorter credit, a cash buffer).","Credit sales and stock both tie up cash; record who owes you and follow up politely."]},{"title":"Start-up budget and one-month cash flow","steps":["Learners complete the finance template (Google Sheets or paper): start-up budget with real prices where possible; variable cost per unit and fully allocated unit cost at their expected sales; price with a reason (cost-plus, competitor and value checked); contribution per unit; break-even units per month; gross and net profit for the month; one-month cash flow by week with realistic sales assumptions.","Learners highlight every number that is a guess rather than a researched price.","The facilitator circulates, checking arithmetic and asking 'where did this number come from?'.","Learners swap with a partner who checks one calculation."]},{"title":"Lean one-page business plan","steps":["Explain the lean one-page plan: problem, customer, value proposition, solution, channels, revenue and price, costs, key numbers (break-even, monthly target), team, next milestones and what you need (money, partners, skills).","A full business plan is usually needed when applying for loans, grants, investors or competitions; the one-page plan is the starting point.","Learners start their one-page plan outline, filling what they already have from sessions 3 to 5."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask learners to share their break-even number and whether it feels achievable.","Set the homework."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Juice business numbers","steps":["Walk through variable cost per unit, fully allocated unit cost, pricing, contribution per unit, break-even, gross and net profit, and a cash-flow table for the juice example, using the formula card."]},{"title":"Cash runs out","steps":["Show a profitable month that still has a negative cash week, and two ways to fix it."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Classify rent and packaging as fixed or variable costs.","guide":"Rent is fixed; packaging is variable."},{"question":"Your variable cost per unit is KES 120, your fully allocated unit cost at expected sales is KES 150, and competitors charge KES 180 to 220. Suggest a price and explain.","guide":"A price above KES 150 (for example KES 190-200), justified by value or difference and checked against competitors; never below KES 120, and above KES 150 to make a profit at the expected volume."},{"question":"Fixed costs are KES 6,000 a month and contribution per unit is KES 40. What is break-even?","guide":"6,000 / 40 = 150 units a month."},{"question":"Variable cost per unit is KES 50, fixed costs are KES 4,000 a month and you expect to sell 200 units at KES 90. Work out the contribution per unit and the fully allocated unit cost.","guide":"Contribution = 90 - 50 = KES 40; fully allocated unit cost = 50 + 4,000 / 200 = KES 70."},{"question":"Why can a profitable business run out of cash?","guide":"Timing - money goes out before it comes in (stock, credit sales, irregular payments)."},{"question":"Name two questions to ask before taking a loan.","guide":"What it will buy and how it brings income; total repayment and ability to repay; alternatives."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the finance template in Google Sheets with formulas and a paper version, and the juice worked example.","Check current official information on KRA PIN registration and government youth funds if you plan to mention specifics; do not give financial advice beyond the basics."],"delivery":["Go slowly on arithmetic; some learners fear numbers. Use the same example throughout.","Praise honest 'guess' labels and push for researched prices."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Money and debt can be sensitive; never ask learners about personal debts publicly.","If a learner discloses serious financial exploitation or pressure, follow up privately and use the DIRA LEARN support routes.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Check finance templates for errors and give feedback.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 6,
   $cv13${"title":"Marketing and customer experience","description":"Choose channels, create marketing messages and practise ethical selling.","objectives":["Describe a simple brand for a small business (name, promise, look) and position it against alternatives","Build a one-page marketing plan (target customer, message, channels, offer, budget, measures)","Map the customer journey and design service standards for key moments","Handle a customer complaint professionally","Use ethical sales techniques - needs questions, benefits, handling objections, closing","Negotiate with a supplier or customer using preparation, options and a walk-away point"],"activities":["Recap and price reactions","Branding and marketing a small business","One-page marketing plan","Customer service and customer experience","Stretch break","Customer journey map and complaint role-play","Sales and negotiation","Sales and negotiation role-play and sales script","Wrap-up and homework"],"expected_output":"One-page marketing plan; Customer journey map with service standards; Sales script","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 5 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners plan how to brand and market their small business on a small budget, design a customer experience that brings people back and handle complaints well, and practise ethical sales conversations and negotiation with customers and suppliers. They produce a one-page marketing plan, a customer journey map and a sales script.","objectives":["Describe a simple brand for a small business (name, promise, look) and position it against alternatives","Build a one-page marketing plan (target customer, message, channels, offer, budget, measures)","Map the customer journey and design service standards for key moments","Handle a customer complaint professionally","Use ethical sales techniques - needs questions, benefits, handling objections, closing","Negotiate with a supplier or customer using preparation, options and a walk-away point"],"topics":[{"title":"Branding and marketing a small business","subtopics":["Simple brand - name, promise, look","Positioning against alternatives","Product, price, place and promotion","Low-cost marketing - word of mouth, referrals, community, WhatsApp, social media","One-page marketing plan"]},{"title":"Customer service and customer experience","subtopics":["Customer journey - before, during, after purchase","Moments of truth","Service standards","Handling complaints (listen, apologise, fix, follow up)","Repeat customers and referrals"]},{"title":"Sales and negotiation","subtopics":["Ethical selling","Needs questions","Features and benefits","Handling objections (price, trust, timing)","Closing and asking for the sale","Negotiation preparation - goal, options, walk-away point","Win-win with suppliers and customers"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and price reactions","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share how customers reacted to your price."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Branding and marketing a small business","kind":"lesson","summary":"Plan a simple brand and low-cost marketing for your business."},{"minutes":15,"title":"One-page marketing plan","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write the one-page marketing plan for your business."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Customer service and customer experience","kind":"lesson","summary":"Design the experience that turns first-time buyers into repeat customers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Customer journey map and complaint role-play","kind":"exercise","summary":"Map your customer journey and practise handling a complaint."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Sales and negotiation","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn ethical selling and basic negotiation with customers and suppliers."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Sales and negotiation role-play and sales script","kind":"group","summary":"Practise a sales conversation and a supplier negotiation, then write your sales script."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Wrap-up and homework","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Summarise and confirm the homework."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Positioning sentence","instructions":"Complete 'Customers choose us instead of [alternative] because [difference].'"}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"One-page marketing plan","instructions":"Write your target customer, key message, channels, first-customer offer, budget and measures."},{"title":"Customer journey map","instructions":"Map before, during and after purchase, mark two moments of truth and write a service standard for each."},{"title":"Sales script","instructions":"Write an opening, needs questions, benefits, objection answers and a closing question."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Complaint role-play","instructions":"In pairs, handle a complaint using Listen, Apologise, Fix, Follow up."},{"title":"Sales and negotiation role-plays","instructions":"In threes, practise a sales conversation and a supplier negotiation with an observer giving feedback."}],"examples":["A Wakulima market fruit supplier negotiation for a juice business.","A Thika salon that sends a thank-you WhatsApp message and a loyalty stamp after each visit.","A Kisii shoe repairer who turned a complaint into a loyal customer by fixing the shoe for free and following up."],"tools":["One-page marketing plan, customer journey map and sales script templates","Objection cards and negotiation cards"],"assessment":[{"question":"Write your positioning sentence."},{"question":"What are the four steps for handling a complaint?"},{"question":"A customer says 'It is too expensive.' Give an ethical response."},{"question":"What is a walk-away point in negotiation?"},{"question":"Name two moments of truth in your customer journey."}],"expected_outputs":["One-page marketing plan","Customer journey map with service standards","Sales script"],"homework":["Use your sales script with at least two real potential customers and note what happened.","Choose the riskiest assumption from your business model to test in session 7."],"cross_track_notes":["Digital Marketing teaches the online channels, copy and campaign measurement in depth. Here learners decide the overall marketing approach for the business, design the customer experience and practise face-to-face and phone selling and negotiation."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and price reactions","steps":["Learners share customer reactions to their price. Discuss what they will change (price, offer, target customer).","Introduce today - getting customers, keeping them and selling honestly."]},{"title":"Branding and marketing a small business","steps":["Simple brand: a memorable name, a promise customers can rely on ('Fresh juice, made this morning'), and a consistent look (colours, logo, clean stall or packaging).","Positioning: complete 'Customers choose us instead of [alternative] because [difference].'","The 4Ps in plain words: Product (what exactly you sell), Price (from session 5), Place (where and how customers buy - stall, delivery, online), Promotion (how they hear about you).","Low-cost marketing for Kenyan small businesses: word of mouth and referrals (offer a reward for bringing a friend), community presence (chamas, churches, mosques, estate groups - with permission), a WhatsApp Business catalogue and status, one main social platform, signage and samples. The Digital Marketing track covers these channels in depth; here learners choose what fits their business and budget.","One-page marketing plan: target customer, key message, channels (two or three), offer for the first customers, budget, and measures (enquiries, sales, repeat customers)."]},{"title":"One-page marketing plan","steps":["Learners complete the one-page marketing plan using their business model and finance numbers.","Partners check that the marketing budget fits the finance template and that the channels match the target customer."]},{"title":"Customer service and customer experience","steps":["Customer journey: before (hearing about you, asking questions), during (ordering, paying, receiving), after (using the product, follow-up, complaints, coming back). Draw it for the juice business.","Moments of truth: the points that decide whether customers return - the first reply on WhatsApp, the delivery time, the taste, how a complaint is handled.","Service standards: simple promises you always keep, for example 'reply within 15 minutes during opening hours', 'deliver within an hour in town', 'greet every customer by name if known'.","Complaints: Listen without interrupting, Apologise sincerely, Fix it (replace, refund or compensate fairly), Follow up. A well-handled complaint often creates a loyal customer.","Repeat customers and referrals: a thank-you message, a loyalty card (paper or WhatsApp stamps), asking happy customers to tell a friend."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Customer journey map and complaint role-play","steps":["Learners map their customer journey (before, during, after), mark two moments of truth and write a service standard for each.","In pairs, role-play a complaint (a late delivery, a wrong item, a rude staff member) using Listen, Apologise, Fix, Follow up. Swap roles."]},{"title":"Sales and negotiation","steps":["Ethical selling: help the customer solve their problem; never lie, pressure or hide costs. Trust is the most valuable asset of a small business.","Sales conversation: greet; ask needs questions ('What is it for?', 'When do you need it?', 'What have you tried?'); recommend and explain benefits; handle objections; ask for the sale ('Shall I put you down for Saturday delivery?').","Handling objections: price ('It is expensive' - explain value, offer a smaller option, never insult); trust ('How do I know it is good?' - samples, reviews, guarantee); timing ('Maybe later' - ask what would help them decide, follow up once).","Negotiation preparation: your goal, your options, your walk-away point (the worst deal you will accept), and what the other side needs. Look for win-win - a supplier may give a better price for regular orders or cash payment; a customer may accept a later delivery for a lower price.","Example: negotiating with a fruit supplier at Wakulima market - buying in bulk twice a week for a 10% lower price in return for reliable payment."]},{"title":"Sales and negotiation role-play and sales script","steps":["In groups of three (seller, customer or supplier, observer), run two role-plays: a sales conversation with an objection card, and a supplier negotiation with a goal and walk-away card. The observer gives feedback.","Each learner writes a short sales script for their business - opening, three needs questions, key benefits, answers to the two most likely objections and a closing question."]},{"title":"Wrap-up and homework","steps":["Ask learners to share the objection they find hardest to handle.","Set the homework and explain that session 7 is about testing the idea with real customers and preparing the pitch."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Complaint handling","steps":["Role-play a poor and a good response to a late-delivery complaint using Listen, Apologise, Fix, Follow up."]},{"title":"Sales conversation","steps":["Role-play a sales conversation with a price objection, showing needs questions and an honest close."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Write your positioning sentence.","guide":"Names a real alternative and a believable difference."},{"question":"What are the four steps for handling a complaint?","guide":"Listen, Apologise, Fix, Follow up."},{"question":"A customer says 'It is too expensive.' Give an ethical response.","guide":"Explain value, offer a smaller or different option, never pressure or insult."},{"question":"What is a walk-away point in negotiation?","guide":"The worst deal you will accept; below it you walk away."},{"question":"Name two moments of truth in your customer journey.","guide":"Specific moments that decide whether customers return."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the templates and the objection and negotiation cards."],"delivery":["Role-plays are the heart of this session; keep lessons short and practice long.","Point learners who want deeper digital marketing to the Digital Marketing track resources."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Keep role-plays respectful; no shaming of 'customers' or 'sellers'.","Remind learners that pressure selling and hidden charges harm customers and the business's reputation."],"after_session":["Review the marketing plans and sales scripts and give feedback.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 7,
   $cv13${"title":"Test and pitch","description":"Use feedback to improve an idea and present a concise business pitch.","objectives":["Design a test of their riskiest assumption (pre-orders, a pilot, a sample sale, a landing page or WhatsApp catalogue test)","Define what result would support or reject the assumption before testing","Interpret test evidence and decide to continue, change or stop","Complete a lean one-page business plan including legal basics and funding options as next steps","Build a 3-minute pitch (problem, solution, customer, evidence, model, money, team, ask)","Give and use peer feedback to improve the pitch"],"activities":["Recap and sales script results","Testing business ideas","Design your test","Completing the lean business plan","Stretch break","Pitch structure","Build your pitch","Peer pitch review"],"expected_output":"Test card (and results if already tested); Completed lean one-page business plan; Three-minute pitch with up to five slides or a one-page visual; Peer review notes and revised assumptions","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 4 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners design and run a small, cheap test of their riskiest assumption, decide what the evidence means, complete their lean business plan with legal and funding next steps, and build a 3-minute pitch backed by evidence. They review each other's pitches and revise their assumptions.","objectives":["Design a test of their riskiest assumption (pre-orders, a pilot, a sample sale, a landing page or WhatsApp catalogue test)","Define what result would support or reject the assumption before testing","Interpret test evidence and decide to continue, change or stop","Complete a lean one-page business plan including legal basics and funding options as next steps","Build a 3-minute pitch (problem, solution, customer, evidence, model, money, team, ask)","Give and use peer feedback to improve the pitch"],"topics":[{"title":"Testing business ideas","subtopics":["Minimum viable product","Test types - pre-orders, pilots, sample sales, smoke tests","Setting a success criterion before testing","Reading results honestly","Continue, change or stop"]},{"title":"Business planning (completing the lean plan)","subtopics":["Lean one-page plan","Legal basics - business name registration on eCitizen, KRA PIN, county permits (check current requirements)","Funding options - savings, family, chamas, SACCOs, youth and government funds, grants, competitions","Milestones"]},{"title":"Pitching and presenting business ideas","subtopics":["Pitch structure","Telling a story with evidence","Numbers that matter","The ask","Delivery - voice, timing, confidence"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Recap and sales script results","kind":"introduction","summary":"Share what happened when you used your sales script."},{"minutes":20,"title":"Testing business ideas","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn how to test an idea quickly and cheaply before investing."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Design your test","kind":"exercise","summary":"Design a test of your riskiest assumption with a clear success criterion."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Completing the lean business plan","kind":"lesson","summary":"Add legal basics, funding options and milestones to your one-page plan."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pitch structure","kind":"lesson","summary":"Learn the structure of a short business pitch."},{"minutes":25,"title":"Build your pitch","kind":"exercise","summary":"Build your 3-minute pitch with your evidence and numbers."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Peer pitch review","kind":"group","summary":"Pitch to peers and use their feedback to improve."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Success criterion","instructions":"Write a success criterion with a number for your riskiest assumption before testing."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Test card","instructions":"Design a cheap, quick, ethical test of your riskiest assumption with a clear success criterion."},{"title":"Lean one-page plan","instructions":"Complete the plan including legal next steps, funding options and three milestones."},{"title":"Three-minute pitch","instructions":"Build your pitch with real evidence, key numbers and a specific ask."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer pitch review","instructions":"Pitch to two peers who use the rubric to give two strengths and two improvements."}],"examples":["Pre-orders from 20 office workers for a juice delivery test.","A Kisii uniform exchange running a one-day pilot before school opening.","A Kitui solar lamp rental idea tested with five households for one week."],"tools":["Test card, lean plan and pitch templates, pitch rubric, timer","Business model, finance template and interview notes from earlier sessions"],"assessment":[{"question":"What is a minimum viable product?"},{"question":"Why set a success criterion before testing?"},{"question":"Name three parts of a business pitch."},{"question":"What should you do with pre-order money if you decide to stop?"}],"expected_outputs":["Test card (and results if already tested)","Completed lean one-page business plan","Three-minute pitch with up to five slides or a one-page visual","Peer review notes and revised assumptions"],"homework":["Run your test this week and record the result and your decision (continue, change or stop). Keep tests honest: do not take money for something you cannot deliver, and do not share customers' details.","Add the result to your pitch and practise it three times within 3 minutes.","Upload your business concept (opportunity statement, business model, finance template, marketing plan, lean plan) to DIRA LEARN.","If you are presenting by recording (a large group, or you cannot present live), record your 3-minute pitch presentation - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - and upload it to DIRA LEARN by the day before session 8."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track builds its final project in session 7. The Entrepreneurship & Business project is judged on evidence, sound numbers and a clear pitch, not on how big the idea is."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Recap and sales script results","steps":["Learners share what happened when they used their sales script with real customers.","Introduce the idea of testing - evidence beats opinions, including your own."]},{"title":"Testing business ideas","steps":["Minimum viable product (MVP): the simplest version of your offer that lets you learn whether customers really want it - for example ten bottles of juice sold at an office, not a whole juice bar.","Test types: pre-orders (take orders and deposits before producing); pilots (serve five customers for a week); sample sales (sell a small batch at a market day); smoke tests (post the offer on WhatsApp status or a catalogue and count real enquiries before you have stock - be honest that it is a pre-launch).","Success criterion before testing: 'If at least 8 out of 20 office workers pre-order at KES 80, the willingness-to-pay assumption is supported.' Decide before you look at results so you do not fool yourself.","Reading results honestly and deciding: continue (evidence supports), change (pivot the customer, price or offer), or stop (save your money and time). Changing or stopping is learning, not failure.","Ethics: never take money for something you cannot deliver; refund pre-orders if you stop."]},{"title":"Design your test","steps":["Learners complete the test card: riskiest assumption, test type, what they will do, who with, when, cost, success criterion, and how they will record results.","Partners check that the test is cheap, quick (within one week), ethical and has a clear number as the success criterion.","Learners who already ran a test (homework) record the results and decision instead."]},{"title":"Completing the lean business plan","steps":["Legal basics as next steps (not required to test): business name registration through the eCitizen Business Registration Service, a KRA PIN, county single business permits, and any sector licences (food handling for food businesses). Requirements and fees change - check the official websites.","Funding options: own savings, family and friends (agree terms in writing), chamas and SACCOs, government youth and enterprise funds, grants and youth business competitions, and later investors. Match the funding to the need and repay what you borrow.","Milestones: the next three steps with dates (for example 'first 20 paying customers by end of January').","Learners finish their one-page plan."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Pitch structure","steps":["Pitch structure for 3 minutes: (1) hook - a story or a striking fact about the problem (20 seconds); (2) problem and customer (30 seconds); (3) solution and value proposition (30 seconds); (4) evidence - interviews, test results, pre-orders (30 seconds); (5) business model and key numbers - price, variable and fully allocated unit cost, contribution per unit, break-even (30 seconds); (6) team - you and why you (15 seconds); (7) the ask and next step (25 seconds).","The ask: what you want from the audience - advice, a first customer, a partner, a space, a small grant. Make it specific.","Delivery: stand or sit tall, speak slowly, look at the audience or camera, use one simple visual or slide per part, practise with a timer.","Show a 3-minute example pitch for Fresh Fold (recorded or live by the facilitator)."]},{"title":"Build your pitch","steps":["Learners write their pitch using the template and create up to five simple slides or a one-page visual.","They include at least one piece of real evidence (interview quotes, numbers, test results) and their key numbers from session 5.","They practise once alone with a timer."]},{"title":"Peer pitch review","steps":["In groups of three, each learner pitches in 3 minutes. Peers use the pitch rubric (clear problem, credible solution, real evidence, sensible numbers, specific ask, delivery) and give two strengths and two improvements.","Learners revise their assumptions list and pitch based on the feedback."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Example pitch","steps":["Deliver or play a 3-minute Fresh Fold pitch and point out each part of the structure."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"What is a minimum viable product?","guide":"The simplest version of an offer that lets you learn whether customers want it."},{"question":"Why set a success criterion before testing?","guide":"To avoid interpreting results to fit what you hoped."},{"question":"Name three parts of a business pitch.","guide":"Any three of hook, problem, solution, evidence, model and numbers, team, ask."},{"question":"What should you do with pre-order money if you decide to stop?","guide":"Refund it."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Prepare the test card, lean plan and pitch templates, the pitch rubric and the example pitch.","Check current official information on business registration and permits if you plan to mention specifics.","Set the session 8 room plan from the track group size (up to 12 - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus a recorded-questions room) and line up co-facilitators and peer panels."],"delivery":["Keep pushing for evidence; a pitch without evidence is a wish.","Learners whose test showed the idea does not work should be praised and helped to pitch what they learned and their changed idea."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Tests must be ethical - no taking money for things learners cannot deliver; refunds if they stop.","Learners must not borrow money to run a test.","Consent and privacy: before using an identifiable person (face, voice or name), a photograph of someone, or a real business's name, logo, prices, customer details or results, the learner gets permission from that person or owner - in writing (a WhatsApp message is enough) for anything shown outside the class. Never children. Learners who cannot or do not wish to publish publicly use the private or simulated option, which is assessed with the same rubric and never marked down."],"after_session":["Check every learner has a pitch draft and a test plan; message those at risk.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb),
  ('entrepreneurship', 8,
   $cv13${"title":"Business pitch showcase","description":"Present an early-stage business concept and identify a realistic validation step.","objectives":["Deliver a 3-minute business pitch with evidence and a specific ask","Answer questions about customers, numbers and assumptions","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves as entrepreneurs and as employable young people using the project as proof","Write a 30-day validation action plan"],"activities":["Showcase set-up and expectations","Pitches and questions - block 1","Stretch break","Pitches and questions - block 2","Feedback round","Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","30-day validation action plan","Celebration and close"],"expected_output":"Delivered business pitch (final project) with rubric score and feedback; Business concept summary (submitted in DIRA LEARN); 30-day validation action plan; Written feedback given to two peers","assessment_notes":"DRAFT (V13.1 curriculum revision, 19 Nov 2026): full lesson plan with timed segments, activities, assessment and facilitator notes. Assessment: 3 questions or tasks, checked against the expected outputs. Needs approval by programme management."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","purpose":"Learners pitch their early-stage business concept with evidence, answer questions about their numbers and assumptions, give and receive feedback, learn to present themselves as entrepreneurs and job candidates, and leave with a 30-day validation action plan.","objectives":["Deliver a 3-minute business pitch with evidence and a specific ask","Answer questions about customers, numbers and assumptions","Give and receive feedback professionally using the rubric","Pitch themselves as entrepreneurs and as employable young people using the project as proof","Write a 30-day validation action plan"],"topics":[{"title":"Pitching and presenting business ideas","subtopics":["Delivering the pitch","Answering questions","Handling tough questions calmly"]},{"title":"Pitching yourself","subtopics":["Entrepreneur self-pitch","Using the project in job applications - skills employers value"]},{"title":"Professional behaviour and workplace readiness","subtopics":["Showcase etiquette","Receiving critical feedback","Following up with guests and potential partners","Pathways - starting, joining a business, employment, further learning"]},{"title":"Next steps","subtopics":["30-day validation plan","Support and networks for young entrepreneurs"]}],"segments":[{"minutes":10,"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","kind":"introduction","summary":"Learn the showcase order, rubric and etiquette."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Pitches and questions - block 1","kind":"assessment","summary":"The first six presenters in each room pitch and answer questions; give rubric feedback to your two assigned peers."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Stretch break","kind":"break","summary":"A five-minute stretch and movement break (seated option available)."},{"minutes":30,"title":"Pitches and questions - block 2","kind":"assessment","summary":"The next six presenters in each room pitch; recorded presenters answer their live questions."},{"minutes":10,"title":"Feedback round","kind":"group","summary":"Share feedback and learn from patterns across pitches."},{"minutes":15,"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","kind":"lesson","summary":"Use your business project to pitch yourself as an entrepreneur or job candidate."},{"minutes":15,"title":"30-day validation action plan","kind":"exercise","summary":"Write your next realistic validation step and a 30-day plan."},{"minutes":5,"title":"Celebration and close","kind":"conclusion","summary":"Celebrate and learn what happens next."}],"guided_exercises":[{"title":"Situation, action, result","instructions":"Describe your business project in interview style using situation, action and result."}],"individual_activities":[{"title":"Business pitch","instructions":"Deliver your 3-minute pitch with evidence and a specific ask, and answer questions for two minutes."},{"title":"30-day validation plan","instructions":"Complete the plan with the next validation step, weekly actions, measure, partner and risk."}],"group_activities":[{"title":"Peer feedback","instructions":"Give rubric-based written feedback to two assigned presenters."},{"title":"Self-pitch practice","instructions":"Practise your entrepreneur self-pitch in pairs."}],"examples":["A Kitui solar lamp rental pitch with a five-household test.","A Nakuru laundry pick-up pitch with 12 pre-orders from hostel students.","A learner who stopped a juice idea after testing and pitched a changed idea for office snack delivery."],"tools":["Learners' pitches (slides or one-page visuals), pitch rubric, feedback cards or DIRA LEARN","Timer and 30-day validation plan template"],"assessment":[{"question":"Pitch scored with the business pitch rubric."},{"question":"What is your next validation step and how will you measure it?"},{"question":"Describe your project using situation, action and result."}],"expected_outputs":["Delivered business pitch (final project) with rubric score and feedback","Business concept summary (submitted in DIRA LEARN)","30-day validation action plan","Written feedback given to two peers"],"homework":["Submit the pitch, business concept and 30-day validation plan in DIRA LEARN.","Send thank-you messages to the customers and guests who helped."],"cross_track_notes":["Every track ends with the same pitch-yourself and workplace-readiness segment; the Entrepreneurship & Business rubric weights evidence and numbers."],"track_project":"A business concept and pitch for an early-stage idea. It includes an opportunity statement backed by customer interviews, a value proposition, a one-page business model, a start-up budget and one-month cash-flow estimate with pricing, a one-page marketing and customer-experience plan, evidence from at least one real test, a 3-minute pitch in session 8 and a 30-day validation action. Privacy option: the business can be a simulated idea, and customer evidence is shown in the pitch without names, photos or contact details unless the person agreed."}$cv13$::jsonb,
   $cv13${"version":"V13.1","segments":[{"title":"Showcase set-up and expectations","steps":["Welcome learners and guests (local entrepreneurs, DIRA staff, mentors, potential partners). Thank interviewees and test customers.","Share the rubric: problem and customer (20%), solution and value proposition (15%), evidence from testing (25%), business model and numbers (20%), pitch delivery and answers (20%).","Assign each learner two presenters to give written feedback to."]},{"title":"Pitches and questions - block 1","steps":["Run the pitches with the session 8 room plan in the facilitator instructions: the first six pitchers in each room go in this block, each with 3 minutes plus 2 minutes of questions.","Learners pitch in order, 3 minutes each plus 2 minutes of questions. Strict timer with a 30-second warning.","Ask at least one question per presenter, such as: 'How many customers have actually paid?', 'What is your break-even and how long will it take to reach?', 'What did your test tell you?', 'What would you do with KES 10,000?', 'What is your biggest risk?'","Score with the rubric and note one strength and one improvement."]},{"title":"Stretch break","steps":["Invite everyone to stand, stretch, walk or drink water for five minutes; offer a seated option for anyone who prefers or needs it.","Restart on time with a one-line reminder of where the session is and what comes next."]},{"title":"Pitches and questions - block 2","steps":["The remaining pitchers in each live room (7 to 12) go now, with the same timer, rubric and question approach as block 1.","In a recorded-questions room, do not replay the recordings (the panel scored them before the session); give each recorded presenter 2 minutes of live questions about their business pitch.","Each room's panel hands its completed business pitch rubric sheets to the room facilitator before the feedback round."]},{"title":"Feedback round","steps":["Learners give written feedback to their two assigned presenters.","Facilitator highlights patterns in strong pitches - real evidence, honest numbers, a clear ask.","Model receiving tough feedback calmly - thank, clarify, decide."]},{"title":"Pitching yourself and workplace readiness","steps":["Entrepreneur self-pitch: 'I am Kevin from Kitui. I test ideas with real customers before spending money. I tested a solar lamp rental with five households; four paid on time and two referred neighbours. I am looking for a partner with a motorbike to expand deliveries.'","Employability: the project shows skills employers want - research, numeracy, customer service, sales, presenting and taking initiative. Practise describing it in an interview using situation, action and result.","Pathways: starting the business in small steps, joining a small business, employment while building the business on the side, further training; support networks such as county enterprise offices, youth business programmes and DIRA alumni. Beware of 'business opportunities' that ask for joining fees or promise quick returns (pyramid schemes).","Professional follow-up: thank guests and test customers within two days; follow up on any offer of help with a short, specific message.","Learners practise their self-pitch once in pairs."]},{"title":"30-day validation action plan","steps":["Learners complete the 30-day validation plan: the main assumption still to test, the next validation step (for example 'get 20 paying customers', 'run a two-week pilot'), four weekly actions, a measure of success, an accountability partner, one risk and a plan.","Learners share their next validation step with their accountability partner."]},{"title":"Celebration and close","steps":["Celebrate each learner's pitch in one sentence.","Explain how to submit the pitch deck, concept summary and 30-day validation action in DIRA LEARN, how results and certificates follow the programme's rules, and how alumni can connect with mentors and youth enterprise funding information."]}],"demonstrations":[{"title":"Model pitch","steps":["Before the session, share the recorded Fresh Fold example pitch as a reference. Do not use showcase time for it."]}],"assessment_guides":[{"question":"Pitch scored with the business pitch rubric.","guide":"Use rubric descriptors; evidence and honest numbers weigh most."},{"question":"What is your next validation step and how will you measure it?","guide":"A specific, realistic step with a number."},{"question":"Describe your project using situation, action and result.","guide":"Clear situation, own actions and a measurable result."}],"facilitator":{"preparation":["Publish the pitch order; check slides or visuals work.","Invite guests (local entrepreneurs, partners) and brief them on respectful questions and safeguarding expectations.","Confirm the room plan: count the learners in the track group, book the rooms and co-facilitators, brief the peer panels, and publish each room's presenter order and the list of recorded presenters by the end of session 7."],"delivery":["Keep strict time; protect nervous presenters from harsh questioning.","Celebrate learners who changed or stopped ideas based on evidence.","Session 8 room plan - Room plan (set in session 7 from the track group size; the session is never extended): up to 12 learners - one room; 13 to 24 - two rooms in parallel; 25 to 36 - three rooms, or two live rooms plus one recorded-questions room. Each live room takes at most 12 presenters: 6 in each 30-minute block, 3 minutes to present and 2 minutes of questions each.","Every room has a facilitator or briefed co-facilitator (another trainer, a mentor or DIRA staff member who knows the rubric and the safeguarding expectations) and a peer panel of two learners who present in the other block. All rooms use the same rubric.","Recorded option: learners in a recorded-questions room, and anyone who cannot present live (absence, connection problems, anxiety, or the privacy option), submit a 3-minute recording - video, or a voice note with slides for low data - by the day before session 8. The panel scores it in advance with the same rubric; in session 8 each recorded presenter answers 2 minutes of live questions (12 per block). If more learners need the recorded option than one room can take, the panel sends written questions and the learner answers them in DIRA LEARN.","Timekeeping: a visible timer with a 30-second warning; stop at 3 minutes. If a block runs late, cut questions to 1 minute - do not extend the session."],"inclusion_and_safeguarding":["Learners who cannot present live can submit a recorded pitch according to the programme's rules.","Guests must not make funding or business offers directly to learners outside programme channels; route any offers through DIRA staff."],"after_session":["Enter rubric scores and feedback in DIRA LEARN grading.","Record attendance."]}}$cv13$::jsonb)
on conflict (track_code, session_no) do nothing;

-- Existing databases (local or, after approval, live): apply now. A fresh `supabase db reset` applies it again at the end of seed.sql.
select item, outcome from apply_curriculum_v13_1();
